export const body = "<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>INTRODUCTION</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>LEVEL ART</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dfc8f9db9f32cc030473.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dfc8f9db9f32cc030473.webp\" width=\"1088\" height=\"972\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Warm-up! A few words about Props Artist.</strong></p>\n<p>As you probably know, there are two totally different jobs referred as “<strong>Environment Artist</strong>”.</p>\n<p>The most common one is the <strong>Props Artist</strong>. Because it’s easy to start with, and because we need <strong>tons of them to create a great game</strong>!</p>\n<p>Props Artists master one or many <strong>3D software</strong> like <strong>Maya</strong> or <strong>3DSmax</strong>, along with <strong>Photoshop</strong>, to create <strong>high quality static meshes</strong> and <strong>objects</strong>.</p>\n<p>They’re aware of the <strong>constraints </strong>of a video game and focus on <strong>quality</strong> and <strong>budget</strong> of <strong>individual objects</strong>.</p>\n<p>Once their objects are done and validated, they quickly start some other and <strong>often don’t bother about integration in the final game</strong>.</p>\n<p>They already know <strong>everything about 3D art</strong> in video games, and that’s why most of Level Artists were once Props Artist that wished to go <strong>beyond the limits of their own software</strong> and <strong>imply more into the game</strong> itself…</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Level Artist</strong></p>\n<p>Basically, your job as a Level Artist consists of <strong>whole level creations</strong>.</p>\n<p>You will be responsible for it during all the process, which can take up to a few months depending of the platform and the type of game.</p>\n<p>Instead of using classic 3D software, you <strong>work directly in the game editor</strong>, allowing you to live in the world you’re creating <strong>with the very eyes of the player</strong>.</p>\n<p>You consider the <strong>big picture </strong>and the <strong>general feeling </strong>of the level instead of small details. Everything in the level is under your control, and you know that the final game will look like exactly the way you’ve made it, because <strong>you are the end of the line.</strong></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/161e6948bc876dc57f2c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/161e6948bc876dc57f2c.webp\" width=\"1051\" height=\"620\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bb2296baef661212fbc4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bb2296baef661212fbc4.webp\" width=\"1001\" height=\"562\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>What you make is what you see</em></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>However, a Level Artist is <strong>never alone</strong>, and has a lot of people to work with.</p>\n<p>You’ll work with your <strong>level designer partner,</strong> refer to the <strong>technical team for level optimization</strong>, <strong>command</strong> to the <strong>Concept Artists</strong> and <strong>Props Artists </strong>team, and <strong>report</strong> to your <strong>Level Art Lead</strong> and <strong>Art Director</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>teamwork</strong> here is really <strong>more important</strong> compared to Props Artists that often refers only to their Leads for questions and validations.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/86d0af28c5090ae301eb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/86d0af28c5090ae301eb.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"330\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6d2a7c032201ce927c6a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6d2a7c032201ce927c6a.webp\" width=\"914\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Player’s experience</strong></p>\n<p>Since a Level Artist was probably <strong>once a Props Artist</strong>, you already know how to create some good looking <strong>meshes</strong>, <strong>textures</strong> and <strong>shaders</strong>.</p>\n<p>But this is only the easy part, and you will be asked to use all your artistic knowledge to make much <strong>more than a visually stunning level</strong>.</p>\n<p>A well done Level Art can <strong>manipulate the player’s feelings</strong>. By working on <strong>level composition</strong>, <strong>theme</strong>, <strong>lights</strong>, you can <strong>induce joy or fear</strong>. You can <strong>stress</strong> the player, or make him <strong>relax</strong>.</p>\n<p>It’s because of Level Art that <strong>the player’s experience</strong> in your environment will be <strong>unique</strong> and <strong>memorable</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Environments can also <strong>tell much more about the story</strong> and <strong>the game background </strong>than characters would do!</p>\n<p>By understanding the ways of <strong>Visual Storytelling</strong>, you can turn any kind of environment into a <strong>credible universe </strong>where <strong>everything means something</strong>, instead of simply putting bricks together to form a path. That makes <strong>navigation more interesting</strong> and <strong>exploration more rewarding</strong>.</p>\n<p>In certain kinds of games, like <strong>RPG</strong> or <strong>investigation</strong> games, it’s <strong>a must have</strong>!</p>\n<p>Level Art is also used to <strong>guide the player all along his navigation</strong> through the level.  If it’s <strong>not done correctly</strong>, players would <strong>get lost</strong>, leading to frustration.</p>\n<p>There are many artistic tips to help the player finding his way at first sight, enhancing the <strong>game experience</strong> and the <strong>feeling of freedom</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d5039d30cacac15a3583.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d5039d30cacac15a3583.webp\" width=\"888\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>In addition of <strong>usual artistic skills</strong> for video game creation, making a level <strong>require a few more</strong>.</p>\n<p>Although quite different from meshes or texture creation, these skills are <strong>closer to traditional art</strong>, like <strong>painting</strong> or <strong>illustration</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/75d6121e85c4ec172182.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/75d6121e85c4ec172182.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"855\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0d3e76df283fb538a86c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0d3e76df283fb538a86c.webp\" width=\"816\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Crucial artistic skill: Observation</strong></p>\n<p>The first of all is a great sense of <strong>observation</strong>.</p>\n<p>It’s obviously the base of anything: observe <strong>reality</strong> or <strong>references</strong>, analyze what makes a world <strong>believable or not</strong>, why objects are usually <strong>placed this way</strong>, what kind of <strong>patterns is frequent </strong>in natural environments, etc.</p>\n<p>It will also be required to <strong>analyze a concept art</strong> and understand <strong>the core of an unusual world</strong> and what makes it so <strong>specific</strong>, <strong>without skipping any little detail</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>There are plenty of useful information you can gather from these two concept arts:</p>\n<ul><li>We can tell that this man is into boxing with all the boxing images on the wall and the sandbag.</li><li>He also has a lot porno stuff: a picture on the wall, a handful of magazines, and his TV is even showing it.</li></ul>\n<p>Therefore, you can tell this man is really into violence and sex. It is not a surprise that he committed some kind of crime.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a6f88a71e26402df9bdb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a6f88a71e26402df9bdb.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"517\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0b72020aa5ca2e58a300.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0b72020aa5ca2e58a300.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"500\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/53612defdadfb0776a42.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/53612defdadfb0776a42.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"637\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fb5e751643701c05f76f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fb5e751643701c05f76f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"641\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Crucial artistic skill: Imagination</strong></p>\n<p>Then comes <strong>imagination</strong>.</p>\n<p>Like every storyteller, you will often have to imagine a whole world from a single concept art picture or photo reference. <strong>Level Art is all about imagination</strong>.</p>\n<p>How can you create an <strong>interesting environment </strong>with only these <strong>props at your disposal</strong>?</p>\n<p>How will you make this second street different from the first one to keep the experience fresh? You have a concept art of a strange alien dining room, but how the corridor nearby should looks like?</p>\n<p>Your <strong>creativity will be solicited</strong> every time and you’ll <strong>need so much imagination</strong> to feed it, literally <strong>live in your own world </strong>in your mind even <strong>before creating it</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Crucial artistic skill: Composition</strong></p>\n<p>The third special Level Artist skill is <strong>the power of composition</strong>.</p>\n<p>It requires <strong>taking a step back</strong> to look at your level and <strong>compose your picture </strong>like a painter would compose his painting. Once again, you <strong>can’t create a level </strong>just by <strong>placing some props randomly </strong>and hoping something great would suddenly appear. You have to think your level as <strong>a succession of painting</strong>. Each time you enter a new corridor, watch over a window, pass by a door, you’ve got <strong>a new picture frame to compose</strong>.</p>\n<p>It’s your job to make <strong>a wise use of any visual tool</strong> to create some <strong>interesting</strong>, <strong>well balanced pictures </strong>anytime.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c7dfa912fab1a2c594cc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c7dfa912fab1a2c594cc.webp\" width=\"1015\" height=\"976\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ea694e576c7466453ebb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ea694e576c7466453ebb.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"724\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Red letters </strong></em><em>represent the </em><em><strong>gameplay beats </strong></em><em>defined by the </em><em><strong>Level Designer </strong></em><em>while </em><em><strong>aesthetics of everything grey</strong></em><em> are defined by the </em><em><strong>Level Artist</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Teamwork skills</strong></p>\n<p>As a Level Artist, you’ll <strong>never be alone</strong>. All along the process of level creation, you will work with a <strong>Level Designer partner</strong>.</p>\n<p>Even if your <strong>jobs</strong> and <strong>responsibilities</strong> will be <strong>extremely different</strong>, <strong>most</strong> <strong>decisions</strong> needs to be <strong>taken together</strong>; you’re two sides of the same coin.</p>\n<p>It’s important for each partner to fully understand the role of the other one. While the <strong>Level Artist</strong> will focus on <strong>the graphic </strong>part, the <strong>Level Designer </strong>will take care of the <strong>gaming experience</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>He will handle all <strong>combat</strong> and <strong>navigation gameplay</strong>, <strong>duration of each sequence</strong> and <strong>use NPC </strong>and <strong>scripts</strong> to keep the player <strong>under pressure</strong>.</p>\n<p>Once you have <strong>understood</strong> your <strong>partner’s job </strong>and <strong>requirements</strong>, you will be able to build a story together.</p>\n<p>Your role doesn’t stop where his start, and it’s always more constructive to <strong>discuss</strong> every detail of your level <strong>together</strong>. <strong>Most great ideas </strong>come from a <strong>good communication </strong>between the two partners.</p>\n<p>In this example, the Level Designer used <strong>basic shapes </strong>to layout the <strong>navigation</strong> and <strong>enemy placements</strong>. Then all these <strong>shapes</strong> will be <strong>replaced with assets </strong>by the Level Artist.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8b5ed49621b8f2e78ab4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8b5ed49621b8f2e78ab4.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/78594ceb9eac63e03dd0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/78594ceb9eac63e03dd0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"396\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fbde428fdc02d6e76176.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fbde428fdc02d6e76176.webp\" width=\"600\" height=\"225\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 17\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bdd17cc7e487193b684f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bdd17cc7e487193b684f.webp\" width=\"600\" height=\"338\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 18\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Technical knowledge</strong></p>\n<p>Since a Level Artist can only do his job on the <strong>Game Editor</strong>, you will probably need to learn and master a whole <strong>new software on each new project</strong>, along with the <strong>specifications of each targeted platform</strong>.</p>\n<p>If you <strong>like to implicate </strong>yourself into <strong>new technologies</strong>, it won’t be a problem since <strong>most of your skills</strong> will still be <strong>usable </strong>from a project to another.</p>\n<p>However, the whole <strong>level optimization </strong>will be <strong>your responsibility</strong>.</p>\n<p>As I mentioned: <strong>You’re the end of the line</strong>, and nobody will come to save you if you didn’t think your level to <strong>run smoothly from the beginning</strong>.</p>\n<p>Expect to spend <strong>a lot of time</strong> on <strong>tweaks</strong> and <strong>optimization</strong>, along with finding some new tricks and ideas to always push back <strong>the limitation of consoles</strong>!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Conclusion</strong></p>\n<p>Like Game and Level Designers, the role of Level Artist implies to be fully involved into video games. Because it’s a great source of inspirations and this is the one and only way to test your own work!</p>\n<p>A Level Artist who doesn’t like playing video games will lack reference, self-criticism, and just won’t be able to test his work the proper way. You want to get better? Play, play, play… and observe of course!</p>\n<p>Then, here’s a little warning. Since you’re at the end of the production line, you must have the shoulders to handle the lateness of any other parts of the project.</p>\n<p>The more the project progresses, the more pressure you‘ll need to handle. Be prepared to deal with a lot of work, and a lot of responsibilities.</p>\n<p>If you aren’t afraid by anything mentioned and still wish to go forward, congratulation: you’ve got what it takes to become a Level Artist!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0fdf2862985a3256f22d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0fdf2862985a3256f22d.webp\" width=\"958\" height=\"698\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 19\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<h2>Topic 01: Level Artists &amp; their relationship with Designers</h2>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>TOPIC INTRODUCTION</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>LEVEL ARTISTS &amp; THEIR RELATIONSHIP WITH DESIGNER</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>A Level Artist is never alone, and has a lot of people to work with.</p>\n<p>You’ll work with your level designer partner, refer to the technical team for level optimization, command to the Concept Artists and Props Artists team, and report to your Level Art Lead and Art Director.</p>\n<p>The teamwork here is really more important compared to Props Artists that often refers only to their Leads for questions and validations.</p>\n<p>This topic will introduce the relationship between level artists and designers.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2f199a336ed658dc8267.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2f199a336ed658dc8267.webp\" width=\"934\" height=\"526\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 20\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/03ebacf118c349a01dd6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/03ebacf118c349a01dd6.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"588\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 21\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Level Workflow – The MCF</strong></p>\n<p>The MCF (Mission Construction Framework) is a common workflow designed to allow Level Designer and Level Artist to build a level together without time loss. It consists of different stages of variable duration, depending of the complexity of the project and the needs of the game (a Nextgen AAA game will allow more time than a short handheld game).</p>\n<p>The content of the MCF differs depending of the type of game (FPS, racing game, etc) however, the basics stay the same.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Hints to work with Level Designers</strong></p>\n<p>The Level Artist can provide a main idea and/or reference for the visual of each area.</p>\n<p>The Level Designer can provide a visual of key actions: if the idea comes from a movie or a comic, get the exact reference to help the Artist impregnate with ambiance and understand exactly where he’s aiming at.</p>\n<p>Always make sure that your partner understands your intentions. If needed, provide sketches and pictures, take some time to explain. Avoid “you will see later when it will be done”.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6e9f6dbea08540426358.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6e9f6dbea08540426358.webp\" width=\"659\" height=\"440\" style=\"--source-width:269px\" alt=\"Illustration 22\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2411f9b5f282adb55399.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2411f9b5f282adb55399.webp\" width=\"1041\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:276px\" alt=\"Illustration 23\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>The Mock-up from GreyBoxes to Awesomeness</strong></p>\n<p>The Mock-Up is a visual layout of the whole level made by the Level Artist from the Level Designer’s Template. It is also known as “speed-model”, “proxy”, “Artist’s Grey Boxes” and many other names.</p>\n<p>It includes every intention of scale, visual composition, lighting mood, expected emotions, etc. When anyone plays your Mock-Up level, he should know exactly how the level will look/feel like when it will be done.</p>\n<p>The Mock-Up is created by the Level Artist from the “3D Template/Grey Boxes” stage to the “Alpha Gameplay”.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h3>Chapter 01: Level Workflow - The MCF</h3>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>TABLE OF CONTENT</strong></p><p><strong>Chapter 01: Level Workflow - The MCF </strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>This chapter contains:</p>\n<ul><li>Mission Construction Framework (MCF)</li><li>Level brief</li><li>Pre-production / Beat doc</li><li>Beat doc: pacing</li><li>3D template / Greybox Level</li><li>First playable</li><li>Alpha gameplay</li><li>Visuals &amp; polish</li></ul></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>CONTENT</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Mission Construction Framework (MCF)</strong></p>\n<p>The<strong> MCF </strong>(Mission Construction Framework) is a common workflow designed to allow <strong>Level Designer </strong>and <strong>Level Artist </strong>to <strong>build a level together without time loss</strong>. It consists of different stages of variable duration, depending of the <strong>complexity</strong> of the project and the <strong>needs</strong> of the game (a Nextgen AAA game will allow more time than a short handheld game).</p>\n<p>The content of the MCF <strong>differs depending of the type of game</strong> (FPS, racing game, etc) however, the <strong>basics </strong>stay the <strong>same</strong>.</p>\n<p>It’s precisely these basics we’ll see in this document, what are the <strong>main roles </strong>of <strong>LA</strong> and <strong>LD</strong> in <strong>each stage</strong>, when you will need to <strong>decide something together</strong>, and what are <strong>your responsibilities</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a608d76f446b0f2739cc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a608d76f446b0f2739cc.webp\" width=\"311\" height=\"406\" style=\"--source-width:260px\" alt=\"Illustration 24\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cc3d76dcef2cf61d6b50.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cc3d76dcef2cf61d6b50.webp\" width=\"810\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 25\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Level brief</strong></p>\n<p><strong>The Level Brief </strong>is a document written by your Leads. It covers <strong>all you need to know </strong>before starting your level, for both <strong>Level Designers </strong>and <strong>Level Artists</strong>.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>When</strong> the level takes place in the game? What the player has encounter before, and will discover after.</li><li>The <strong>storyline </strong>of the level.</li><li>The <strong>settings</strong> of the level (Environment, time of the day, weather, etc).</li><li>The <strong>challenges</strong> for the player (type of gameplay, range of difficulty, stress level, etc)</li></ul>\n<p>I recommend you to <strong>read everything</strong>, even if it does <strong>not concern precisely your job</strong>. It will help you to get a <strong>better idea</strong> of what we expect for this level and a <strong>better understanding</strong> of your LD’s needs.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9cf59b5e8f07ef67b8fc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9cf59b5e8f07ef67b8fc.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"996\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 26\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Pre-production / Beat doc</strong></p>\n<p>This is the first step of the level creation. The Level Designer and Level Artist will <strong>work together to decide </strong>what the level will <strong>look like</strong>, what the player should <strong>experience</strong>, what <strong>important memories</strong> he will <strong>keep</strong>, etc.</p>\n<p>At this stage, almost <strong>nothing is written in stone</strong>, and it’s important for the two partners to <strong>build every aspect of their story together</strong></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1ba9e185ea0e50bb1f7c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1ba9e185ea0e50bb1f7c.webp\" width=\"1006\" height=\"838\" style=\"--source-width:260px\" alt=\"Illustration 27\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/facd55a9d706e7279330.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/facd55a9d706e7279330.webp\" width=\"802\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 28\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>After delivering the full Beat Document, everyone should be able to fully understand what your level will look like, and validate it.</p>\n<p>If people watch your documents and say “I want to play this level!”, you’re on the good way!</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a07e309db0adbd9402ba.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a07e309db0adbd9402ba.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"734\" style=\"--source-width:260px\" alt=\"Illustration 29\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Beat doc: pacing</strong></p>\n<p>One important element of making a level (or a game as a whole) is pacing. It is the way you set up the events in your level and your goal is to keep the player engaged throughout.</p>\n<p>To control pacing, LDs use movement <strong>incentive</strong> and movement <strong>deterrent.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Movement incentives:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Threat</li><li>Objective</li><li>Movement</li><li>Time limits</li></ul></td><td><p><strong>Movement deterrents:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Tension (perceived threat)</li><li>Obstacles</li><li>Change in gameplay  or movement<br> Ex: Puzzles or dialogue choices</li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>We have two variables for pacing: <strong>Player agency (</strong>gameplay) and <strong>Certainty of Delivery </strong>(storytelling)</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Cutscenes:</strong> low agency, high certainty</li><li><strong>Environmental storytelling: </strong>high agency, low certainty</li><li><strong>Triggered dialogue: </strong>variable for both</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Narrative systems: </strong>high certainty, high agency</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d8334ef23b996e0379bb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d8334ef23b996e0379bb.webp\" width=\"482\" height=\"615\" style=\"--source-width:260px\" alt=\"Illustration 30\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b17449e5e2ccb93a8a6d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b17449e5e2ccb93a8a6d.webp\" width=\"1149\" height=\"694\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 31\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>3D template / Greybox Level</strong></p>\n<p>Now we know exactly what we want to do, it’s time to go in the editor! Like in the first stage, Level Designer and Level Artist will need to work together to build the <strong>foundation of their world</strong>.</p>\n<p>For the moment, the level is <strong>fully gameplay oriented</strong>, so the Level Designer should <strong>keep the control</strong> of the level’s geometry to be able to <strong>instantiate quickly</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>At this time, your level should already give a sense of <strong>scale</strong> and <strong>perspective</strong>.</p>\n<p>A grid texture is used to have <strong>a better perception of scale</strong>.</p>\n<p>The artist can set up a <strong>first light direction </strong>to help the <strong>reading of perspective</strong>.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9c610db01f16e1201191.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9c610db01f16e1201191.webp\" width=\"1130\" height=\"528\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 32\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8eebe5bae0affd00f92a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8eebe5bae0affd00f92a.webp\" width=\"1045\" height=\"899\" style=\"--source-width:260px\" alt=\"Illustration 33\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a9adf18fe5923d204c1d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a9adf18fe5923d204c1d.webp\" width=\"1084\" height=\"660\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 34\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>First playable</strong></p>\n<p>In the First Playable stage, the Level designer still have the <strong>full control </strong>of gameplay area’s geometry.</p>\n<p>It allows him to <strong>freely iterate </strong>to find the <strong>best topology</strong> for this level’s gameplay. The Level Artist mostly works on the <strong>non-gameplay areas</strong> (<strong>backgrounds, for example</strong>) to prevent him to lose some time if the Level Designer wants to iterate the place he’s working on.</p>\n<p>At this point, the Level’s <strong>topology</strong> and <strong>design</strong> is <strong>finalized</strong> and <strong>validated</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>The <strong>landmark</strong> (here, a mountain) is placed; the artist can start replacing <strong>grid</strong> <strong>textures</strong> by <strong>placeholder textures </strong>to give a better feeling of this special environment. He can also start working on<strong> non-playable areas </strong>(<strong>background</strong> and <strong>upper floors</strong>, since that’s <strong>away from the player path</strong>).</p>\n<p>The <strong>first</strong> <strong>enemies</strong> have been placed by the designer along with <strong>a few lights</strong>, since in this case it was required for the gameplay.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/17ce066dd88770149302.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/17ce066dd88770149302.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"631\" style=\"--source-width:260px\" alt=\"Illustration 35\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/34b33afd808a69b85292.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/34b33afd808a69b85292.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"668\" style=\"--source-width:260px\" alt=\"Illustration 36\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d03972392908ae55ae4e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d03972392908ae55ae4e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"800\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 37\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Alpha gameplay</strong></p>\n<p>Now the Level’s topology is <strong>validated</strong>, and we are sure the level Designer <strong>won’t iterate on it anymore</strong>.</p>\n<p>Therefore, the level Artist can start working on both <strong>gameplay</strong> and <strong>non-gameplay areas</strong>, and populate it with props (while still <strong>respecting</strong> the <strong>essential gameplay elements </strong>such as <strong>heights</strong>, <strong>collisions</strong>, <strong>dynamic objects</strong>, etc).</p>\n<p>At the end of Alpha Gameplay stage, the Level Design is <strong>feature complete</strong>. <strong>Gameplay</strong> and <strong>pacing</strong> are <strong>stabilized</strong> so that final assets can be built.  All <strong>creative </strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a5991380e77462885c92.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a5991380e77462885c92.webp\" width=\"1132\" height=\"622\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 38\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Background is done. The Artist can start </em><em><strong>integrating assets</strong></em><em> in gameplay area (without affecting gameplay) and start </em><em><strong>working on the ground </strong></em><em>(here, some Unreal Terrain).</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4004722188beea3e5bba.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4004722188beea3e5bba.webp\" width=\"929\" height=\"621\" style=\"--source-width:260px\" alt=\"Illustration 39\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Another example of level in “Alpha Gameplay” quality. </em><em><strong>Lights</strong></em><em>, </em><em><strong>props</strong></em><em> and </em><em><strong>details</strong></em><em> are </em><em><strong>not placed yet</strong></em><em>, but every </em><em><strong>gameplay element is present and functional</strong></em><em>. </em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3b1d4d5f0a95cfe6c442.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3b1d4d5f0a95cfe6c442.webp\" width=\"925\" height=\"644\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 40\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Visuals &amp; polish</strong></p>\n<p>After Gameplay Alpha, <strong>the Level Designer can hand over the full control of graphic maps to Level Artist. </strong>From now on, the Level Artist will be able to add art to every area (while still respect the essential gameplay elements such as heights, collisions, dynamic objects, etc).</p>\n<p><strong>Don’t misread the following chart: this stage is one of the longest and can take up to a few weeks!</strong></p>\n<p>It include the 1<sup>st</sup> Viewable stage, 2<sup>nd</sup> Viewable stage, Detailed World stage, and Final pass (after QA feedbacks). I’ve summed it up because during this stage, every interaction between Level Designers and Level Artists are quite the same.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1bec6bb5da4e048f9bf8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1bec6bb5da4e048f9bf8.webp\" width=\"1059\" height=\"596\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 41\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Part of a level before polishing</em><br></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/22af1ea0ef2dfdd08384.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/22af1ea0ef2dfdd08384.webp\" width=\"1056\" height=\"523\" style=\"--source-width:285px\" alt=\"Illustration 42\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Overpainting</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6bce9bba08821bc85b17.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6bce9bba08821bc85b17.webp\" width=\"1060\" height=\"596\" style=\"--source-width:260px\" alt=\"Illustration 43\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>After overpainting and polishing</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h3>Chapter 02: Hints to work with Level Designers</h3>\n\n<p><strong>scroll-bookmark-38</strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>TABLE OF CONTENT</strong></p><p><strong>Chapter 02 - Hints to work with Level Designers </strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>This chapter contains:</p>\n<ul><li>Working with the Level Designer</li><li>Questions to ask</li><li>See through the Greybox</li><li>Two types of Level Designer (LD)</li><li>Advice</li><li>Collision</li><li>Camera constraints</li><li>Type of gameplay</li><li>Design intentions of each areas</li></ul></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>CONTENT</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Working with the Level Designer</strong></p>\n<p>The Level Artist can provide a <strong>main idea </strong>and/or <strong>reference</strong> for the visual of each area.</p>\n<p>The Level Designer can provide <strong>a visual of key actions</strong>: if the idea comes from a movie or a comic, get the <strong>exact reference </strong>to help the Artist impregnate with <strong>ambiance</strong> and <strong>understand exactly where he’s aiming at</strong>.</p>\n<p>Always make sure that your partner understands your intentions. If needed, provide <strong>sketches</strong> and <strong>pictures</strong>, take some <strong>time to explain</strong>. <strong>Avoid “you will see later when it will be done”.</strong></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/67efce40fb33991e5a56.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/67efce40fb33991e5a56.webp\" width=\"571\" height=\"286\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 47\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f6eede54694d628b20aa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f6eede54694d628b20aa.webp\" width=\"572\" height=\"300\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 48\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Mood board</strong> is a very useful tool for this step. All you need to is compile a set of inspirations for your project.</p>\n<p>This tool can be your guide toward the vision you have in mind, your motivation to keep working, and it also help the Level Designer understands your direction better.</p>\n<p>And the reason why you need multiple references rather a specific source is originality. You can’t create something new/original with just one source of reference. You need to get the best out of many to shape your own.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/91748c4aeb416e899a71.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/91748c4aeb416e899a71.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"395\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 49\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8c28f3a4d3aab7ac444f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8c28f3a4d3aab7ac444f.webp\" width=\"1071\" height=\"603\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 50\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d9c5736446a7ac773366.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d9c5736446a7ac773366.webp\" width=\"1071\" height=\"603\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 51\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Questions to ask</strong></p>\n<p>What about the <strong>visual setting </strong>(<strong>environment,</strong> <strong>weather, time of the day</strong>).</p>\n<p>Ask for a <strong>full level walkthrough </strong>(even just a story, <strong>in early stage</strong>).</p>\n<ul><li>What kind of <strong>emotions</strong> the player should feel? Which are the <strong>tension area </strong>and where he will <strong>breathe</strong>?</li><li>What are the <strong>Level Designer’s guidelines</strong>?</li><li>What are the most <strong>important requirements from the client?</strong></li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>See through the Greybox</strong></p>\n<p>Communicate to <strong>clearly identify what is everything for</strong>.</p>\n<p>What must be <strong>graphically present</strong>.</p>\n<p>What is just a <strong>navigation blocker</strong>.</p>\n<p>What must become <strong>the focal point </strong>of each scene.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0539cd1327caa0837cb1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0539cd1327caa0837cb1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"678\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 52\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/47e96b4f9f9a8caa2d5c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/47e96b4f9f9a8caa2d5c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:131px\" alt=\"Illustration 53\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6d31f0446026d8d7128a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6d31f0446026d8d7128a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"641\" style=\"--source-width:131px\" alt=\"Illustration 54\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><em>Once you know the exact use of every element of the LD Grey Boxes, you can imagine something </em><em><strong>really different from the original mood</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Two types of Level Designer (LD)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Sensitive Level Designer</strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Action-based Level Designer</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Has <strong>his own idea </strong>of what the level should look like, it’s important for him.</p>\n<p>Allow more of <strong>discussion about environment</strong>, but you will need to confront your ideas.<strong>Easier </strong>to work with in the <strong>beginning</strong>, but <strong>less creative </strong>for the Artist.</p></td><td><p><strong>Only care about gameplay</strong> (challenges, AI, pacing, etc).</p>\n<p>Can have <strong>no idea of what the level should look like</strong>, he doesn’t care.</p>\n<p>Give a full <strong>freedom of creativity </strong>for the Artist.<br>Require to be <strong>more imaginative</strong>.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3aee812155ef81b8ecb1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3aee812155ef81b8ecb1.webp\" width=\"598\" height=\"370\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 55\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Always know “why”, to build a rock-solid structure.</strong></em><em> Everything can be changed by anyone as long as you don’t know </em><em><strong>“why” </strong></em><em>it is like this.</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Advice</strong></p>\n<p>It’s also your level, not only the one of the Level Designer: Don’t try to <strong>avoid confrontation</strong>. Challenge your partner!</p>\n<p>From the <strong>beginning</strong> and almost until the <strong>end</strong>, <strong>nothing is carved in stone</strong>. If something is <strong>bugging</strong> you (a shape feels unrealistic, you think the player won’t understand something), it is YOUR DUTY to <strong>challenge</strong> your partner to <strong>find a solution</strong>!</p>\n<p>Defend your ideas.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Understanding the designer’s guideline<strong> </strong>to prevent problems before it happens.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Metrics</strong>: define every gameplay element: how high your character can jump, how fast he moves, how big is his weapon,… Metrics are the reference for every piece of game design.</li><li><strong>Scale</strong>: refers to the <strong>relative size </strong>of something, whether it be characters, objects or the game world. Scaling should be done according to game type.</li></ul>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1ecbc7524b42c061b091.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1ecbc7524b42c061b091.webp\" width=\"530\" height=\"377\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 56\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e29bb8e7e66bcf11be7d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e29bb8e7e66bcf11be7d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"506\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 57\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f3068b4cddf4c0d6f79c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f3068b4cddf4c0d6f79c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 58\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/44171363c57b35d8fad4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/44171363c57b35d8fad4.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 59\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This is a </em><em><strong>collision mesh</strong></em><em>. It’s what the </em><em><strong>game engine sees </strong></em><em>and essentially what </em><em><strong>defines gameplay mechanics</strong></em><em>. But </em><em><strong>players won’t see</strong></em><em> it.</em></p>\n<p><em>Here we have the </em><em><strong>walking surface in light green</strong></em><em>, </em><em><strong>high covers in pink</strong></em><em>, and </em><em><strong>low covers in teal</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Collision</strong></p>\n<p>While most designers <strong>won't care too much about visuals</strong>, they will <strong>always care about collisions</strong>. Most designers <strong>tools</strong> are based on <strong>collisions</strong> (AI, Navigation, Pathfinding...)</p>\n<p>When LD create the <strong>first level greybox</strong>, they define where the <strong>main collisions </strong>should be (ground shape, level boundaries, important covers, etc)</p>\n<p>When LA update the level to <strong>Mock-up</strong> and then <strong>Final Art</strong>, he should <strong>never modify the collision shapes and location</strong>. LD still need them!</p>\n<p>If for any reason, LA <strong>need to modify the shape or location of a collision</strong>, he should <strong>always get the approval of his LD first!</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Camera constraints</strong></p>\n<p>There are two prominent types of camera angle: First Person Perspective (FPP) and Third Person Perspective (TPP).</p>\n<p>While FPP lets the player experience the game through the eye of his character, TPP offers a wider range of visual presentation. In FPP you can only see what in front of your character, but in TPP you see your character and his surroundings at the same time. However, as the industry moves forward, there are many variations of TPP: over the shoulder like God of War (2018), top-down (isometric) like Diablo, or a standard one like in Assassin’s Creed and many other hack ‘n slash.</p>\n<p>It’s all down to deliver a level that’s nice to look at and fun to play based on the point of view of the game. Just remember to play test and use the player camera in your game editor.</p>\n<p>In addition, some games like Metal Gear Solid use both FPP and TPP, so you should be more deliberate when designing levels for these games.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ff69a62f4f5ebbdf7ee1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ff69a62f4f5ebbdf7ee1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 60\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c65f5de397433d7b50d1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c65f5de397433d7b50d1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 61\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c1cb807c4dc5c919b60f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c1cb807c4dc5c919b60f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"609\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 62\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/40d55d0dc6a27599404e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/40d55d0dc6a27599404e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 63\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Type of gameplay</strong></p>\n<p>Different games have different needs for gameplay.</p>\n<p>Shooting game needs relatively large environment to provide range and covers for the player. Your player should be able to move around quickly and smoothly while shooting his enemies.</p>\n<p>Tight spaces work better for games built upon close melee combat. Sometimes an empty, circular white ground is enough because the player’s attention will most be on his enemies, not the details in the environment.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Design intentions of each areas</strong></p>\n<p>Every area has its own story to tell and challenges to be overcome. The level art should be done to thematically tie in with them.</p>\n<p>This is a Divine Beast from Breath of the Wild called Van Medoh; it is basically a mechanical construct that functions like a giant eagle. At its core, Van Medoh is a game level with puzzles and combat encounters, but it stands out by presenting the challenges in a creative form. There are multiple ways to traverse and unlock different parts of this giant robot. The main theme of this “level” is wind, so you will find yourself manipulating air current and gliding both in and outside a lot.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/99822bba9ae5fdf6750e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/99822bba9ae5fdf6750e.webp\" width=\"570\" height=\"479\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 64\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h3>Chapter 03: The Mock-up from GreyBoxes to Awesomeness</h3>\n\n<p>scroll-bookmark-52  </p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>TABLE OF CONTENT</strong></p><p><strong>Chapter 03: The Mock-up from GreyBoxes to Awesomeness</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>This chapter contains:</p>\n<ul><li>Mock-Up</li><li>Important criteria</li><li>Pre-production</li><li>Pre-production</li><li>Level design requirements</li><li>Everything needs a purpose</li><li>Comparing with reality</li><li>PRO-TIP</li><li>Emotions</li><li>Landmark</li><li>Use your imagination</li><li>PRO-TIP</li><li>Define different zones</li><li>Justify your blockers</li><li>Compose pictures &amp; scenes</li><li>PRO-TIP</li><li>Define different zones</li><li>Camera &amp; Scale</li></ul></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>CONTENT</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Mock-Up</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>Mock-Up</strong> is a <strong>visual layout </strong>of the whole level made by the <strong>Level Artist </strong>from the <strong>Level Designer</strong>’s Template. It is also known as “<strong>speed-model</strong>”, “<strong>proxy</strong>”, “<strong>Artist’s Grey Boxes</strong>” and many other names.</p>\n<p>It includes every intention of <strong>scale</strong>, <strong>visual composition</strong>, <strong>lighting mood</strong>, <strong>expected emotions</strong>, etc. When anyone plays your Mock-Up level, he should <strong>know exactly how the level will look/feel like</strong> when it will be done.</p>\n<p>The Mock-Up is created by the Level Artist from the “<strong>3D Template/Grey Boxes</strong>” stage to the “<strong>Alpha Gameplay</strong>”.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3a2768ca28cdc94caeb3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3a2768ca28cdc94caeb3.webp\" width=\"818\" height=\"612\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 68\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cc25f0b7b68efda39c97.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cc25f0b7b68efda39c97.webp\" width=\"928\" height=\"611\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 69\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Important criteria</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Art mock-up needs to be <strong>fast to build </strong>and <strong>iterate</strong>.</li><li><strong>Every object </strong>(props, buildings...) need to be <strong>recognizable</strong>.</li><li>The <strong>location</strong>, <strong>ambiance</strong> and <strong>mood</strong> should be <strong>identifiable</strong>.</li></ol>\n<p>The level need to be <strong>broken down into speed-models</strong>.<a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/886c88de4762e39c26eb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/886c88de4762e39c26eb.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"491\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 70\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Everything can be <strong>decided</strong> (and more important: <strong>validated</strong>!) during the Mock-Up. It’s the best moment to try some <strong>good views</strong>, to test a <strong>composition</strong>, to propose a <strong>scene</strong>, because there’s almost <strong>no consequence if you fail</strong>. Since everything can be made and tested quickly, we can quickly iterate to find the best result.</p>\n<p>Basically it’s the <strong>perfect moment </strong>to show all <strong>your ideas</strong>, what YOU would like to make in YOUR level, and get it validated.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6dbd5aa102f02fd864d7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6dbd5aa102f02fd864d7.webp\" width=\"1028\" height=\"720\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 71\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4d06ca56f12498f595c4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4d06ca56f12498f595c4.webp\" width=\"566\" height=\"633\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 72\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Pre-production</strong></p>\n<p>This is the best moment to gather <strong>knowledge</strong>.</p>\n<p>Before starting a level, you should gather <strong>a</strong> <strong>maximum amount </strong>of information about the game you’re about to work on. Your Level Designer can be a good <strong>source of information</strong>, but you can also ask for the official documentation of the game (<strong>Graphic Bible</strong>, <strong>Game Design Document </strong>(GDD), <strong>Kick Off sliders</strong>, etc.)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Pre-production</strong></p>\n<p>What <strong>kind of game </strong>are we doing? (Shooter, racing, simulation,…)</p>\n<ul><li>What is <strong>specific</strong> to this kind of game?</li><li>What are our <strong>reference games</strong>?</li><li>Can I play them to get a better idea of what we expect from me?</li><li>What is our <strong>targeted audience</strong>?</li></ul>\n<p>What is the <strong>expected Rating</strong>? (Avoid using some gore details for a game rated “E for Everyone”)</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/de7821bc9755fbab26b5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/de7821bc9755fbab26b5.webp\" width=\"615\" height=\"448\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 73\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a812268e55b82d18ec22.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a812268e55b82d18ec22.webp\" width=\"879\" height=\"985\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 74\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>This level takes place in a village where people are being corrupted and poisoned to become zombies. People are frightened so they will hide inside and the street will be empty.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/35dfa5ed80fbfa0a6057.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/35dfa5ed80fbfa0a6057.webp\" width=\"1081\" height=\"608\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 75\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Since Warcraft III is a real-time strategy game, it is very important for the environments to be clean and readable apart from the units. The mood of this mission is set by the dark color scheme and night atmosphere; the lack of human presence also helps. All the building have very few colors and use rectangular shapes while the units have more rounded shapes to create contrast. In this particular instance, gameplay is king.</p></td></tr><tr><td><ul><li>What about the <strong>universe</strong>?</li><li>Is it a <strong>realistic</strong> or <strong>cartoon</strong> setting?</li><li>What is the <strong>storyline</strong> of the game?</li><li>Is there a <strong>story</strong> around my level?</li></ul>\n<p>Each client is <strong>different</strong> and will <strong>expect different things</strong>. Try to know what and you’ll be able to focus on it.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3643662a036a7a282bd2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3643662a036a7a282bd2.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 76\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Sci-fi vs Fantasy</em></p>\n<p><em><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0b08e6b4e0b8114e79c0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0b08e6b4e0b8114e79c0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"293\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 77\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></em><em>Cartoony vs Realistic</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Fantasy or sci-fi, the environment still has to follow some specific rule and logic to be believable.</p>\n<p>And if the game has a realistic setting, it won’t necessarily be easy because people will pay closer attention to what they are familiar with.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5204c8ed90aab94aa09a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5204c8ed90aab94aa09a.webp\" width=\"1109\" height=\"937\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 78\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3a2f750a0f86eceb78c8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3a2f750a0f86eceb78c8.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"322\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 79\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Fixed camera angle (Resident Evil 1) vs behind-the-shoulder camera angle (Resident Evil 4)</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/67fba87eccd7f2bd2443.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/67fba87eccd7f2bd2443.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 80\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>First-person perspective in Resident Evil 7</em></p></td><td><ul><li>What about the <strong>game</strong><strong>camera</strong>?</li><li>Is it <strong>attached to the character </strong>or <strong>the environment</strong>?</li><li>Is it <strong>controlled by the player </strong>or by <strong>the level design</strong>?</li><li>Is it <strong>possible</strong> for the camera to <strong>get close to objects</strong>? (i.e. in cutscenes)</li><li>Last but not least, what is <strong>the most important thing</strong> for the client?</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Level design requirements</strong></p>\n<p>Once you know more about the <strong>gameplay</strong> and <strong>level design requirements</strong>, you should know exactly what you <strong>can do </strong>and what you <strong>should avoid</strong>. Now you have <strong>less chance </strong>to do <strong>critical mistakes</strong>, or need to <strong>redo some work</strong>!</p>\n<p>Depending of the game, LD’s requirements can be <strong>specific metrics</strong>, <strong>special camera</strong>, <strong>interactive object</strong>, and much more. Ask your Level Designer to know exactly <strong>which rules </strong>he must <strong>follow</strong>, and it will help both of you.</p></td><td><p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b576b5e67eb5b8dbf20e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b576b5e67eb5b8dbf20e.webp\" width=\"975\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 81\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5c13e2c6d983e98379cc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5c13e2c6d983e98379cc.webp\" width=\"1053\" height=\"655\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 82\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>In Overlord (2007), the player </em><em><strong>can’t jump nor fall from cliffs</strong></em><em>. Knowing this, it becomes a lot </em><em><strong>easier</strong></em><em> to create some nice </em><em><strong>open spaces </strong></em><em>from a directive Level design, even </em><em><strong>without using big blockers</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/78ea3a75876908520ca0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/78ea3a75876908520ca0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 83\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Climbing is one of the core mechanics</strong></em><em> in Assassin’s Creed, and the game uses </em><em><strong>different primitive shapes</strong></em><em> for </em><em><strong>climbable spots</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>The two pictures below are a perfect case of Design requirement. Both games are <strong>shooters</strong>, but <strong>covers</strong> must be created in a <strong>totally different </strong>way because of their <strong>specific game design</strong>!</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><em><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5f764464d5355749a922.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5f764464d5355749a922.webp\" width=\"928\" height=\"692\" style=\"--source-width:135px\" alt=\"Illustration 84\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></em></p>\n<p><em>Unreal Tournament III (2007) is a </em><em><strong>fast shooting game </strong></em><em>where most weapons have big physical explosive ammo, so they can afford this kind of </em><em><strong>semi-open covers</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td><td><p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a788fbfd2c640ca2fff7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a788fbfd2c640ca2fff7.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"615\" style=\"--source-width:135px\" alt=\"Illustration 85\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong></p>\n<p><em>In a </em><em><strong>realistic shooting game</strong></em><em> (here COD6:MW2, 2009), covers must be </em><em><strong>plain</strong></em><em> and </em><em><strong>precise</strong></em><em>. Players must hide behind and feel </em><em><strong>safe</strong></em><em>. They should be able to </em><em><strong>pick beyond borders </strong></em><em>so you can’t afford using </em><em><strong>some strange shapes</strong></em><em>, </em><em><strong>place holes </strong></em><em>and </em><em><strong>openings</strong></em><em>, etc.</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/88fd3d6c8725d8232c54.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/88fd3d6c8725d8232c54.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"599\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 86\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This object can block the player but not his line of sight.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7fb6df44cee47b558759.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7fb6df44cee47b558759.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"609\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 87\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This path is usable for NPC only, so we don’t need to put a lot of detail</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Everything needs a purpose</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Each element </strong>required by your Level Designer <strong>doesn’t have the same purpose</strong>. Try to identify first <strong>what</strong> is each element and <strong>why</strong> is it here.</p>\n<p>Do we need to:</p>\n<ul><li>Block player’s <strong>navigation</strong>?</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Block player’s <strong>line of sight</strong>?</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Block camera’s <strong>line of sight</strong>?</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Path usable by <strong>NPC</strong> <strong>only</strong> or player only?</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Comparing with reality</strong></p>\n<p>Once we know the <strong>purpose</strong> of each object, we can start looking for a <strong>meaning </strong>to everything.</p>\n<p>Pay a close attention to the <strong>SIZE</strong> and the <strong>SHAPE</strong> of each element, then go through your references to find something that can match.</p>\n<p>Try to seek first if you can achieve these sizes and shapes with props you already have.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c6d7ee164d9cca9395dd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c6d7ee164d9cca9395dd.webp\" width=\"1078\" height=\"608\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 88\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Broken walls as covers</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/13b8bb87b2bfe2fb6193.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/13b8bb87b2bfe2fb6193.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"550\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 89\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8734a60aed328e3b1f60.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8734a60aed328e3b1f60.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 90\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>A similar scene in Uncharted 4: A Thief’s End</em></p></td><td><p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong></p>\n<p>For example, when you’re assigned to build an urban level for a shooter, you can find some reference picture like this and use these props for different gameplay purposes:</p>\n<ul><li>Those bikes can be used to block navigation so the player won’t be able to enter the local houses.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>If your game is a shooter, this car can be a semi-open cover. You can also add some broken crates as covers on the ground for a chaotic shoot-out scene.</li><li>The hanging clothes will block the player’s line of sight, but they shouldn’t block his navigation, meaning that his character should be able to jump through them.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Emotions</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>expected Emotions </strong>are usually defined in your <strong>Level Brief</strong>.</p>\n<p>It concerns the <strong>general mood </strong>of your level. What kind of emotions do we expect players to feel when they will play our level?</p>\n<p>You can enhance these emotions when composing.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8ab7aa4f9968a0c9e1dc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8ab7aa4f9968a0c9e1dc.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"428\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 91\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>COD7: Black Ops (2010): While the town is suffocating because of a chemical weapon bombing, the player discovers some children playgrounds. These elements are highly emotional, because even if the player doesn't se dying children, he knows there were some children here before.</p></td></tr><tr><td></td><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/baa4614dda9a3dea580a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/baa4614dda9a3dea580a.webp\" width=\"810\" height=\"608\" style=\"--source-width:122px\" alt=\"Illustration 92\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The burnt body tells the player that the fence is electric.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/466eb432ade99402c6c3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/466eb432ade99402c6c3.webp\" width=\"811\" height=\"608\" style=\"--source-width:122px\" alt=\"Illustration 93\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The morbid conclusion of the people trying to protect the children in the sewers in ‘The Last of Us’.</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Landmark</strong></p>\n<p>Landmarks are <strong>important</strong> for the player <strong>to find his way through your level</strong>, to <strong>know</strong> where he <strong>should be heading</strong>.</p>\n<ol><li>Define your landmarks: what <strong>must be seen </strong>almost all the time?</li><li>How can we <strong>arrange those blocks </strong>to allow a <strong>clear view</strong> of your landmarks?</li></ol>\n<p>Tip: if your level has a landmark, try to develop it first then design everything else around it.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b80a13db6d3b0707973f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b80a13db6d3b0707973f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"834\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 94\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>In Half Life 2(2005), the mysterious Combine Tower is an important landmark. The player can see it almost every time, because the level was designed to be able to do so.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9689efb3ef68709a9c84.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9689efb3ef68709a9c84.webp\" width=\"889\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 95\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>This jagged mountain in “Uncharted 4” appears throughout various stages of the level. It helps remind the player where he needs to go after exploring other areas and killing the enemies.<a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e35d37b650894a6780ad.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e35d37b650894a6780ad.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 96\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Use your imagination</strong></p>\n<p>Try to <strong>go beyond template </strong>and <strong>open the scope</strong>. Find a <strong>meaning</strong> for every element of the level design template.</p>\n<p>Once you knew the <strong>exact purpose </strong>of each element in the LD template, you can start to find a <strong>meaning</strong> to it. <strong>Find opportunities for memorable views.</strong></p>\n<p>Keep in mind that your Level Designer only gives the limit of the playable area, <strong>not the limits of the level</strong>! You can find a lot of good opportunities <strong>to open the view</strong> and <strong>offer</strong> players some <strong>interesting visuals</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ec26dbadb453cd66905a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ec26dbadb453cd66905a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"682\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 97\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b3ea4943dd96dcd4e945.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b3ea4943dd96dcd4e945.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"967\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 98\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong></p>\n<ol><li>The Level Design Template is pretty simple here: a corridor with a door at the end. However, this shape is not very realistic and can be hard to change into something logical.</li><li>The first step is to open the walls, to change the room into a more logical shape (a square) then add some props to close back the navigation path to the original design. To add some variations, the Level Artists opened the ceiling to create a backyard. We can also notice two more doors (blocked), one behind those stairs on the left, leading to the second floor.</li><li>Creating paths like this enhance the realism of the scene with Visual Logic: “If this is a building, then people live here. If people live here, then they must have a way to enter their houses.” Notice how the path is definitely close to avoid giving the player a wrong idea of the direction he needs to take.</li></ol></td></tr><tr><td><p>As the Level Designer only care about gameplay, he won’t care often how the level will look.</p>\n<p>This big “wall” on the left doesn’t need to stay this way!</p>\n<p>Once we know its real purpose (closing the level, it’s one of the level’s border) we can open the view and offer the player a nice looking panorama.</p>\n<p>There’s no ambiguity about the direction, since the roasting is well closed.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a15102b7d75320d98019.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a15102b7d75320d98019.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 99\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9a3e9ab0c27787fb9baf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9a3e9ab0c27787fb9baf.webp\" width=\"1014\" height=\"627\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 100\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>You can see five different areas in this Intention Map for Ghost Recon: Future Soldier.</em><em><br></em><em> From right to left: an opium field, a safe village, a ruins field, an open battleground and a cave.</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Define different zones</strong></p>\n<p>One key <strong>to help players find their ways </strong>in your level is to <strong>define a few very different zones</strong>. The goal is to enable the player to think “ok, I’m in this area” <strong>at first sight</strong>.</p>\n<p>We’ll have the time to go back on this subject later, but just start to think about it when you’ll imagine your level through the <strong>Designer’s Template</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Justify your blockers</strong></p>\n<p>It’s often difficult to find an explanation to every LD’s blockers, because there are plenty of them in each level.</p>\n<p>Usually, you can explain them with a few well placed props. Look at the props you already have in your level, and try to think how you could use them differently. Some props can be placed lying on the ground to become blockers (i.e. closets, fallen trees, etc).</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a6539f8c0f842c6b8efc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a6539f8c0f842c6b8efc.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"394\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 101\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This doesn’t look like a blocker at all, the player may think he has to go this way.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3bef65eeced4ca8600e1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3bef65eeced4ca8600e1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"369\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 102\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This is also a barrier with some barbwires, but it reall looks more like a blocker. No one could think the path goes further.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/31b425494e1799ecd64e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/31b425494e1799ecd64e.webp\" width=\"960\" height=\"410\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 103\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Sometimes, a simple blocker is enough</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3b29944b9bd2b0cc98bd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3b29944b9bd2b0cc98bd.webp\" width=\"929\" height=\"867\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 104\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>You can often reuse some props you already have to block a path</em></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7f6c291890f116ac734d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7f6c291890f116ac734d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"468\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 105\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Destruction makes easy blockers</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4dac235600b0a5914c4a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4dac235600b0a5914c4a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"593\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 106\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Don’t forget to think in 3 dimensions! You can alternate tall blockers with gaps. Gaps or cliffs have the advantage to block the player without breaking the line of sight.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Compose pictures &amp; scenes</strong></p>\n<p>Having hard time to compose a level from nothing? Take some time to review your <strong>basics</strong>!</p>\n<p><strong>&gt;&gt; Identify your “picture frames”</strong></p>\n<p>It’s easier to create a nice visual composition when the “<strong>frame</strong>” is clearly defined. <strong>Opened windows </strong>and doors are very useful of course, but there are many more: <strong>Straight corridors</strong>, <strong>Level’s entry points</strong>, <strong>scripted camera events</strong>, and more.</p>\n<p>Try to identify it from the <strong>beginning</strong> and you will <strong>know exactly where to put your efforts</strong>.</p>\n<p>Each window, door, or natural opening gives you the opportunity to realize a well done composition.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/52b4c4b697f4e9644e92.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/52b4c4b697f4e9644e92.webp\" width=\"924\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 107\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/831aa29485900c906c09.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/831aa29485900c906c09.webp\" width=\"1109\" height=\"623\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 108\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5f83513efa05011fd8c4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5f83513efa05011fd8c4.webp\" width=\"1109\" height=\"621\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 109\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>&gt;&gt; Care about balance</strong></p>\n<p>Balance doesn’t mean <strong>symmetry</strong>! Try to identify the places where the level design is perfectly symmetrical, and then <strong>break this symmetry </strong>by using a different artistic interpretation for each side. It will enhance the <strong>realism </strong>of your scene.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>&gt;&gt; Blank walls are NEVER interesting</strong></p>\n<p>Think about how to create a meaning for it or turn them into something else.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4c5d149ff5392965ed30.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4c5d149ff5392965ed30.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"429\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 110\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This flat ceiling is not very appealing… let’s turn it into something better, with the props we already have!</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/20f77c50f6ee79504e73.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/20f77c50f6ee79504e73.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"327\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 111\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Borders of the level can often be opened to offer nice scenery. Be careful though, avoid creating a false appeal for the player!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1aa85229ef4042b2925e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1aa85229ef4042b2925e.webp\" width=\"901\" height=\"531\" style=\"--source-width:135px\" alt=\"Illustration 112\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Dead Rising 2 (2010): Even when you need to keep some straight walls, try to use some additional geometry to make them interesting, catch player’s eyes, and give them a meaning.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d95eba1a18829ed4bdea.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d95eba1a18829ed4bdea.webp\" width=\"901\" height=\"531\" style=\"--source-width:135px\" alt=\"Illustration 113\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>COD7: Black Ops (2010): a few props and a lot of information in the wall’s texture can be enough.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/48a193ee3b6e16ef7ac2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/48a193ee3b6e16ef7ac2.webp\" width=\"900\" height=\"530\" style=\"--source-width:135px\" alt=\"Illustration 114\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Water puddles are a good way to add some contrast to a ground when it’s too dark and uniform</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d98596349343c7da3f05.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d98596349343c7da3f05.webp\" width=\"901\" height=\"530\" style=\"--source-width:135px\" alt=\"Illustration 115\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Casted shadows on a wall can make it rich and interesting, even without extra props or details.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2c02786b75883fa8b4e4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2c02786b75883fa8b4e4.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"316\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 116\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Ghost Recon Future Soldier (2012, here in Alpha stage) </em><em><strong>Four different zones </strong></em><em>of the same level. Pay attention to the </em><em><strong>different ground textures</strong></em><em>, the </em><em><strong>alternation of open and closed spaces</strong></em><em>, the use of </em><em><strong>organic and man-made elements </strong></em><em>to make </em><em><strong>each zone </strong></em><em>easily </em><em><strong>recognizable</strong></em><em>.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7444aaf291796851c98b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7444aaf291796851c98b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"317\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 117\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Define different zones</strong></p>\n<p><strong>&gt;&gt; Make your different zones clearly identifiable.</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>What you should avoid</strong></p>\n<p>CounterStrike (1999): Most games of the previous generation did not take care of differentiate their zones. In result, players often got lost during campaigns and needed a longer time to “learn” levels in multiplayer matches.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ba49c68b7d2d34b0006f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ba49c68b7d2d34b0006f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"886\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 118\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/934016729c5a79e77345.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/934016729c5a79e77345.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"924\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 119\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>What you should avoid</strong></p>\n<p>Anthem (2019): despite having great art direction and high quality assets, Anthem still falls short in regards to distinguishable landscapes.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>&gt;&gt; Backgrounds and silhouette skyline</strong></p>\n<p>The Mock-Up is also the moment to set up the <strong>silhouette</strong> of your background.</p>\n<p>For backgrounds element, the <strong>silhouette</strong> is much <strong>more important </strong>than the details or textures, and can give you the <strong>feeling of a particular place</strong> immediately.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/68a810a8884f56254b50.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/68a810a8884f56254b50.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"881\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 120\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Camera &amp; Scale</strong></p>\n<p><strong>&gt;&gt; Where to put details / what is the main view</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>main mistake </strong>of most Level Artist beginners is to <strong>create</strong> and <strong>judge</strong> their level while <strong>using only the editor </strong>(or 3D software) <strong>camera</strong>.</p>\n<p>The only <strong>important view </strong>is the one you will <strong>offer to the player</strong>, so <strong>test</strong> <strong>your level with the game camera as often as possible</strong></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/98948e6d0b1d1d7e5a12.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/98948e6d0b1d1d7e5a12.webp\" width=\"1043\" height=\"596\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 121\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>You can’t trust this view, because you are the ONLY ONE who will see your level like this.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/79aafae200e51d40e638.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/79aafae200e51d40e638.webp\" width=\"1043\" height=\"785\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 122\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This is how everybody will see you level!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/275af4dd8b37139ebf57.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/275af4dd8b37139ebf57.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"650\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 123\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>The “<strong>main view</strong>” is what 95% of players will see: <strong>the main path</strong>, focused on the <strong>main objectives</strong>.</p>\n<p>Everything else is <strong>bonus</strong>.</p>\n<p>Everything in the RED area should be greatly detailed, because the player can get close to it.</p>\n<p>The YELLOW area need less detail: it’s still in the gameplay area but since it’s above the camera, the player can’t get close to it.</p>\n<p>The BROWN area can be really low in props and details: it’s part of the background (non-gameplay area) so we are sure the camera will never get a closer view.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Don’t forget to take the <strong>camera design </strong>into account! The important things are not what is <strong>close to the player</strong>, but what take the <strong>most pixels in screen space</strong>!</p>\n<p>If your game scope is featuring some cutscenes with specific camera angles, ask from the beginning where are they supposed to be shot. Try to get the storyboard (if available) so you can know exactly what will come close to the camera</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7468be92bbaa4697793d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7468be92bbaa4697793d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"559\" style=\"--source-width:135px\" alt=\"Illustration 124\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>COD7: Black Ops (2010): Try to know from the beginning </em><em><strong>where the cutscenes will be shot in your level</strong></em><em>. It will avoid a lot of bad surprises, like this camera coming way to close of a low quality mesh.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/43bc91d5fe1332dcd9fc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/43bc91d5fe1332dcd9fc.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"444\" style=\"--source-width:135px\" alt=\"Illustration 125\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Diablo 3 (2011): In games with a “Top-Down” camera, most of your details should be focused </em><em><strong>on the ground</strong></em><em>, since the ground will occupy 80-90% of the total screen space.</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fabf1c3f83af85ff2826.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fabf1c3f83af85ff2826.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"603\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 126\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>COD6: Modern Warfare2 (2009): This game is a </em><em><strong>First Person Shooter</strong></em><em>, so the camera is </em><em><strong>located through the eyes of the character</strong></em><em>. You can keep a </em><em><strong>1:1 scale </strong></em><em>for your environment.</em></p></td><td><p><strong>&gt;&gt; Level metrics: the concept of SCALE</strong></p>\n<p>Depending of the type of game you are making, and especially the type of camera, you may have to adjust the general scale of your Level and your objects in order to give a feeling of realism.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4394ec0fe6a70ca9fcee.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4394ec0fe6a70ca9fcee.webp\" width=\"822\" height=\"656\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 127\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>World of Warcraft (2004): From a realistic point of view, every object here is FAR TOO BIG!! But since the game has a distant camera, the room must be bigger than real-life. If the scale of the room is bigger, they had to scale the objects too in order to keep a coherency between everything.</em></p></td><td><p><em><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ad135e445bdb07eb46d0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ad135e445bdb07eb46d0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"579\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 128\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></em><em>Gears of War (2006): This game uses an external camera. Notice how tall this corridor is (about 6m high, 4m wide) to allow the camera to move inside without colliding with the walls or the ceiling.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/165d86b3d4db70ccb939.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/165d86b3d4db70ccb939.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"473\" style=\"--source-width:259px\" alt=\"Illustration 129\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The concept of scale is also relative to the gameplay: check the number of enemies in each room and the character movement to know if you need to upscale some part of environment. </em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0debc2e5a55fcf9680a4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0debc2e5a55fcf9680a4.webp\" width=\"580\" height=\"282\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 130\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>COD7: Black Ops (2010): In a First Person Shooter, </em><em><strong>cover metrics are really important</strong></em><em>. If this Level Artist understood that, he could’ve used more time to </em><em><strong>polish</strong></em><em> the edge of this crappy asset!</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/46069f4e769834c28e2b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/46069f4e769834c28e2b.webp\" width=\"584\" height=\"280\" style=\"--source-width:286px\" alt=\"Illustration 131\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Prince of Persia (2008): in Platformer games, player metrics are crucial for the Level Artist. It allows you to know exactly where you </em><em><strong>can change a shape</strong></em><em> and where you </em><em><strong>can’t</strong></em><em>, where you need to </em><em><strong>keep a line straight</strong></em><em>, etc… </em></p></td><td><p><strong>&gt;&gt; Player metrics</strong></p>\n<p>Player metrics are every important value for the main character. It can concern <strong>navigation</strong>, <strong>jump heights</strong>, <strong>cover sizes</strong>, camera <strong>distances</strong>, etc. Learn them quickly to ensure your level will be <strong>fully optimized </strong>for the player.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h2>Topic 02: The Art of Level</h2>\n\n<p>scroll-bookmark-75  </p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>TOPIC INTRODUCTION</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>THE ART OF LEVEL</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>In 1983, players were excited to run Mario through a series of 2D planes; primitive shapes and simple colors were enough to convey the mood and tone. However, with the increasing pixel count of today's standards, levels have to be more detailed. This chapter, therefore, aims to help you build believable and attractive environments for video games.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0a41cc86a4de5dcece0b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0a41cc86a4de5dcece0b.webp\" width=\"767\" height=\"524\" style=\"--source-width:282px\" alt=\"Illustration 135\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5eed98126066bf3cd627.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5eed98126066bf3cd627.webp\" width=\"868\" height=\"975\" style=\"--source-width:263px\" alt=\"Illustration 136\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Chapter 04 - Level Concept Art Reading</strong></p>\n<p>Many Artists underestimate how much useful information can be found in Concept Arts. They just take a look at it and think “Ok, let’s do it!”  Tons of problems will come afterward: no logic, many missing details, problems of scale, wrong colors or ambiance that doesn’t match the client’s need, and most of all, a huge loss of time.</p>\n<p>Most problems can be avoided if you know how to look at a Reference Concept Art. Take enough time to gather all information you can get from it, and you will save a LOT of time later!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Chapter 05: Visual Composition</strong></p>\n<p>There are plenty of things that contribute to a level: assets, lighting, textures, ambiences and more. In this chapter, we’d like to introduce you to some of the Visual Composition tools and tips in Level Art.</p>\n<p>There will be 3 main parts:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Composition elements: </strong>we will provide you different tools to help improve not only the aesthetic but also the readability of your levels.</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Composition principles:</strong> this section will focus on how you put everything you know together harmoniously and meaningfully.</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Photography techniques: </strong>we will briefly introduce you to “Frame within frame”, “Rule of thirds”, and “Leading lines”, which can be useful in some cases.</li></ul></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5d8f34392b3a999e096d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5d8f34392b3a999e096d.webp\" width=\"1147\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:282px\" alt=\"Illustration 137\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b74c0e8849528d0523f9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b74c0e8849528d0523f9.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:263px\" alt=\"Illustration 138\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Chapter 06: Architecture Logic</strong></p>\n<p>About props, architecture, levels and video games,…</p>\n<p>Twenty years ago, video games were all about <strong>imagination</strong>. As long as the player understood what each element was, he could enjoy the gameplay and imagine all the rest.</p>\n<p>Today, we want <strong>more</strong>. Now technology allows us to display plenty of details, players need <strong>realistic environments</strong>. They need to feel the world around them as if it was <strong>the real one</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>A realistic environment is more than some photographic textures mapped on hi-poly models</strong>. To feel realistic, the environment needs to <strong>fit the same rules than the real world, the same logic</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Chapter 07: Reality Check</strong></p>\n<p>“<em>Because Reality does NOT always feel realistic in a video game!”</em></p>\n<p>We always talk about “realism” in video game environments, but we can’t achieve it by “copying reality”.</p>\n<p>Our goal is to achieve a <strong>“feeling of reality”</strong>. We must identify clearly what makes an environment feel realistic, and that’s primarily the purpose of this lesson. Through this document, we will see different aspect of reality, define if it feels realistic or not, and give some advice to improve the realism of your scene.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0b3ce81b051a3e81fdff.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0b3ce81b051a3e81fdff.webp\" width=\"877\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:282px\" alt=\"Illustration 139\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h3>Chapter 04: Level Concept Art Reading</h3>\n\n<p><strong>scroll-bookmark-86</strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>TABLE OF CONTENT</strong></p><p><strong>Chapter 04 - Level Concept Art Reading </strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>This chapter contains:</p>\n<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Level Architecture</li><li>Reveal every detail you can get</li><li>Know the scale of what you’re looking at</li><li>Materials</li><li>Shapes and structures</li><li>Game consideration</li><li>Conclusion &amp; checklist</li><li>Read a full level concept art</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Pro-tip</li><li>Some generic advice in concept art reading</li></ul></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>CONTENT</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>Many Artists underestimate how much useful information can be found in Concept Arts. They just take a look at it and think “Ok, let’s do it!”  Tons of problems will come afterward: <strong>no logic</strong>, <strong>many missing details</strong>, <strong>problems of scale</strong>, <strong>wrong colors</strong> or <strong>ambiance</strong> that doesn’t match the client’s need, and most of all, <strong>a huge loss of time</strong>.</p>\n<p>Most problems can be avoided if you know how to look at a Reference Concept Art. Take enough time to gather all information you can get from it, and you will save a LOT of time later!</p>\n<p>The first part of this lesson will focus on reading a <strong>Level Architecture Concept Art: </strong>Understand how everything is made, understand the structure’s logic, how every material works together…</p>\n<p>Then we’ll approach some case of <strong>Full Level Concept Art</strong>:</p>\n<p>What are the main themes? Can we <strong>deduct a prop list </strong>from the picture? How <strong>the lighting is made</strong>? What are our<strong> first leads for visual storytelling</strong>?</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5eed98126066bf3cd627.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5eed98126066bf3cd627.webp\" width=\"868\" height=\"975\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 143\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/974f476f44117a0bc0e9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/974f476f44117a0bc0e9.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"621\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 144\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Reading a Concept Art for Level Architecture is both simpler and more difficult than reading one for a full level.</strong></em></p>\n<p><em>This time we don’t care about </em><em><strong>props, lighting</strong></em><em>, </em><em><strong>VFX </strong></em><em>or </em><em><strong>post-process</strong></em><em>. At least, you don’t need in appearance, but props here can teach you a lot about how to think your architecture!!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/42dd476b90089f1e02f9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/42dd476b90089f1e02f9.webp\" width=\"864\" height=\"593\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 145\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Don’t misread this template: </em><em><strong>it doesn’t mean we just have to put a texture on walls and platform</strong></em><em>! We can add a lot of details, as long as the general shape stays the same</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Level Architecture</strong></p>\n<p>First, let’s take a look at the grey box template to <strong>know exactly what the client expects from us</strong>:</p>\n<p>As we can see on the template, we only need to create the <strong>ground</strong>, <strong>walls</strong>, <strong>ceiling</strong>, <strong>platform</strong>, <strong>doors</strong>, <strong>stairs</strong> and <strong>catwalk</strong>. No need to lose time on small details then!</p>\n<p>The most important things to take care are:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Architecture style </strong>(shapes, volumes)</li><li><strong>Types of materials</strong> (different metals, wood,…)</li><li><strong>Architecture logic</strong> (how things are attached together a logical way)</li><li><strong>Textures</strong></li><li><strong>Unity</strong> through this part of level (how everything fits together and seems to belong to the same building)</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p>If you have any doubt about what you should create and what you should ignore, just imagine you run a building company and you want to rent this place to a new lodger: Every solid structure will be there, only objects are missing since they will be provided by the lodger himself when he will move in!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d56d349c558f593b8f30.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d56d349c558f593b8f30.webp\" width=\"990\" height=\"655\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 146\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/62e293796d32647ce3f2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/62e293796d32647ce3f2.webp\" width=\"1131\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 147\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Reveal every detail you can get</strong></p>\n<p>First, we can notice this picture is pretty <strong>dark</strong>. There’s a lot of place where it’s very <strong>difficult to see details</strong>, like on <strong>the sides</strong>, <strong>along the walls </strong>(the most important for us!!) or <strong>under the platform</strong>. Let’s tweak those levels in Photoshop in order to have a better look!</p>\n<p>Many details appear now, some are really important for us like:</p>\n<ul><li>The exact <strong>geometry</strong> of those <strong>metal walls</strong></li><li>The exact <strong>shape</strong> of the <strong>ceiling</strong></li><li>The <strong>real material </strong>of the <strong>floor</strong></li><li>Many <strong>texture hints</strong></li><li>The <strong>detailed geometry </strong>under the platform, how everything is <strong>fixed</strong></li></ul>\n<p>The <strong>right wall </strong>is a <strong>totally different material</strong>, which means a lot for the rest of the level!</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Know the scale of what you’re looking at</strong></p>\n<p>When we look at a concept art, it’s difficult to know the scale of everything.</p>\n<p>The best concept artists have a deep understanding of scale and can create some very realistic pictures, but <strong>most of them just draw without caring about it</strong>. It results in some pictures impossible to re-create in 3D!</p>\n<p>Let’s check the scale of this picture:</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5ed9dba7392879cb6159.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5ed9dba7392879cb6159.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"622\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 148\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>First, find some objects with a well-known scale: here we have railings. Usually, railings are 90cm or 100cm high to allow people to hold it and prevent them for falling. For smaller scale, we could choose stairs’ steps, which are usually 15 to 20cm high.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dac573419b9df1ebc325.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dac573419b9df1ebc325.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"470\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 149\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Now we have some strong scale reference, we can easily guess the scale of everything else by tracing some perspective lines (follow natural lines in the picture, like floor tiles’ junctions, steps, etc.</em></p>\n<p><em>This picture seems to have a pretty correct scale (we can check machines’ control panels; it’s exactly at hands and eyes level.) This means everything here can be used as reference. You don’t need to spend too much time drawing these lines; it’s just for the example. You should be able to imagine it, to visually check the scale.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c11afd7a32497b9eb214.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c11afd7a32497b9eb214.webp\" width=\"686\" height=\"634\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 150\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>A) Painted metal:</strong></em><em><br></em><em> </em><em><br></em><em>Almost everything is made of </em><em><strong>painted metal</strong></em><em>. </em><em><strong>Time</strong></em><em> scattered the paint layer and we see different kind of </em><em><strong>rust </strong></em><em>under, depending of what kind of metal it is. Two </em><em><strong>walls</strong></em><em> are made of </em><em><strong>corrugated steel </strong></em><em>while the </em><em><strong>pipes</strong></em><em> and </em><em><strong>beams</strong></em><em> are made of </em><em><strong>painted iron </strong></em><em>(you can guess because there is </em><em><strong>some rust </strong></em><em>where the </em><em><strong>anti rust paint went off</strong></em><em>).</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Materials</strong></p>\n<p>We now know what we need to build (from the grey box template), we have revealed all the hidden details, and we have the approximate scale of everything. We are now ready to check HOW to do it!</p>\n<p>Notice we are <strong>not talking </strong>about the <strong>shader’s material </strong>(blinn, phong, etc) but the <strong>material</strong> every <strong>object</strong> is made of! (Metal, wood, concrete, etc).</p>\n<p>Before thinking about <strong>geometry</strong>, think how those things are made in <strong>real life</strong>. Once you know the <strong>real material</strong>, you will know how to do a <strong>realistic geometry</strong> and a <strong>realistic texture</strong>!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7f87c707b34ade5d4fdc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7f87c707b34ade5d4fdc.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"459\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 151\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>B)     Concrete tiled floor :</strong></em><em><br></em><em> </em><em><br></em><em>At first glance, it’s difficult to understand the material of the ground since it’s</em><em><strong> covered by debris</strong></em><em> (left picture).</em></p>\n<p><em>However, when we lighten the picture a bit, we can notice some large concrete tiles in a corner of the picture (right picture). It’s obviously the primary material to use. We can add the rubbles later with texture blend or vertex painting, depending</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/243e3701428bd0b23c2c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/243e3701428bd0b23c2c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"528\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 152\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>C)     Steel floor tiles  :</strong></em><em><br></em><em> </em><em><br></em><em>The platform is made of </em><em><strong>wide antislip </strong></em><em>steel panels.</em></p>\n<p><em>Notice that this surface is </em><em><strong>very dirty</strong></em><em>. Don’t be afraid to use a texture </em><em><strong>containing a lot of information</strong></em><em>. You can build this by tiles and then rotating them in every direction to break the tiling !</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/be441c013f54e80eed4c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/be441c013f54e80eed4c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"208\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 153\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>D)    Raw steel:</strong></em><em><strong><br></strong></em><em><strong> </strong></em><em><br></em><em>The railings are obviously made of a different metal: the specular is </em><em><strong>very sharp </strong></em><em>and </em><em><strong>intense</strong></em><em>, so we can deduct there’s </em><em><strong>no paint </strong></em><em>here.  Here again, don’t hesitate to use a maximum of much </em><em><strong>contrasted information </strong></em><em>in the diffuse and especially in the specular map.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7fbba35a7328c1016ea2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7fbba35a7328c1016ea2.webp\" width=\"1073\" height=\"566\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 154\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>E)     Alternative raw steel:</strong></em><em><br></em><em> The material composing the stairs is </em><em><strong>not very well defined</strong></em><em>. However, we can be pretty sure it’s another kind of steel, concerning the </em><em><strong>sharpness</strong></em><em> and </em><em><strong>intensity</strong></em><em> of the specular and the particular shape of those steps.</em></p>\n<p><em>Since this material </em><em><strong>is only used by those stairs</strong></em><em>, feel free to create a unique normal map to bring maximum details into this shape</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><em><strong>F)     Stripes painted danger signs:</strong></em></p>\n<p><em>There’s not much information the artist gave us about those stripes. Judging by the specular on the edge and the curved scratch on the black stripe, we can assume this is also some metal plates.</em></p>\n<p><em>The easiest method here would be to create those plates separately, then instantiate it wherever we want in our level</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8db6c56fe69ee89e2b8f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8db6c56fe69ee89e2b8f.webp\" width=\"710\" height=\"450\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 155\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/57adea60da759637d3da.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/57adea60da759637d3da.webp\" width=\"1045\" height=\"455\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 156\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>G) Opaque glass or Plexiglas</strong></em></p>\n<p><em>The </em><em><strong>ceiling</strong></em><em> of this hangar is made of a </em><em><strong>flat</strong></em><em>, </em><em><strong>slick</strong></em><em>, </em><em><strong>non-reflective material</strong></em><em>. It can be some kind of </em><em><strong>opaque glass </strong></em><em>or </em><em><strong>Plexiglas tiles</strong></em><em>.</em></p>\n<p><em>This one is the </em><em><strong>less important </strong></em><em>if we consider the </em><em><strong>position of the main light source</strong></em><em>: this part will be </em><em><strong>almost hidden by darkness</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e588bfdd9b08bd16474a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e588bfdd9b08bd16474a.webp\" width=\"568\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 157\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>H) Reinforced hard concrete wall</strong></em></p>\n<p><em>Last but not least:</em></p>\n<p><em>If we lighten the whole picture to a certain level, we can notice the </em><em><strong>right wall </strong></em><em>is not made of </em><em><strong>corrugated steel </strong></em><em>and </em><em><strong>metal</strong></em><em> </em><em><strong>beams</strong></em><em> like the two other one, but a kind of </em><em><strong>concrete reinforced</strong></em><em> with </em><em><strong>metal beams and rivets</strong></em><em>.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>This material is really important</strong></em><em>, because it </em><em><strong>changes the architecture type </strong></em><em>of the whole building! We are probably in a </em><em><strong>hangar</strong></em><em>, </em><em><strong>attached to the left of a main building made of concrete</strong></em><em>. This means </em><em><strong>behind the left</strong></em><em> and </em><em><strong>face up walls </strong></em><em>this is outside, but </em><em><strong>behind the right walls the building continue</strong></em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/973908380d378101ec50.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/973908380d378101ec50.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"535\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 158\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Here, the Photoshop level tweaking we did in the first place can reveal the whole structure of the platform.  Those </em><em><strong>metal tiles are not the platform itself</strong></em><em>: they are </em><em><strong>supported</strong></em><em> by a </em><em><strong>heavy structure of metal beams</strong></em><em>!</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8b9f7f35da357e1108ed.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8b9f7f35da357e1108ed.webp\" width=\"1146\" height=\"704\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 159\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Shapes and structures</strong></p>\n<p><strong>A)</strong>  <strong>Understand the building’s structure</strong></p>\n<p>Now we know about the materials, we can think about the building structure, and <strong>how everything stands together as one piece</strong>. Think with logic: the <strong>most solid </strong>material can <strong>handle</strong> some <strong>less solid ones</strong>; <strong>heavy</strong> materials <strong>can’t be supported </strong>by <strong>light materials</strong>, etc. You should quickly be able to identify the structure of your building.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>B) Proceed to piece level</strong></p>\n<p>From the biggest structure, proceed to a smaller one. Continue your reading by going to <strong>smaller</strong> and <strong>smaller pieces</strong>. For each piece, <strong>try to understand where this element is attached</strong>, <strong>what is supporting it</strong>, etc.</p>\n<p>Often, the <strong>direct attach </strong>point is <strong>not represented </strong>in the <strong>concept art</strong>, so you have to guess by yourself. <strong>Metal pieces </strong>can be <strong>welded together </strong>or <strong>riveted</strong>, <strong>wood planks </strong>can be <strong>nailed</strong> or <strong>screwed</strong>, <strong>temporary constructions</strong> can be just <strong>attached</strong> by <strong>wires</strong> or <strong>tapes</strong>, etc.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fe4e9f13536a2da8e185.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fe4e9f13536a2da8e185.webp\" width=\"1052\" height=\"522\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 160\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1862673d0c6b4fc63513.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1862673d0c6b4fc63513.webp\" width=\"826\" height=\"792\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 161\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>C) Identify any modular parts</strong></p>\n<p>We now know <strong>how everything is attached together</strong>. We can now look at our concept art to identify <strong>which parts </strong>are <strong>modular</strong> and <strong>can be reused </strong>through our entire scene.</p>\n<p>There are two benefits in this method:</p>\n<ul><li>First, the <strong>realism</strong>: in real life, we always use <strong>manufactured pieces</strong> to build anything. Those pieces are created in <strong>large quantities</strong>, so it’s <strong>logica</strong>l to find many <strong>small identical parts</strong> instead of <strong>unique large ones</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>Then, there is a <strong>huge gain of time </strong>and <strong>quality</strong> to think with pieces. Just focus on the <strong>quality of a small piece</strong>, then <strong>duplicate</strong> it and <strong>rotate/flip </strong>it in many directions to <strong>break</strong> every eventual <strong>tiling effect</strong>. This method also <strong>helps</strong> to keep a <strong>better texture ratio</strong> and allow you to <strong>display more information </strong>in your textures.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>D)     Stripes painted danger signs:</strong></p>\n<p>There’s not much information the artist gave us about those stripes. Judging by the specular on the edge and the curved scratch on the black stripe, we can assume this is also some metal plates.</p>\n<p>The easiest method here would be to create those plates separately, then instantiate it wherever we want in our level.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3cb95aadb9e0481cf3b8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3cb95aadb9e0481cf3b8.webp\" width=\"577\" height=\"87\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 162\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>We can see some correct references of </em><em><strong>attachments along joints</strong></em><em> to fix them to the wall. However, these pipes </em><em><strong>can’t be attached directly to the wall</strong></em><em>, because there are some </em><em><strong>huge beams between the pipes </strong></em><em>and </em><em><strong>the wall</strong></em><em>.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3b01d5c46f260d888410.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3b01d5c46f260d888410.webp\" width=\"577\" height=\"96\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 163\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>One you’ve notice it, you can </em><em><strong>put these attachments at their right place </strong></em><em>and </em><em><strong>fix these pipes to the beams instead of the wall</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3d2e9c1210249b1397c4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3d2e9c1210249b1397c4.webp\" width=\"711\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 164\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Here is another mistake: the </em><em><strong>metal plate is blended in the concrete ground</strong></em><em>. In real life, those two materials </em><em><strong>can’t be blended</strong></em><em>!</em></p>\n<p><em>Even if the metal plate is </em><em><strong>covered by dust</strong></em><em>, it should </em><em><strong>keep its shape</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a4cda902a8c719ffae3b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a4cda902a8c719ffae3b.webp\" width=\"554\" height=\"308\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 165\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>According to the </em><em><strong>size of the character </strong></em><em>and the </em><em><strong>position of the camera</strong></em><em>, we can guess the areas colored in </em><em><strong>green (playable ground) </strong></em><em>and </em><em><strong>blue (surroundings at player’s scale)</strong></em><em> will be </em><em><strong>approached </strong></em><em>by the camera </em><em><strong>often</strong></em><em>. The area displayed in </em><em><strong>yellow will never be approached </strong></em><em>by the camera, so you can afford to create </em><em><strong>fewer details</strong></em><em> or a </em><em><strong>smaller texture ratio / UV size</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Game consideration</strong></p>\n<p>Never forget that your models will be <strong>used for playing inside</strong>. Even while reading your reference concept art, try to identify what will be <strong>close to the camera </strong>and what will be <strong>far</strong>.</p>\n<p>Remember that the <strong>silhouettes are more important</strong> <strong>than texture when a mesh is viewed from a distance </strong>(example: <strong>ceiling details </strong>and <strong>depth</strong>, or <strong>shapes of windows</strong>).</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Conclusion &amp; checklist</strong></p>\n<p>Now you know how much important information you can discover if you watch your reference concept arts carefully! Here is a convenient checklist to sum up everything we’ve learned and ensure you didn’t forget anything:</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a56b987cd59cf12c7b75.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a56b987cd59cf12c7b75.webp\" width=\"565\" height=\"457\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 166\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Read a full level concept art</strong></p>\n<p>Concept Arts for Level Building are carrying much more information, but are often less detailed than Level Architecture Concept Arts. Let’s see what we should focus when “reading” this kind of picture before building a level.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Theme:</strong> What is the <strong>main intention </strong>of the level? What does it <strong>mean</strong>? What kind of <strong>story</strong> is told here?</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Importance, specific focus:</strong> What is <strong>specifically highlighted </strong>in this picture? Is it the <strong>lighting</strong>, the <strong>ambiance</strong> or some <strong>specific objects</strong>? Sometimes the <strong>gameplay can be displayed </strong>too, and give <strong>hints about how to build the level</strong>!</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Props set &amp; composition:</strong> Can you <strong>build a prop list </strong>from this? What seems to be the <strong>most important </strong>objects?</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Ambiance:</strong> What kind of <strong>feelings</strong> is expressed here? What the player should <strong>experience</strong>?</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Lighting &amp; Colors</strong>: What are the <strong>main colors</strong>? How the <strong>lighting </strong>is done? What are the <strong>main light sources</strong>? <strong> </strong></li><li><strong>VFX, post process:</strong> Is there some <strong>specific VFX </strong>or <strong>full screen post-process </strong>you can guess from the picture?</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Pro-tip</strong></p>\n<p><strong>CONCEPT ART 1</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Beware of misreading!</strong> <strong>Some common mistakes here could be:</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Seeing the colors but <strong>forgetting about the contrast</strong>, or <strong>paint the whole sky in grey-blue </strong>and the whole <strong>ground lighted by orange flames</strong>. Forgetting the concept of <strong>small light zones</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Using a <strong>wrong saturation</strong>: either <strong>desaturate the whole picture by post-processing</strong>, or <strong>putting bricks colors everywhere</strong> instead of <strong>covering the landscape with ashes and dusts</strong>, and <strong>punctuate it with colored details</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Not paying attention to <strong>details</strong>: the <strong>sharp metal scrap</strong>, the <strong>laying skeletons</strong>, the <strong>complex ground </strong>and <strong>shrapnels</strong> could <strong>easily be missed </strong>if you don’t look at this picture long enough.</li></ul>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6e544998a3204daba9d8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6e544998a3204daba9d8.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"575\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 167\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Lots of <strong>look-alike elements</strong> = <strong>modular level composition</strong> (kit of static meshes).</p>\n<ul><li>Theme: <strong>ossuary</strong>, <strong>human skeletons</strong> &amp; <strong>architectural skeletons </strong>as well.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>The <strong>sky isn’t so dark</strong>, but the <strong>sunlight is less bright</strong> than usual lights.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>The lighting is made by zone: <strong>diffuse dark grey-blue ambiance</strong>  with <strong>localized orange-yellow bright points</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Buildings are a <strong>mix of modern architecture </strong>(<strong>metal structures, metal sheets</strong>) and <strong>older architecture </strong>(<strong>red bricks</strong>).</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Pay attention to <strong>silhouettes</strong>: the <strong>sharp metal scrap perforated by bullets</strong> seems really <strong>important </strong>because of its <strong>position</strong>: <strong>higher than the median line </strong>means something that will <strong>give the player a feeling of danger and strong memories</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>The picture is <strong>desaturated</strong>, but NOT by <strong>post-process</strong> (some <strong>saturated color spots </strong>are still <strong>visible</strong>) but by <strong>dust </strong>and <strong>ashes</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>The <strong>ground is very complex</strong>: <strong>irregular</strong> and <strong>covered by mess</strong>, <strong>scraps</strong>, <strong>bones</strong> and <strong>dust</strong>. We can imagine a <strong>character collision lower </strong>than the <strong>visual</strong>, to feel <strong>their feet buried </strong>under the <strong>ashes</strong> as they walk.</li><li><strong>Thick fog</strong>, <strong>glowing dust </strong>near <strong>light sources</strong>, <strong>flying ashes </strong>and s<strong>hrapnels</strong> = the atmosphere is <strong>dense</strong> and <strong>saturated</strong>.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>CONCEPT ART 2</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n<p>This one is much more complex for a level artist, because it seems to show only <strong>characters</strong> and <strong>vehicles</strong>. Anyway, with a good interpretation, you can also find some useful details!</p>\n<p>The <strong>sky</strong> seems to be <strong>very bright</strong>, but we only see a <strong>very small portion </strong>of it. Also, there is <strong>not much light </strong>on the <strong>ground level</strong>. This means the <strong>buildings of this street must be very tall</strong>, with some <strong>strong silhouettes by the skyline</strong>.</p>\n<p>If we look closely, we can notice there are <strong>different layers in the background</strong>: the <strong>far layer </strong>is <strong>very blurred</strong>, but a smaller building is <strong>roughly drawn with sharp brushes</strong>. We can conclude there are <strong>different sizes of building</strong>, maybe built on <strong>different times </strong>and <strong>never destructed</strong>.</p>\n<p>Looking at the buildings again, they’re all drawn with <strong>large squares brushes</strong>, with lot of <strong>contrast</strong> and <strong>shadows</strong>: like blocs. This seems to confirm what we saw before: this city has been built by <strong>adding extra components </strong>on the top of <strong>existing buildings </strong>instead of destroying them and build something new; maybe a “Blade Runner” inspiration.</p>\n<p>We can’t see much of the ground, but <strong>colors</strong> and <strong>bullet splashes </strong>tend to a <strong>rough</strong>, <strong>sandy ground</strong>, <strong>soaked by rain </strong>or living people in <strong>poor conditions</strong>.</p>\n<p>There’s <strong>no sign of urbanism</strong>, which means we are now in a <strong>secondary street</strong>, not a <strong>main axis</strong>.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/27b3039ef1d438aef0f0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/27b3039ef1d438aef0f0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 168\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Anyway, a great question remains about these <strong>two rusty sticks</strong> in the foreground: there can be some really different meanings:</p>\n<ul><li>Maybe it's a <strong>barricade</strong>, so it means the city has been <strong>drawn in war for a long time</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Maybe it's a <strong>piece of broken vehicle</strong>, like the one in the <strong>second plane</strong>, so it means there’s <strong>more than just one vehicle</strong>, maybe a bombed convoy.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Maybe it's just <strong>some piece of metal scraps</strong>, trashed away by the population, so this street is probably filled by <strong>more garbage</strong>, <strong>randomly thrown away </strong>in the dirt.</li></ul>\n<p>Each option leads to a totally <strong>different level</strong> so when in doubt, <strong>ask the concept artist </strong>or <strong>your lead </strong>for more details before taking a wrong direction, you will save some time !</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>CONCEPT ART 3</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n<p>This one is a very far view of the whole level. The main character would only take a few pixels in this view and the camera would be close to him. Impossible to guess anything about props or composition. What can we take from it?</p>\n<p>Take a close look at geometry! Every instruction is here:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Alternation</strong> between <strong>rocks</strong> and <strong>grass</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Contrast</strong> between <strong>hard rock’s lines </strong>and <strong>round grass shapes</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>How to use <strong>lines</strong> and <strong>silhouettes</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>Remember that any “<strong>small detail</strong>” in this view will be a <strong>major topology shape </strong>in <strong>player’s view</strong>!</li></ul>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f6fc099e5a459e70a464.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f6fc099e5a459e70a464.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"560\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 169\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Some generic advice in concept art reading</strong></p>\n<p>Seeing the concept art is <strong>not enough</strong>, you have to <strong>pay attention to every single detail </strong>and <strong>think of every aspect </strong>to finally “digest” the picture. When you can close your eyes and live in the universe you've seen on the picture, you've looked at it long enough.</p>\n<p>Your level is <strong>not bound by the concept art</strong>: you can <strong>add you own things</strong>, but at the only condition you have <strong>reached the concept art before</strong>. First <strong>reach the concept</strong>, AND THEN go <strong>beyond</strong>.</p>\n<p>When does the level reach the concept art? When you take a look at the screen, if during a single second, you think to have seen <strong>the same thing in the level and on the concept art</strong>, you’ve<strong> succeeded</strong>!</p>\n<p><strong>Communication is always a good thing</strong>: when you hesitate about something, ask the <strong>concept artist</strong>, <strong>your lead </strong>or <strong>your art director </strong>instead of <strong>taking a random direction</strong>. Time is gold, and you often can't afford to lose some by restarting everything you've done because of a misreading.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h3>Chapter 05: Visual Composition</h3>\n\n<p><strong> </strong><strong>scroll-bookmark-101</strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>TABLE OF CONTENT</strong></p><p><strong>Chapter 05: Visual Composition</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>This chapter contains:</p>\n<ul><li>Lines: vertical</li><li>Lines: horizontal</li><li>Lines: curved</li><li>Negative space</li><li>Colors</li><li>Balance</li><li>Asymmetrical balance</li><li>Symmetrical balance</li><li>Other types of balance</li><li>Emphasis</li><li>Rhythm</li><li>Unity</li><li>Frame within frame</li><li>Rule of thirds</li><li>Leading lines</li></ul></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>CONTENT</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>There are plenty of things that contribute to a level: assets, lighting, textures, ambiences and more. In this chapter, we’d like to introduce you to some of the Visual Composition tools and tips in Level Art.</p>\n<p>There will be 3 main parts:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Composition elements: </strong>we will provide you different tools to help improve not only the aesthetic but also the readability of your levels.</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Composition principles:</strong> this section will focus on how you put everything you know together harmoniously and meaningfully.</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Photography techniques: </strong>we will briefly introduce you to “Frame within frame”, “Rule of thirds”, and “Leading lines”, which can be useful in some cases.</li></ul>\n<p>Before we begin, keep in mind that none of these is set in stone. It’s all about the intentions behind the level creation. Depending on what type of story or emotion you want to deliver, you can choose to utilize or to break these rules. Let’s be flexible.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f555694ff79576dd3ab2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f555694ff79576dd3ab2.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 173\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/04517d957ba5a96f538d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/04517d957ba5a96f538d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 174\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/92c276d0438883ec1de8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/92c276d0438883ec1de8.webp\" width=\"911\" height=\"731\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 175\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cf6d92815d4d0f46fc87.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cf6d92815d4d0f46fc87.webp\" width=\"912\" height=\"584\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 176\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Lines: vertical</strong></p>\n<p>Lines are our primary tool to communicate direction (leading line) and length.</p>\n<p>Vertical lines usually present scale and power. Since we are overwhelmed by things taller than us, you can use this line to impress players. It can be a reveal of something enormous or monumental.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Lines: horizontal</strong></p>\n<p>Horizontal lines, on the other hand, are calm or peaceful because they are usually horizons. You can use this line in scenes with low stress level. This is where your players will take some rest and have some time to appreciate the scenery. However, don’t limit yourself to just “the sea” or “the horizon”.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7cccfa2284d85aa2e287.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7cccfa2284d85aa2e287.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 177\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fe273d9e6f2b2dbf6888.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fe273d9e6f2b2dbf6888.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"536\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 178\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cffac9f7d25c7732f9fa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cffac9f7d25c7732f9fa.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"312\" style=\"--source-width:132px\" alt=\"Illustration 179\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Thanks to its calming nature, horizontal lines can help elevate the peaceful and pleasing aspects of an area. You can see this a lot in low-stress interior areas like bedroom.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cb2e8fbf794c649279e8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cb2e8fbf794c649279e8.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"318\" style=\"--source-width:132px\" alt=\"Illustration 180\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>But as we already said at the beginning, these rules are not absolute. You can have a chaotic area with many horizontal lines.</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Lines: curved</strong></p>\n<p>Curved lines give a sense of meandering and make your composition softer and more natural. They can come in handy when you build landscapes or natural environments.</p>\n<p>But that doesn’t mean you can’t use them for man-made environments.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4984866d880b44e624c7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4984866d880b44e624c7.webp\" width=\"1108\" height=\"624\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 181\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6073e116c51a2b250a5b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6073e116c51a2b250a5b.webp\" width=\"933\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 182\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Negative space</strong></p>\n<p>Negative space refers to the space around or between the subject(s). Photographers utilize this concept to present shapes, sizes and compositions in a more contrived manner. The main idea is to put emphasis on the focal point of the composition. Because when everything fights for attention, nothing gains attention.</p>\n<p>In the first picture, the white space behind the character strengthens his silhouette, making him more imposing. As for the second picture, the sky forms negative space to contrast with all elements on the ground, so you can tell them apart.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3e2aeb7b41e424e09384.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3e2aeb7b41e424e09384.webp\" width=\"821\" height=\"921\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 183\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Well-defined shapes and good use of negative space make each element of this scene distinguishable even when shadows cast broadly over it.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f5a455834c08dc0328b4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f5a455834c08dc0328b4.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"492\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 184\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/01d4c1d55828d543b800.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/01d4c1d55828d543b800.webp\" width=\"889\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 185\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Breath of the Wild has a lot of negative space to highlight its landmarks. Thanks to the empty space, you can easily spot unusual objects which in turns urges you to explore.</p>\n<p>You can see same principle applied to the upcoming Gods and Monsters. As for Middle Earth: Shadow of War, your main objectives in the game is to takeover the castles/citadels, so the game puts less emphasis on the surrounding areas.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/881d90cb7e9e7d3d201b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/881d90cb7e9e7d3d201b.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 186\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>In addition of emphasizing core elements in the environments (in this case, the giants), Shadow of the Colossus uses barren landscapes to evoke emptiness, loneliness, and makes the giants more intimidating.<a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3d2de32539d4afeebc8a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3d2de32539d4afeebc8a.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"484\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 187\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>This is the Facing Worlds map in Unreal Tournament. Even when you blur or add the palette filter, every element is still distinct and readable. Nothing blends together.<a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/82db1ce778b297139385.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/82db1ce778b297139385.webp\" width=\"916\" height=\"655\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 188\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5416295f4b21ff921c07.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5416295f4b21ff921c07.webp\" width=\"937\" height=\"671\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 189\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1ee6ebfe070f8c54a3cf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1ee6ebfe070f8c54a3cf.webp\" width=\"937\" height=\"675\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 190\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a5dc5df8d2d4221a6e47.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a5dc5df8d2d4221a6e47.webp\" width=\"852\" height=\"980\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 191\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>But not every level has to embrace negative space. The Favela map in COD: Modern Warfare 2 is a rendition of a real life location. Therefore, the game has to prioritize realism over gameplay. As you can see, when we blur and apply filter to the picture, everything in the town blends together, which could get the player lost. While the player might feel uncomfortable, this is the intended experience.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b17e1af9e96b05f6ab59.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b17e1af9e96b05f6ab59.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"321\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 192\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Assassin’s Creed: Odyssey’s attempt at recreating ancient Greece is remarkable. While the environments can be visually overwhelming at times, they are faithful and effective in delivering a realistic and immersive experience. In conclusion, there will be some cases where you can ignore negative space for the sake of something more important – as long as the game specifically asks for it.<a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b6d4bb73ce8d595a8be5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b6d4bb73ce8d595a8be5.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"431\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 193\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Colors</strong></p>\n<p>Color is the most expressive visual tool of all. It can manipulate one’s feeling or perception toward the subject matter. And due to this ambiguous nature, it’s extremely hard to pin down exactly how to use colors in every situation. But at the same time, this ambiguity offers unlimited possibilities for practical applications.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f6c70e8d93ecf25a7de5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f6c70e8d93ecf25a7de5.webp\" width=\"1109\" height=\"625\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 194\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/19370f7b532f35d37bf3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/19370f7b532f35d37bf3.webp\" width=\"1081\" height=\"625\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 195\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Realistic vs fantastical</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f79d7214c1ef01d6433f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f79d7214c1ef01d6433f.webp\" width=\"890\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 196\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>There are two elements we need to consider when using colors: value and saturation.</p>\n<p><strong>Value</strong> is the lightness or darkness of a color. It can be increased or decreased by adding white or black, or by increasing or decreasing the intensity of the lighting.</p>\n<p><strong>Saturation</strong> refers to the intensity of a color. In good hand, saturation can manipulate the mood and the feeling of the scene, or draw attention to the author’s desired area/spot.</p>\n<p>With value and saturation together, you technically have an infinite number of color combinations. But how you use them is up to your intention, and the story you want to tell the player.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Colors are widely known for theirs association with emotions.</p>\n<ul><li>Red is dangerous.</li><li>Green is safe.</li><li>Purple is suspicious.</li><li>Blue is calm.</li></ul></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/000bcf2014928ac9b574.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/000bcf2014928ac9b574.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 197\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1ec43c702372c0c8f379.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1ec43c702372c0c8f379.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"643\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 198\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>But colors aren’t fixed to any specific emotion.</p>\n<ul><li>Red can be love or passion.</li><li>Green can be toxic.</li><li>Purple can be majestic.</li><li>Blue can be cold.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Balance</strong></p>\n<p>Human tend to unconsciously divide the screen into two equal halves and use the center point as a reference for balance. You can call that center point a “fulcrum”.</p>\n<p>Visual weight refer to <strong>color</strong>, <strong>value</strong>, and <strong>size</strong>: hot vs cold, dark vs light, large vs small, etc.</p>\n<p>By appropriately distributing these elements on each side, artists can enhance the mood or the narrative, or even manipulate the player’s emotion.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3ea4695edafbb232e9a3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3ea4695edafbb232e9a3.webp\" width=\"602\" height=\"526\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 199\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5f7422f95efcae3ed305.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5f7422f95efcae3ed305.webp\" width=\"892\" height=\"934\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 200\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Asymmetrical balance</strong></p>\n<p>People usually associate balance with symmetry, but that is not necessarily the case, which is why you should have a look at asymmetrical balance first. It happens when artists place objects of different sizes and weights unevenly around the fulcrum to equalize each other.</p>\n<p>For instance, you can offset a large dominant element on one side with some smaller elements on the other.</p>\n<p>Asymmetrical balance delivers a higher level of visual tension and gives the player the opposite emotional effect – excitement, curiosity, or anxiety – compared to symmetrical balance.</p>\n<p>Since asymmetry creates a lot of differences in a scene, it encourages people to explore and leads them around the space.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Symmetrical balance</strong></p>\n<p>First off, we need to make one thing clear that symmetry in balance <strong>doesn’t mean having the exact same look on both left and right</strong>. It actually refers to the balance of visual weight: <strong>light</strong> elements to balance <strong>light</strong> elements, <strong>heavy</strong> elements to balance <strong>heavy</strong> elements, etc.</p>\n<p>While asymmetrical compositions keep you feel on edge, symmetrical compositions ease your eyes and mind. They are <strong>peaceful</strong>, <strong>calm</strong> and even <strong>tranquility</strong> at times.</p>\n<p>Symmetrically balanced environments evoke a calm and formal feeling. But it can also feel isolating and intimidating depending on the context.</p>\n<p>The most interesting aspect of symmetrical balance is that it leads people through a space, not around it.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/93337380845ba60944a6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/93337380845ba60944a6.webp\" width=\"890\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 201\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/878ed25f6c822ad92c3d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/878ed25f6c822ad92c3d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"603\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 202\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/767ea1cc5104f22d47d4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/767ea1cc5104f22d47d4.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"651\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 203\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Other types of balance</strong></p>\n<p>Some other types of symmetrical balance includes:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Translatory symmetry</strong>: elements at the same height in the Y axis are copied from left to right.</li><li><strong>Rotational (Radial) symmetry</strong>: the surroundings elements are balanced around a common pivot.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Emphasis</strong></p>\n<p>Emphasis is a common concept. You should have a focal point in your level and design everything else to support it.</p>\n<p>Without a focal point, an image can seem <strong>drab</strong> and <strong>lifeless</strong>, as the eye does <strong>not have a particular place to look at</strong>, and is not rewarded for having tried.</p>\n<p>By manipulating the different elements in Image Composition, an artist can guide a viewer’s eye as he or she sees fit.</p>\n<p>You can achieve this by:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Isolating</strong> the focal point</li><li><strong>Contrast</strong></li><li>Put it in the most <strong>dominant</strong> position in the frame (middle)</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Angle</strong> the surrounding elements <strong>toward your focal point</strong></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/733ca569833c2b80269e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/733ca569833c2b80269e.webp\" width=\"879\" height=\"987\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 204\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4c38d8eae7ffff639639.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4c38d8eae7ffff639639.webp\" width=\"1049\" height=\"655\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 205\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The electric storm is the most dominant element in the scene.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0061c24dc1331e2522b0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0061c24dc1331e2522b0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 206\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The smaller buildings are arranged to lead your eye to the biggest one.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/44de62ab19539c9fb226.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/44de62ab19539c9fb226.webp\" width=\"1049\" height=\"655\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 207\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Red text contrasts with white walls.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d02cf2b723e618f3d60b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d02cf2b723e618f3d60b.webp\" width=\"1051\" height=\"656\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 208\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Color draws attention.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ac4809e49ac1443b75fa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ac4809e49ac1443b75fa.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"325\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 209\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Splinter Cell: Blacklist</strong></em><em> and </em><em><strong>Metal Gear Solid V: The Phantom Pain</strong></em><em> are stealth games, so you’re supposed to play under the radar and take enemy down in silence. Light sources are functional gameplay elements in those games because you can shoot them to sneak in easier. Therefore, they should be getting the most attention in the area.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/aa8e3a1a05a14d6c8b48.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/aa8e3a1a05a14d6c8b48.webp\" width=\"851\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 210\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Rhythm</strong></p>\n<p>Rhythm is the repetition of similar visual elements but no identical objects are formed.</p>\n<p>Rhythm is visually soothing, and rhythmic pattern can naturally graps people’s attention.</p>\n<p>In composition, rhythm is used to create depth, a sense of continuation/movement or place emphasis on an object (it doesn’t look the others).</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f8cd1923c6e66491fd2a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f8cd1923c6e66491fd2a.webp\" width=\"1056\" height=\"593\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 211\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/478337fb46d95222b200.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/478337fb46d95222b200.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"648\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 212\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Unity</strong></p>\n<p>Unity allows the brain to <strong>assimilate different elements</strong> found in a scene and <strong>interprets them as a single whole</strong>.</p>\n<p>The objects viewed usually have some <strong>affinity to each other</strong>; they could be of <strong>similar shape</strong>, for example, or even of <strong>similar color</strong>.</p>\n<p>This patterning gives the impression that the objects viewed all <strong>belong to the same big family</strong>.</p>\n<p>You can achieve unity through proximity. For instance, when you place your props, try small clusters or groups of them instead of randomly scatter individual objects around.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5012d5700e47e78ca4f0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5012d5700e47e78ca4f0.webp\" width=\"937\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 213\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Frame within frame</strong></p>\n<p>Frame within frame is a common photography technique used to provide more depth and context to the pictures. Depth because you have a clear foreground and background to compare; context because you can easily see the intention of the phographer through what is framed and what is not.</p>\n<p>The easiest way to create your frame is to use openings like doors, windows, or archways. This technique is particularly effective in survival-horror games where your character is alone. It gives an impression that you are being watched over by someone or something.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a2b566b585737294ff96.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a2b566b585737294ff96.webp\" width=\"892\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 214\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d4879c2990010f742947.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d4879c2990010f742947.webp\" width=\"871\" height=\"941\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 215\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Frame within frame is a common photography technique used to provide more depth and context to the pictures. Depth because you have a clear foreground and background to compare; context because you can easily see the intention of the phographer through what is framed and what is not.</p>\n<p>The easiest way to create your frame is to use openings like doors, windows, or archways. This technique is particularly effective in survival-horror games where your character is alone. It gives an impression that you are being watched over by someone or something.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dbed1d3e6a6ce03cf411.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dbed1d3e6a6ce03cf411.webp\" width=\"1055\" height=\"594\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 216\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Sometimes, framing can be done subtlely like this as long as the intention (to put something in the focus) remains true.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e978dc77569e7c274b91.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e978dc77569e7c274b91.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 217\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8ba142f047c20763ee5c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8ba142f047c20763ee5c.webp\" width=\"953\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 218\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Multiple examples from Breath of the Wild.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e03f5696ec6a51b60e78.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e03f5696ec6a51b60e78.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 219\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Titanfall 2</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/74ab00d1b700db77fe8e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/74ab00d1b700db77fe8e.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"324\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 220\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Rule of thirds</strong></p>\n<p>It’s fair to say that Level Artists have an easier time to compose than photographers since there are no environmental constraints (lighting, object placements, etc). So, they can easily adopt photography techniques to freely compose their “virtual pictures”.</p>\n<p>One of the most common techniques is the Rule of thirds. Basically you just need to divide your frame into 3 equal sections vertically and horizontally. When you place your objects at the intersections, they will grab more attention.</p>\n<p>You can also put another main object in an opposing intersection to build a more dynamic composition.</p>\n<p>This technique is particularly good to show the <strong>relationship between a character and his/her surroundings</strong>. The reason is it allows the frame to capture more of the environment while still maintaining a strong focus on the character.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cb828eff08817dea8aaa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cb828eff08817dea8aaa.webp\" width=\"890\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 221\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6e768687ebf13c75aad6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6e768687ebf13c75aad6.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 222\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f48eaef50ac1587ad61a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f48eaef50ac1587ad61a.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 223\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Shadow of the Colossus is the prime example of Rule of Thirds in video game. The camera will always adjust to place your character at one of these four intersections.</p>\n<p>Resident Evil 4 with its over-the-shoulder camera angle also applies Rule of Thirds: you and your enemies will always occupy two diagonal intersections.</p>\n<p>So, the bottom line is this technique requires some specific game design to work. Or it’ll just briefly shine in cutscenes or quick-time-events.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Leading lines</strong></p>\n<p>Another common photography technique to use is leading lines: Use the existing elements in the environment to draw one’s attention to the main subject or the focal point. Since we build our own environment, we’ll have freely use this without any pre-existing constraints like in real life.</p>\n<p>It’s believed the best way to use leading lines is to have them coming from the corner(s) of the picture. However, there’s always exception.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cb19cfdfebfdc48cd4da.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cb19cfdfebfdc48cd4da.webp\" width=\"1139\" height=\"689\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 224\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ae6c3ec5e7d3e2472391.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ae6c3ec5e7d3e2472391.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 225\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/02cec4e9863fdd3b986e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/02cec4e9863fdd3b986e.webp\" width=\"808\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 226\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Adding leading lines to your greybox could help the reading of the environment a lot.</p>\n<p>In this example, the player has to choose between left and right. In order to draw his attention toward the right, there is a yellow cable running to the right side and a couple of blue pipes suggesting the same direction.</p>\n<p>Besides, those orange cones kind of block the left path. With this, the level subtlety tells the player to go right without stopping him from freely exploring.</p>\n<p>Adding these early on in the development could help playtesting go a bit smoother.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>And sometimes, all you need is some inspiration. Find your references beyond video games because you don’t want to replicate works from other game designers.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0ebae3e04a98ad28de0b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0ebae3e04a98ad28de0b.webp\" width=\"669\" height=\"731\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 227\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/670a63e8ef61b162562d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/670a63e8ef61b162562d.webp\" width=\"640\" height=\"791\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 228\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Though the Level Artists cannot control the camera, they can control how players approach their environments. For example, these two movie shots direct your attention forward because they use symmetrical balance. Besides, the main subject is in the middle of the shots.</p>\n<p>The same can be achieved in games without taking the camera control from the player. In these two scenes, the environments also use symmetrical balance, and the objectives are also placed ahead.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d55daf493367d036b47f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d55daf493367d036b47f.webp\" width=\"636\" height=\"707\" style=\"--source-width:280px\" alt=\"Illustration 229\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/eb682d4d8881fedd528f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/eb682d4d8881fedd528f.webp\" width=\"785\" height=\"831\" style=\"--source-width:265px\" alt=\"Illustration 230\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h3>Chapter 06: Architecture Logic</h3>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>TABLE OF CONTENT</strong></p><p><strong>Chapter 06: Architecture Logic</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>This chapter contains:</p>\n<ul><li>Identify every material</li><li>Think about the weight</li><li>Define the main structure</li><li>Consider the possible shapes</li><li>Think about the weight</li><li>Realistic shapes vs textures</li><li>Relationship weight/volume</li><li>Relationship line/shape</li><li>Modular architecture</li><li>Modular architecture: believability &amp; time-saving</li><li>Modular architecture: different scales</li><li>Modular architecture: enhance quality</li><li>Pieces and building elements</li><li>Where it starts, and where it ends</li><li>PRO-TIP</li><li>Junction points</li><li>Don’t leave empty spaces without purpose</li><li>Depth and perspective</li><li>Flat geometry</li><li>Texture information</li><li>Details at all scales</li></ul></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>CONTENT</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>About props, architecture, levels and video games,…</p>\n<p>Twenty years ago, video games were all about <strong>imagination</strong>. As long as the player understood what each element was, he could enjoy the gameplay and imagine all the rest.</p>\n<p>Today, we want <strong>more</strong>. Now technology allows us to display plenty of details, players need <strong>realistic environments</strong>. They need to feel the world around them as if it was <strong>the real one</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>A realistic environment is more than some photographic textures mapped on hi-poly models</strong>. To feel realistic, the environment needs to <strong>fit the same rules than the real world, the same logic</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c9f9d9bd71867cd92d94.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c9f9d9bd71867cd92d94.webp\" width=\"673\" height=\"352\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 231\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b74c0e8849528d0523f9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b74c0e8849528d0523f9.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 232\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Unrealistic architecture in Assassin’s Creed: Unity</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d21dd2a09e1c32412ecc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d21dd2a09e1c32412ecc.webp\" width=\"805\" height=\"906\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 233\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>This is particularly true in Level Architecture, because people are used to seeing architecture around them all day long. Even if they don’t understand these rules, they are used to it. It’s part of their world.</p>\n<p><strong>If a level architecture is not logical, they won’t feel the game realistic.</strong> Or even worse: If they notice some illogical elements, it will totally <strong>break their immersion</strong>!! In the other hand, if the level architecture is perfectly logical, they won’t notice it specifically but they will experience a strong feeling of realism. Today, <strong>the most realistic games are those with the best Visual Logic</strong>.</p>\n<p>Note that we talk here about a “<strong>realistic feeling</strong>”, it applies also to cartoon-rendered games (Team Fortress2, Naruto, Prince of Persia, etc) or biased graphic games (Mirror’s Edge…). <strong>Visual Logic is much more important than render style.</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Identify every material</strong></p>\n<p>The first step is to <strong>identify every different material </strong>we will use in our architecture. This is important because each material has <strong>different properties</strong>, can take <strong>different shapes</strong>, and all <strong>cannot be used in a same way</strong>.</p>\n<p>In this example, we can already guess many Architectural Logic rules:</p>\n<ul><li>The <strong>heavy structure </strong>is made only of <strong>concrete bricks </strong>and <strong>wood beams</strong>, every other material is <strong>too weak</strong>.</li><li>The wood beams can be <strong>casted in the concrete ground</strong> or <strong>hold by the concrete tiles wall</strong>.</li><li>The <strong>plywood planks </strong>and <strong>corrugated iron tiles </strong>can only be <strong>fixed on wood beams</strong>.</li><li>The <strong>small windows </strong>can only be <strong>fixed by woods</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>With these few rules, we can already avoid many mistakes, like holding a <strong>concrete roof with corrugated iron tiles</strong> or <strong>putting some windows directly</strong> <strong>between concrete bricks without wooden frame</strong>.<a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7d193da820133eeaeb7c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7d193da820133eeaeb7c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"895\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 234\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3fb45269f6214bbb5099.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3fb45269f6214bbb5099.webp\" width=\"777\" height=\"857\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 235\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Think about the weight</strong></p>\n<p>Considering the <strong>weight</strong> of each building material is a good way to <strong>avoid mistakes </strong>and <strong>illogical architecture</strong>. No need to do any <strong>complicated calculations</strong>; just know which material is <strong>lighter</strong> or <strong>heavier </strong>than the other ones will be enough!</p>\n<p>Most of the time, <strong>heavy </strong>materials are used to build <strong>structures</strong> and <strong>lighter</strong> materials are <strong>attached </strong>to them.</p>\n<p>Here is a quick weight reminder of the most generic materials.</p>\n<p>Keep in mind that the weight can dramatically vary from one material to another (reinforced concrete can be really heavier than concrete bricks in example).</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Define the main structure</strong></p>\n<p>Once you know which material you can <strong>dispose of</strong> and their <strong>respective weights</strong>, you can <strong>define the main structure of your architecture</strong>: What is <strong>holding </strong>your building together? Which element is the <strong>strongest</strong>? Are the <strong>walls strong enough to hold everything </strong>or do you need some <strong>pillars to support the roof</strong>?</p>\n<p>Again, knowing the structure of your architecture will help you avoid many possible mistakes: Each element must be <strong>attached to the main structure </strong>or to a <strong>secondary structure attached to the main one</strong>. Always attach heavy elements (metal beam, concrete wall) on the main structure or your building will appear illogical and unrealistic!</p>\n<p>Considering the weight of each building material is a good way to avoid mistakes and illogical architecture. No need to do any complicated calculations; just know which material is lighter of heavier than the other ones will be enough!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9db8d62c208d03018568.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9db8d62c208d03018568.webp\" width=\"806\" height=\"999\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 236\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6cff2b7786b054d7ca0f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6cff2b7786b054d7ca0f.webp\" width=\"760\" height=\"897\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 237\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Consider the possible shapes</strong></p>\n<p>Each material behave differently. The final shape of a building strongly depend of the materials it’s made of.</p>\n<p>Here are a few examples from <em><strong>The Elder Scroll III: Morrowind</strong></em><em> (2002)</em>. As you can see, buildings made of <strong>cut stones</strong> tends to gives some <strong>straight lines</strong>, <strong>square angles </strong>and <strong>blocky volumes</strong>. You understood by now that a “<strong>stone building</strong>” is not only <strong>a</strong> <strong>texture of stone mapped on a building</strong>, it must follow the <strong>logic of stone</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>smallest element</strong> in this architecture is a <strong>stone cube</strong>, so the <strong>scale </strong>and <strong>shape </strong>of the whole building must be thought according to it!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>The next buildings are made of <strong>clay</strong> and <strong>coating</strong>, which allow some <strong>smoother</strong> and <strong>rounder shapes</strong>. There are <strong>no sharp edges</strong> or <strong>thin shapes</strong>, because the clay would become <strong>fragile</strong> and would <strong>break</strong>.</p>\n<p>Imagine if the artist had used a stone texture on this shapes, or a clay texture on the first one: This would have appeared <strong>totally illogical</strong>, no one would believe it. That’s why it’s important to <strong>consider the building materials </strong>you want to use and the <strong>possible shapes </strong>you can (or can’t) create with it.</p>\n<p>The last one is really particular: This mansion is totally made of <strong>roots.</strong> This is still architecture, since we have some <strong>rooms</strong>, <strong>gates</strong> and <strong>bridges</strong>, but this building is designed to feel <strong>natural instead of man-made</strong>. The <strong>same wood texture </strong>could have been used to create <strong>beams</strong> and <strong>planks</strong>, but it would totally change the <strong>feeling</strong> and “<strong>story</strong>” of this building!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ccbfdba3011430c835d4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ccbfdba3011430c835d4.webp\" width=\"732\" height=\"854\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 238\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Think about the weight</strong></p>\n<p>NEVER begin a shape in 3D if you don’t know which material you are about to create! If you begin to model something and think “I will decide the material later with the texture”, you can be sure you will make many mistakes and will need a lot of time to correct them! The shape must be carefully considered during every stage of your modelisation.</p>\n<p>ALWAYS ask yourself “Is it possible to get this shape with this material?” or “Did I already see this shape in real life?”</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/89e98bffc28529e2b7d9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/89e98bffc28529e2b7d9.webp\" width=\"694\" height=\"714\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 239\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/87d8095961499040fb54.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/87d8095961499040fb54.webp\" width=\"712\" height=\"809\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 240\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Realistic shapes vs textures</strong></p>\n<p>Many people think that <strong>realistic textures </strong>are the key to create some <strong>realistic environment</strong>.</p>\n<p><em><strong>Mirror’s Edge</strong></em><em> (2008)</em> is the perfect example to understand that it’s WRONG.</p>\n<p>This game has a really <strong>biased graphic style</strong>: almost <strong>no texture information</strong>, just some <strong>clean</strong>, <strong>streamlined shapes </strong>and <strong>bright colors</strong>.</p>\n<p>But most of all, every environment has a <strong>perfect Architectural Logic</strong>! Even without detailed textures, <strong>each shape </strong>is so <strong>precise</strong> and <strong>accurate</strong> that there is no doubt for the player. He can instantly recognize everything and “feel” it like the real world.</p>\n<p>When you look at those two screenshots, you don’t need to wonder what that <strong>white</strong>, <strong>green</strong> or <strong>black element </strong>is: you “know” it’s a <strong>metal stair </strong>or a <strong>plaster wall </strong>because <strong>its shape is so correct it couldn’t be something else</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Relationship weight/volume</strong></p>\n<p>Now you’ve considered the weight of your building materials and their possible shapes, but don’t forget the <strong>relationship between those two aspects</strong>.</p>\n<p>The <strong>bigger </strong>your shape is, the <strong>heavier </strong>it will become! It can be a problem with <strong>heavy material </strong>such as <strong>concrete </strong>and <strong>metals</strong>. There are two major problems we can encounter with large/heavy shapes:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>The toughness of your material</strong>: a piece of concrete can break just because of its weight if it’s not reinforced by steel. Metal usually <strong>can’t break easily</strong>, but it would <strong>bend</strong> if the shape is <strong>too long</strong>!</li></ol>\n<p><strong>The convenience of building: </strong>some materials can be built on site (like concrete) but some must be <strong>manufactured in factories </strong>and then <strong>carried by truck</strong>. That’s why you will <strong>never see a single piece of metal longer than 20m</strong></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5317339949c49d5bfcba.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5317339949c49d5bfcba.webp\" width=\"735\" height=\"641\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 241\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/847f881c2f1ac6961085.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/847f881c2f1ac6961085.webp\" width=\"855\" height=\"768\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 242\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Relationship line/shape</strong></p>\n<p>Our eyes can instantly recognize a shape by its <strong>silhouette</strong> but it may take a while it the silhouette is not clearly visible. That’s why we should use lines to make this shape <strong>more evident</strong>, <strong>more visible</strong>. When there’s a <strong>break</strong> in a shape, there should be some <strong>lines </strong>to indicate it.</p>\n<p>This is particularly true with <strong>slopes</strong>: When the ground is <strong>not flat</strong>, we don’t notice it easily if there’s <strong>no lines </strong>to give the eye a hint about the <strong>angle change</strong>. On this picture, you can notice a <strong>double line </strong>when the <strong>slope begins</strong>.  Can you imagine the ground without this line? Would you guess the shape at first sight?</p>\n<p>This is also true inside a <strong>shape</strong>. Here we have a double problem. The shape feels strange <strong>without vertical line </strong>and the material shape is not <strong>realistic.</strong> This metal wall is made of <strong>big squared tiles</strong>, there’s no way those tiles would have been <strong>bended </strong>like this. By adding a <strong>vertical line</strong>, we both <strong>restore the shape and the architectural logic</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/907560a7b087015cfb35.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/907560a7b087015cfb35.webp\" width=\"853\" height=\"840\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 243\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Modular architecture</strong></p>\n<p>A “module” is <strong>a piece </strong>of building or environment that can be organized with others in order to build something. It can be some piece of walls, doors and windows you assemble together to build a house, a set of props you use to compose a scene, etc. We call “modular” everything that is not built out of one single piece.</p>\n<p>There’s many advantage to think your architecture with modules instead of trying to build it in one block: you will <strong>save a lot of time</strong>, be able to get a <strong>better texture ratio </strong>without exploding your memory budget, and often get a much <strong>more realistic </strong>building than if you build it in one piece!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Modular architecture: believability &amp; time-saving</strong></p>\n<p>If you look around you, you will notice that every construction is made of <strong>a few patterns</strong>, <strong>repeated many times</strong>. This is always true, whatever the construction is. Even with the most complicated buildings.</p>\n<p>If you need to create <em><strong>Notre Dame de Paris</strong></em> (on the picture) you could think first it would take some weeks, because there are so many details to do! But if you take a closer look, you will notice <strong>nothing is unique</strong>! Almost every detail belongs to a module that can be repeated many times!</p>\n<p>The best solution would be to take some time to carefully look at this picture and list every module you need. In fact, this magnificent cathedral is only made of around <strong>10 different modules</strong>!</p>\n<p>Once you know it, you can build each module separately (ensure it fits together though), then assemble them to build a <strong>fast</strong> and <strong>realistic</strong> cathedral!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3f5f29388544b1325053.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3f5f29388544b1325053.webp\" width=\"657\" height=\"685\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 244\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Modular architecture: different scales</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>sizes</strong> of modules you will build depend on your <strong>game engine</strong>.</p>\n<p>If your game engine allows massive specific objects, like full interiors or full buildings, then you are totally free to use whatever size you desire and assemble everything in one big object in Maya or Max.</p>\n<p>But if your game engine is an <strong>instance-based engine </strong>(it’s the case of most recent engines, like <strong>Unreal3</strong> or <strong>CryEngine3</strong>), then you should think your architecture modules as <strong>smaller</strong>, <strong>independent props</strong> and assemble it directly in the engine.</p>\n<p>Although the production pipeline is quite different, the main idea stays the same<strong>: identify the different modules, realize them in 3D and assemble them together to build your architecture</strong>.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9d7f79cd144e4ba1480f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9d7f79cd144e4ba1480f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"363\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 245\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>An instance-based Architecture: Can you identify the different modules? Does it seem realistic?</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/52de5f70c613eac52c59.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/52de5f70c613eac52c59.webp\" width=\"755\" height=\"396\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 246\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This method works even better if you add some geometry to offset some tiles or break some corners, to give the whole mesh a story (people used to walk here, this tile has been moved, a heavy object felt on this one and broke it, etc).</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Modular architecture: enhance quality</strong></p>\n<p>Building your architecture from modules is a good opportunity to enhance your texture ratio and create variations without any extra cost in performances.</p>\n<p>Tiles are a good example: Instead of trying to create a large texture including many tiles to prevent the player to notice the <em>“tiling effect”, </em>you can just build one or two single tiles with full texture resolution and then duplicate it and rotate it to add variations!</p>\n<p>Although the production pipeline is quite different, the main idea stays the same<strong>: identify the different modules, realize them in 3D and assemble them together to build your architecture</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/79533ad0deabd0bfa4a8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/79533ad0deabd0bfa4a8.webp\" width=\"755\" height=\"438\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 247\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>In this example, from two shapes of tiles, a whole living pattern has been made. Can you imagine how this floor would have been empty with a single tiling texture without geometry and variations?</em></p></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/aeeb6b27abfc0ff10721.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/aeeb6b27abfc0ff10721.webp\" width=\"1034\" height=\"688\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 248\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2fe84533b095db0edf9c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2fe84533b095db0edf9c.webp\" width=\"1034\" height=\"736\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 249\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Pieces and building elements</strong></p>\n<p>What we call “modules” only belong to the world of Video Games. Real architects don’t build their buildings out of modules, but with <strong>pieces of building materials</strong>. Fortunately, you don’t need to carry thousands of concrete tiles and steel beams to build your level, but you need to understand how those pieces are <strong>made</strong>, <strong>organized </strong>and <strong>attached </strong>together in order to create a <strong>realistic architecture</strong>!</p>\n<p>In most case, you don’t need any technical knowledge: just carefully observe the real world and pay attention to how different materials are used.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Where it starts, and where it ends</strong></p>\n<p>Every piece has a defined size: it starts and ends somewhere. Most of the time, <strong>it won’t be cut except if there is no other solution!</strong> It’s ugly, less solid, and represents extra work during the construction.</p>\n<p>Usually, every dimension is carefully thought so builders won’t need to cut pieces. Think about it when you will create it in 3D!</p>\n<p>This rule applies with every piece created in a factory like tiles, bricks, crafted</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/66ccae3cc41456c79409.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/66ccae3cc41456c79409.webp\" width=\"828\" height=\"548\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 250\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>PRO-TIP</strong></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5fefd1f40f77e576965f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5fefd1f40f77e576965f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"239\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 251\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Set the tiling carefully to avoid cutting something that can’t be cut.</em></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f831744a2bbb34d0e6fa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f831744a2bbb34d0e6fa.webp\" width=\"1012\" height=\"570\" style=\"--source-width:133px\" alt=\"Illustration 252\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/31f9ebb6d2cfe14448f9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/31f9ebb6d2cfe14448f9.webp\" width=\"774\" height=\"851\" style=\"--source-width:133px\" alt=\"Illustration 253\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><em>If you are forced to cut some pieces or change your material suddenly, don’t leave a sharp cut like this! This is highly unrealistic (you will never meet this case in real life) and will make your level look old gen!</em></p></td><td><p><em>If you are forced to cut your pieces or change the material suddenly, just hide the transition with a piece of geometry. You will get a more polished and realistic level (tiles to metal transition on the left, bricks to concrete and metal on right).</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Junction points</strong></p>\n<p>One of the most commonly forgotten aspects of Architecture in video games is how things are attached together.  It may seem not so important, but in fact it adds a lot in terms of realism! You can do it either with geometry or textures.</p>\n<p>One of the most commonly forgotten aspects of Architecture in video games is how things are attached together.  It may seem not so important, but in fact it adds a lot in terms of realism! You can do it either with geometry or textures.</p>\n<p>Think about the propriety of your materials and ask yourself <strong>“how can this piece be firmly attached to the rest of my level?”</strong></p>\n<p>Small pieces of metal can be welded; heavy pieces of metal must be nailed together or drawn in fresh concrete, wood can be nailed, screwed or nested, glass can’t be pierced but can be fixed between plastic pieces, etc…</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e9e4a33f91d5919b71fb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e9e4a33f91d5919b71fb.webp\" width=\"770\" height=\"806\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 254\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/314a98f09786ecfd80e5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/314a98f09786ecfd80e5.webp\" width=\"673\" height=\"697\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 255\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Tiling dramatically visible from a distance (could break with beams, panels, etc)</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Don’t leave empty spaces without purpose</strong></p>\n<p>A common mistake of Level Architects is to keep some wide surfaces empty. By “empty” I mean using just a tiling texture on a large surface without adding <strong>extra details</strong>, <strong>geometry</strong>, <strong>decals</strong>, or whatever. Despite empty spaces are common in real life, it never looks good in a video game environment for multiple reasons.</p>\n<p>The first one is purely visual: as we seen in this lesson, to achieve a sense of realism we need to “<strong>give the eyes a million details to look at</strong>”.<em> </em>If a surface lacks details, the player will immediately “experience” it as a videogame environment and it can break the immersion.</p>\n<p>The second reason is technical: a surface that lacks details will result in a very visible tiling when viewed from a distance! There is tons of way to add details or break the tiling of a texture: we can add geometry (volumes, offsets, structure element, etc), cut the surface into pieces and recompose it in a different order, add decals, etc.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Depth and perspective</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Opportunity for shadows</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Depth and perspective </strong>is a crucial concept to master. It gives to any building a very <strong>realistic feeling</strong>. Depth is achieved by using <strong>different level of geometry</strong> in order to obtain <strong>an effect of parallax </strong>when viewed by a moving camera (i.e. the player!)</p>\n<p>In Architecture, the ceiling is a great opportunity to add some depth and perspective, since <strong>its collision is not really important for gameplay</strong>!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/00a0e77f533d160ca117.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/00a0e77f533d160ca117.webp\" width=\"714\" height=\"454\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 256\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Here is one bad example of flat and empty ceiling, like many beginners use to create without paying attention.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>The 3 other pictures are good examples: by adding geometry different level of geometry in their ceiling, they added a lot of depth and created many good opportunities for shadows.</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4e71cf6665b6523d9f5d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4e71cf6665b6523d9f5d.webp\" width=\"481\" height=\"234\" style=\"--source-width:84px\" alt=\"Illustration 257\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>In the first one, the geometry is so deep that the ceiling blend in shadows, resulting a sensation of mystery as the player can’t see the real ceiling.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fa68f95df6e203350fc0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fa68f95df6e203350fc0.webp\" width=\"481\" height=\"249\" style=\"--source-width:84px\" alt=\"Illustration 258\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The second picture feature a ugly ceiling texture with a very visible tiling effect, but since they added a lot of details with beams and structure, our eyes are not paying attention to the tiling texture behind!</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9fff31201823a738acdf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9fff31201823a738acdf.webp\" width=\"679\" height=\"189\" style=\"--source-width:84px\" alt=\"Illustration 259\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The third example is using two very simple and small textures (32*32px). However, their different beams sizes and orientations create many values of shadows and highlight. The final result is quite realistic, clean and complex, despite the low resolution textures used to create it!</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d97e4a3c14e5bb8ca0dd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d97e4a3c14e5bb8ca0dd.webp\" width=\"820\" height=\"783\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 260\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Flat geometry</strong></p>\n<p>Sometimes it can be difficult to add depth and geometry, especially when you need to keep a flat surface, for a navigable ground in example. However, with a bit of imagination you can use various materials in your ground and obtain a more interesting result even while keeping a pretty flat ground!</p>\n<p>In industrial or futuristic architecture, grates are perfect to add a lot of depth and perspective without changing the ground level: You can open large areas under the map and add whatever you want, depending of the level’s theme.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Texture information</strong></p>\n<p>Besides geometry, you can add a lot of details by using more information in your textures. Texture information is every detail that explains the story of the surface: marks, stains, destruction, repairs, modification, etc. You can learn more about texture information in the Visual Storytelling in Architecture lesson.</p>\n<p>Here are two examples about how it works in-game. Both scenes come from AAA games released in 2007):</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c2c8d5b79fd21001a5dd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c2c8d5b79fd21001a5dd.webp\" width=\"578\" height=\"224\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 261\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This texture is full of information: stains, leaks, dirt, bevels, repair marks, etc. A piece of architecture build with a lot of texture information will tends to be full of details to catch the eye of the player, so he will pay less attention to the low polygon modeling.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a57565f9529124ea8a1b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a57565f9529124ea8a1b.webp\" width=\"577\" height=\"205\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 262\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>In the other hand, this texture doesn’t display much information. The artist probably chose this one to avoid any visible tiling. However, without texture information to bring details, this geometry feels totally empty and unrealistic! It feels much more like a videogame than a real piece of architecture, even if there’s much more geometry than the previous picture (plinths, door frame, etc)!!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e37ad2b017b7b9648d15.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e37ad2b017b7b9648d15.webp\" width=\"595\" height=\"473\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 263\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Details at all scales</strong></p>\n<p>We hear often about the need of “Big, Medium, Small details”. This is especially true with textures information! If you want to prevent a texture to feel too empty or too tiled, you must include details at all scales. This texture here is a good example.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>The ground of this bridge from Crysis 2 is a good example of “details at all scales”.</p>\n<p>From a <strong>close distance</strong>, this texture <strong>remain crisp</strong> because of the <strong>thin grain </strong>of the concrete.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f900a4416e9b9159fb88.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f900a4416e9b9159fb88.webp\" width=\"587\" height=\"184\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 264\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>From a <strong>medium distance</strong>, the texture is pretty <strong>realistic in term of architecture</strong>: it’s a bare concrete ground, some construction lines are visible, and some holes are present. This <strong>surface tells its own story</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c7ec03cb151499a16df0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c7ec03cb151499a16df0.webp\" width=\"587\" height=\"188\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 265\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>From a <strong>long distance</strong>, the texture tiling is not visible because the artist added some water puddles in <strong>order to break the tiling effect</strong>. The player’s eyes will focus on the high contrast details.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/abe46f919d9295c27dee.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/abe46f919d9295c27dee.webp\" width=\"584\" height=\"195\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 266\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/302623f19e5b9b06e8de.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/302623f19e5b9b06e8de.webp\" width=\"822\" height=\"448\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 267\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Even without detailed textures, everything is logic and realistic: tile placements, door junctions, door frame, handles…</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/07adebf8164e720dcc6a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/07adebf8164e720dcc6a.webp\" width=\"817\" height=\"368\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 268\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>It’s interesting to notice they didn’t use additional textures for their ceiling, just use geometry to break the tiling and add some opportunities for shadows. If the ceiling would have been totally flat, it would have felt strange and empty.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b1928caa85ce326c7084.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b1928caa85ce326c7084.webp\" width=\"543\" height=\"256\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 269\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Look at the junction between the black chains and the beams. Does it feel strange?  Nothing connect the chains and the metal beams! It’s just floating, resulting a pretty unrealistic look.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d80f6b2842287cb11220.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d80f6b2842287cb11220.webp\" width=\"679\" height=\"408\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 270\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>This scene from the same level is really better: some hooks attach the wires to the beams, different texture to connect the rock wall to the dust ground. Everything is connected logically!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9192ebcc456de57d9374.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9192ebcc456de57d9374.webp\" width=\"1045\" height=\"439\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 271\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f1e712bb2c864d50b77a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f1e712bb2c864d50b77a.webp\" width=\"1006\" height=\"527\" style=\"--source-width:268px\" alt=\"Illustration 272\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>By adding some geometry in this two pictures, the artist broke the tiling of these ceramic tiles and created some more interesting visuals!</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1cc12fddd23abfa733f6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1cc12fddd23abfa733f6.webp\" width=\"968\" height=\"532\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 273\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Everything about this bridge makes sense.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>This area had many different problems: First, the beams supporting the catwalk were visually too thin compared to the catwalk itself. It felt fragile and looked like it can break if we walk on the bridge. The position wasn’t good either: a support must be fixed on something solid, not on a grate!!</p>\n<p>The railings were made of very thin pieces of metal: this could be realistic, but cause a typical problem in video games: it generates a lot of aliasing when viewed from a distance! The metal elements should be thicker then.</p>\n<p>Finally, the path was opened on the right. Think about the people who work here! It’s dangerous; they can fall if it stays open! That’s why it should be closed to feel more realistic.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/58083e17cb067cfaf9bd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/58083e17cb067cfaf9bd.webp\" width=\"820\" height=\"871\" style=\"--source-width:277px\" alt=\"Illustration 274\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h3>Chapter 07: Reality Check</h3>\n\n<p><strong> </strong><strong>scroll-bookmark-147</strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>TABLE OF CONTENT</strong></p><p><strong>Chapter 07: Reality Check</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>This chapter contains:</p>\n<ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Less is more</li><li>A million details to look at</li><li>A million details to look at</li><li>Impossible structure</li><li>Strange natural formation</li><li>Linear repetition</li><li>Detail exaggeration</li><li>Illusion of symmetry</li><li>Movement</li><li>Stereotype</li><li>Exaggerate, but avoid doing “too much”</li></ul></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>CONTENT</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>“<em>Because Reality does NOT always feel realistic in a video game!”</em></p>\n<p>We always talk about “realism” in video game environments, but we can’t achieve it by “copying reality”.</p>\n<p>Our goal is to achieve a <strong>“feeling of reality”</strong>. We must identify clearly what makes an environment feel realistic, and that’s primarily the purpose of this lesson. Through this document, we will see different aspect of reality, define if it feels realistic or not, and give some advice to improve the realism of your scene.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2f7226d3cb26aed21f51.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2f7226d3cb26aed21f51.webp\" width=\"760\" height=\"866\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 278\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/451c12efad16073e0f75.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/451c12efad16073e0f75.webp\" width=\"930\" height=\"572\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 279\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Barcelona Pavilion: Do you think this kind of architecture would feel realistic in a video game?</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a2c6fa5b3bf15dc54062.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a2c6fa5b3bf15dc54062.webp\" width=\"930\" height=\"616\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 280\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Less is more</strong></p>\n<p><strong>&gt;&gt; “Less is more” , or why Mies Van Der Rohe wouldn’t create video game environment.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Mies van der Rohe</strong>, one of the four most famous modern architects in the world in the mid-20th century. Mies insisted on the architectural philosophy of “<strong>less is more</strong>” and advocated a new concept of flow space in handling methods. The details of his designs are <strong>reduced to an absolute state </strong>that cannot be streamlined. Many works are <strong>almost</strong> <strong>completely exposed</strong>, but they are <strong>noble</strong> and <strong>elegant</strong>, and have made the structure itself sublimate into architectural art.</p>\n<p>Since the last century, architects of the real world are fond of the “less is more” trend. It’s a major example of what doesn’t work at all in the videogame world!</p>\n<p>The less details you put in your environment, the more our eyes will focus on what they see. Player’s brain will notice “this is not reality, this is a 3D mesh” and it would break the immersion.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>A million details to look at</strong></p>\n<p><strong>&gt;&gt; « Give the eye a million details to look at »</strong></p>\n</td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1b4b807c800ac979df48.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1b4b807c800ac979df48.webp\" width=\"900\" height=\"441\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 281\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Not enough “information” in textures, not enough props, a homogenous light. Even if this room could exist in real-life, it doesn’t feel realistic in a game.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4dc38efed17b6d9f9dd4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4dc38efed17b6d9f9dd4.webp\" width=\"902\" height=\"437\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 282\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>With a lot of detail, and a strong contrasted light, this place feels much more realistic. It’s because our eyes have many details to look at so it won’t spend too much time to analyze each element. (As long as you keep “unity”. See Visual Composition course for more detail)</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/111e92135d6756166c09.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/111e92135d6756166c09.webp\" width=\"966\" height=\"507\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 283\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Left: a carrier corridor in real life. Right: a carrier corridor in Crysis (2007). Notice how the artist opened the walls in order to add thousand of details that catch the light, cast shadows and create contrast.</em><em><br></em><em> At the end, this corridor feels realistic to the player, even if it’s really different from reality!!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fe7bc6bb94614f0b984e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fe7bc6bb94614f0b984e.webp\" width=\"964\" height=\"445\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 284\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Real life: an airport runway</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b34bb8988b1b21641822.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b34bb8988b1b21641822.webp\" width=\"966\" height=\"396\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 285\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>COD7: Black Ops (2010): this airport runway looks like the reference picture, but doesn’t feel realistic in-game because it feels too empty!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e5dbbd6318bd594577db.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e5dbbd6318bd594577db.webp\" width=\"798\" height=\"997\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 286\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>A million details to look at</strong></p>\n<p>A common way to improve the level of detail of a level, along with the feeling of perspective and pacing, is to create some openings and fill them with props and objects. This is one of the major differences between 20<sup>th</sup> and 21<sup>st</sup> century games, as you can notice in these screenshots from <em>Half-Life (1998)</em> and <em>Unreal 2: The Awakening (2002).</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Impossible structure</strong></p>\n<p>Famous architects like to create <strong>unusual shapes </strong>and <strong>impossible structures</strong>. In real life, you immediately understand this structure is <strong>possible</strong>, because you <strong>can see it exists</strong>.</p>\n<p>In a video game level, you must convince the player that your world is <strong>believable </strong>by yourself! So you should <strong>avoid strange architecture</strong>, as long as the <strong>Level Brief doesn’t ask for it specifically</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c763044edc2deb2deb88.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c763044edc2deb2deb88.webp\" width=\"888\" height=\"798\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 287\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Bibliothèque Nationale François Mitterrand (Paris): What is the problem of these stairs railings on the left?</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/476268d77a5a23d5969e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/476268d77a5a23d5969e.webp\" width=\"894\" height=\"666\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 288\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Strange natural formation</strong></p>\n<p>Same thing than the architectural issue, try to keep some <strong>mainstream references </strong>and avoid copying special cases.</p>\n<p>This tropical island looks like it had been made by a beginner with a height map editor: vertical mountains with round heads, same forest density everywhere… Although it’s real, it wouldn’t feel realistic at all in a game!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Linear repetition</strong></p>\n<p>Man-made elements are full of repetition. You will often need to add some variation to enhance their realism.</p>\n<p>Rooftops silhouettes in London: Notice how the perfectly aligned repetition doesn’t feel realistic. To use this formation in-game, we should add some variation in their shapes and placement</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/46f2b694602fbad52cfe.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/46f2b694602fbad52cfe.webp\" width=\"838\" height=\"456\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 289\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0a5042dc1d03d2758f92.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0a5042dc1d03d2758f92.webp\" width=\"838\" height=\"457\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 290\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Detail exaggeration</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Small details </strong>are often <strong>exaggerated</strong> in order to make them <strong>visible</strong>.</p>\n<p>Look at the size of these cables, at least 5cm diameter! But if there were drawn in real size, we wouldn’t see it at all. Worst, it would cause some horrible aliasing problems!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Illusion of symmetry</strong></p>\n<p>Since we (humans) always tried to achieve perfection in art, architecture quickly went toward symmetry. But in real life, nothing is really symmetrical! A real symmetrical environment seems too perfect and unrealistic.</p>\n<p>The scene above is <strong>perfectly symmetrical </strong>and seems <strong>totally</strong> <strong>unrealistic</strong>. The real scene is below: <strong>not totally symmetrical</strong> but much <strong>more believable</strong>.</p>\n<p>However, it’s possible to <strong>start from a symmetrical environment</strong> and then <strong>break this symmetry </strong>by populating with an <strong>unsymmetrical props </strong>and <strong>decal compositions</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d0ef56dcfa01b951226d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d0ef56dcfa01b951226d.webp\" width=\"775\" height=\"731\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 291\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6be81b80c86f96ef62d6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6be81b80c86f96ef62d6.webp\" width=\"782\" height=\"358\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 292\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Another good trick is to offset the entrance and exit of the room to avoid discovering the place from the symmetry axis.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Movement</strong></p>\n<p>Movement adds a lot to the <strong>realistic</strong> feeling of your level. Especially in exteriors environment, everything is moving in the real world. Even without requiring specific animations or characters, there’s a lot a tricks you can use to bring some movement in your level, like animated UVs to create <strong>wind effects</strong> in vegetation or fabrics.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f4de44e5ef7374e3dc17.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f4de44e5ef7374e3dc17.webp\" width=\"969\" height=\"541\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 293\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e9be2de6329fc36804b8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e9be2de6329fc36804b8.webp\" width=\"866\" height=\"397\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 294\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Grass and dust flying around along with the helicopters, people walking or running all around the place: movement everywhere makes a scene more realistic.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3b29e121bf82aada4502.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3b29e121bf82aada4502.webp\" width=\"870\" height=\"877\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 295\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Stereotype </strong></p>\n<p>When you’ve never experienced something, you only know the stereotype. Some ideas are so strong in people’s mind they won’t recognize something as “true” if you don’t give them some pieces of what they expect. Try to learn how your targeted audience imagines things, so you will know what they expect to see in your level.</p>\n<p>The real Shanghai… and the Shanghai as it’s seen by most people ! Players need to see these traditional roofs, red columns everywhere, and lot of Chinese texts to feel like they’re in Shanghai. This vision of Shanghai match with the one they already had in their mind, so they can immediately identify the place.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Same thing happen with objects and props. If a player always uses an object in a certain way, he will expect other objects of the same kind to behave in the same way. (i.e. red barrel explodes, fire hurts, ice slips, wood breaks, etc).</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/eb494406c94602a26932.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/eb494406c94602a26932.webp\" width=\"722\" height=\"221\" style=\"--source-width:125px\" alt=\"Illustration 296\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Red Barrels are known to be explosive in almost every shooting game. In this scene, everything would obviously explode if the player shoots into one of them.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ddaff10c2d1b97975044.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ddaff10c2d1b97975044.webp\" width=\"978\" height=\"363\" style=\"--source-width:125px\" alt=\"Illustration 297\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>But barrels and tanks can easily be interpreted as something explosive! When you use one of these shape, be careful to avoid using symbols or text that remind something explosive, or players will be frustrated if they can’t burst them into flames.</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Exaggerate, but avoid doing “too much”</strong></p>\n<p>How these boats will manage to get out of here now??</p>\n<p>How these helicopters can fly so close to each other without colliding??<br>What did the developers had in mind when realizing this final scene for COD7: Black ops?</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1fddc6e2babbf54b659a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1fddc6e2babbf54b659a.webp\" width=\"873\" height=\"425\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 298\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h2>Topic 03: Level Beyond Environment</h2>\n\n<p>scroll-bookmark-165  </p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>TOPIC INTRODUCTION</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Level Beyond Environment</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>Realism is important, but playability and meaning are even more so. Levels are, first and foremost, playgrounds for people to express themselves in the game's fantasy; the game's fantasy also needs to present engaging stories for them to maintain interest. Thus, a great level is one that can seamlessly guide and immerse players.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9230163364d8948fc6d3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9230163364d8948fc6d3.webp\" width=\"836\" height=\"421\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 302\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e8b32e7f3e4213cf6d46.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e8b32e7f3e4213cf6d46.webp\" width=\"684\" height=\"774\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 303\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Chapter 08: Guiding Players with Environment</strong></p>\n<p>There are several ways to guide and drive the player through your level, and assure he will naturally go exactly where you want him to go.</p>\n<p>Nowadays, with growing concurrence and the problem of gamer “casualisation”, it’s more and more important to keep the player from getting lost. A well driven player will feel more free while playing your level, and less restrained by the level’s limits. This will crucially enhance the gaming experience of your level.</p>\n<p>As Level Artist and Level Designer, the readability of your level is the responsibility of both of you.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Chapter 09: Visual Logic &amp; Visual Storytelling</strong></p>\n<p>Among Level Artist’s tools, <strong>Visual Logic </strong>and <strong>Visual Storytelling </strong>are two of the most <strong>powerful</strong> and <strong>complex </strong>to use. Their mastery is one of the key elements that distinguish a great Level Art from a poor one. In this lesson, we will learn how to ensure a correct Visual Logic to get <strong>a believable world</strong>, and then we’ll cover how to achieve some great Visual Storytelling in order to make your levels <strong>unique </strong>and <strong>unforgettable</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5fd43d09b324eb082914.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5fd43d09b324eb082914.webp\" width=\"487\" height=\"563\" style=\"--source-width:267px\" alt=\"Illustration 304\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/90b943cb683f7fcee555.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/90b943cb683f7fcee555.webp\" width=\"525\" height=\"296\" style=\"--source-width:278px\" alt=\"Illustration 305\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></strong><strong><br></strong></p></td><td><p><strong>Chapter 10: Level Optimization Guideline</strong></p>\n<p>No one likes spending some time to optimize the level. It’s a fact. (I even suspect technical artists to dislike this part too… Am I right?) <strong>The problem is: no one likes playing a level with a bad framerate</strong>, huge loading time, wrong textures LOD, etc. So someone will need to do it, right? And for your level, dear Level Artist, this person is you.</p>\n<p><strong>Optimization is easy!</strong> Art is difficult, because it requires sensitivity, judgment; it’s subjective so maybe it won’t please everyone. But optimization is easy: Just follow the rules! Just go through your checklist <strong>frequently</strong> and ensure everything is stays within the limits, and everything’s gonna be alright…</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h3>Chapter 08: Guiding Players with Environment</h3>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>TABLE OF CONTENT</strong></p><p><strong>Chapter 08: Guiding Players with Environment</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>This chapter contains:</p>\n<ul><li>How to attract players’ attention</li><li>Contrast</li><li>Composition &amp; Visual storytelling</li><li>Weenie</li><li>Motion</li><li>Cutscene</li><li>Character</li><li>Pickup</li><li>Help the player identify different areas</li><li>Help player identify different areas: Landmark</li><li>Help player identify different areas: Style</li><li>Guide player to his own way</li><li>Guide player to his own way: Sign</li><li>Guide player to his own way: Line</li><li>Wrap up for this chapter</li><li>Attract methods</li><li>Identify methods</li><li>Guide method</li></ul></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>CONTENT</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>There are several ways to guide and drive the player through your level, and assure he will <strong>naturally </strong>go exactly where you want him to go.</p>\n<p>Nowadays, with growing concurrence and the problem of gamer “<strong>casualisation</strong>”, it’s more and more important to keep the player from getting lost. A well driven player will feel <strong>more free </strong>while playing your level, and <strong>less restrained </strong>by the level’s limits. This will crucially enhance the gaming experience of your level.</p>\n<p>As Level Artist and Level Designer, the readability of your level is the responsibility of both of you.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e8b32e7f3e4213cf6d46.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e8b32e7f3e4213cf6d46.webp\" width=\"684\" height=\"774\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 306\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a6c160d4f3ee552d738a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a6c160d4f3ee552d738a.webp\" width=\"789\" height=\"868\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 307\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Here is the outline of this guide:</p>\n<ul><li>How to attract players’ <strong>attention</strong></li><li>How to help the player <strong>identify</strong> your <strong>different areas</strong></li><li>How to give the player some guides so he can <strong>decide his own way</strong></li><li>Conclusion and comparisons of different tools</li></ul>\n<p>These are immersed navigation tools, so it’s part of the game environment. Unlike discrete tools which are general interfaces used throughout the game, like maps or compass, immersed tools have specific applications in the individual environments. These tools also <strong>don't stand out as much </strong>as discrete ones and generally <strong>aren't perceived as a part of the interface</strong>.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>This is because they are built out of the components (<strong>geometry</strong>, <strong>textures</strong>, <strong>lights</strong>, <strong>characters</strong>...) that are also used to build the environment itself, which makes them the responsibility of the Level Artist. It also means that most of them <strong>won't be consciously used </strong>by the player. Instead, they serve as <strong>subtle instruments </strong>by which to guide the player but with this unobtrusiveness comes the danger that some players may just <strong>overlook</strong> them.</p>\n<p>The fact that they're an immersed part of the level also makes them a lot harder to classify. This is why I'm grouping them based on their purpose. I'll explain each purpose and then give a few examples on how to achieve the desired effect with different components.</p>\n</td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c7c744eb4eb74fe1f0ee.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c7c744eb4eb74fe1f0ee.webp\" width=\"743\" height=\"813\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 308\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/21da29feaf60e775d525.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/21da29feaf60e775d525.webp\" width=\"716\" height=\"783\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 309\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>How to attract players’ attention</strong></p>\n<p>When using this tool, the aim is to attract the player's attention to a certain location. This is often used in <strong>linear</strong> level designs with complicated rooms to make it clear where the player has to move in order to continue. It can also be used in <strong>less linear </strong>levels <strong>to highlight important areas of interaction</strong> -- such as places that the player will revisit often.</p>\n<p>Essentially, the idea behind this is to make this target area look more important and thus interesting to the player, so that he moves closer. This can be done quite subtly, so that the player is unconsciously pulled towards where you want him to be. Of course it can also be done using more blatant means which will most likely cause the player to respond more consciously. As you can imagine, there's quite a number of ways to go about this, so I'll try and list a couple examples below.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Contrast</strong></p>\n<p>If we want to attract the player, we first want to catch his eye. Here, painting techniques help us a lot. After all, painting has been manipulating and steering the human eye for millennia. What's true for paintings is true for levels: Visual contrast is a good method to catch a player's eye. The higher the contrast, the more likely the area is to be noticed. For example, light coming into a dark room from a doorway will attract the player's eye and that makes him a lot more likely to approach the doorway.</p>\n<p>In general, light works really well as a method to highlight areas and paths. There's a reason that a lot of theme park rides use light to mark their exits: So the visitors instinctively head toward the light and thus avoid congestion. Believe me, it works; while working on a solo campaign level I had to run through a level to get to a certain point in the game to test something. To get there I had to go past a T-section in a vent shaft area.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b88e6a10ec2f00fd0068.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b88e6a10ec2f00fd0068.webp\" width=\"873\" height=\"948\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 310\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8fa50843801166ced1c6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8fa50843801166ced1c6.webp\" width=\"774\" height=\"824\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 311\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>And even though I was familiar with the level I always took the wrong turn there. Why? Because I was playing without thinking consciously and that route was lighted better. And as soon as we had that changed I didn't end up going the wrong way.</p>\n<p>To further expand on how important this is, there's also what's called the \"<strong>squint test</strong>\": you squint and look at a screenshot of your game. The brightest area is where people's eyes will most likely be drawn. There's even a squint mode feature in the Unreal Engine 3 editor that blurs your viewport to simulate such a test.</p>\n<p>Of course, apart from light, you can also use other methods to create a contrast. You could use a unique shape that stands out to attract the player's attention. One arched doorway next to four rectangular ones will look a lot more important. You can also use color to create a contrast. A red door in an otherwise white room is going to catch the player's eye much more than a grey one will.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Composition &amp; Visual storytelling</strong></p>\n<p>Similar to how the technique of contrast is informed from painting techniques, this method is too. Here, objects in the environment subtly point towards where the player has to go. The lines of the objects are oriented so that if the eye follows them it will reach the intended target. This works especially well if these lines cross the intended boundaries directly. For example, a plank lying over a chasm will give the player the hint that jumping over said chasm is the way to continue.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5cbd03239e28cf70329b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5cbd03239e28cf70329b.webp\" width=\"840\" height=\"862\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 312\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/531cfaf517e0807b5c1b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/531cfaf517e0807b5c1b.webp\" width=\"788\" height=\"849\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 313\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Weenie</strong></p>\n<p>A \"Weenie\" type of Landmark. It's a term taken from the theme park designers. It refers to a large structure in the distance that's clearly visible to the theme park visitor.</p>\n<p>What these weenies do is give <strong>reference points</strong> <strong>for navigation</strong> within the park as whole. Additionally, they also look <strong>interesting</strong> and <strong>important</strong> so that the visitor wants to come closer and check them out. The same principle can be applied to level design.</p>\n<p>A good example of this in use is in the game <em>Mirror's Edge</em>. Here, the player learns that he has to follow <strong>the red objects</strong> to get to his goal. And in one map there's a large red radio tower visible in the background. The shape is already unique as most other buildings are skyscrapers but add to this the color red which both stands out and already has some navigational meaning and this Weenie is sure to draw the player in.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1ebf6134c0a1d776b248.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1ebf6134c0a1d776b248.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 314\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/536e5c79fe8596ad1848.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/536e5c79fe8596ad1848.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 315\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Motion</strong></p>\n<p>Motion is an excellent way to attract attention. Based on how our human eyes and brain work, <strong>animation</strong> and <strong>movement</strong> easily catch someone's eye. This increases the effectiveness of a visual component tenfold, as compared to static clues. Whether it's sparks, moving doors or flickering lights -- they all work equally well.</p>\n<p>However, using motion also means that player interest is a lot <strong>more conscious than with unmoving elements</strong>. This also includes <strong>scripted events </strong>such as a crane breaking down, or a door being blasted off its hinges. These events will grab the player's attention and surely make him want to investigate.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Cutscene</strong></p>\n<p>Short cutscenes can also be an excellent tool to focus a players' attention on a certain area. For example when entering a big room, the camera shows the contents of the room and ends up focusing on the closed door at the other side. This clearly tells the player that this door is important and probably the way to leave this room. Of course, this interrupts gameplay briefly -- but a cutscene can communicate complex routes quite clearly.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1663e0f2f0e122300182.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1663e0f2f0e122300182.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 316\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e5dc4efc139f67540f25.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e5dc4efc139f67540f25.webp\" width=\"480\" height=\"270\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 317\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/233c85272258ab8a9fdf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/233c85272258ab8a9fdf.webp\" width=\"960\" height=\"721\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 318\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Another way to use characters is to use friendly units to show the way. If your allies are moving along the planned path and through a doorway, the player will take notice of that route and probably follow them -- though only if he notices them leave.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a39322e4c97e803b93af.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a39322e4c97e803b93af.webp\" width=\"866\" height=\"481\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 319\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Character</strong></p>\n<p>Adding characters to a level can help the player navigate. For example, in a shooter, the player will be used to attacking his targets and then moving past them. If he's suddenly being attacked from a balcony in a house, then this will focus his attention on that area. He might wonder how to reach this position to get a better vantage point, or to grab whatever items his enemies will drop.</p>\n<p>Whatever the case, the player now knows that there's something up there which makes him likely to investigate. Additionally if the game features some sort of <strong>hit-direction compass</strong> then the player also gets the direction of his attackers as a clue. This makes this work even if the player's not looking at them directly when hit.</p>\n<p>For this reason it might make sense to have one character leave through the doorway, and another one take up a position next to it. That way, even if the player misses the initial character leaving he can still spot the second one and come to the conclusion that the doorway is important</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Pickup</strong></p>\n<p>Just as with characters, pickups (aka power-ups) will draw the players' attention. One reason is that pickups are most often highlighted to be clearly visible: Maybe they even rotate or pulsate. Either way, they often stand out visually. Add to this the fact that most players will want to pick them up and you've got a perfect tool to attract players to a location or to pull them along a route. This can also be used well in multiplayer maps where there usually are lots of items around.</p>\n<p>In <em>G-Force,</em> they've used this technique to help the player find the way to continue. The environment of many levels requires the player to frequently ascend and descend, however the camera cannot be used to look up or down in regular gameplay.</p>\n<p>Because of this, the tops of shelves or tables might not immediately seem like destinations. However, if he sees a pickup rotating on the table, the player is much more likely to investigate. The rotating pickups make the route visible even at the sides of the screen.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/41d8b2526326f837cbe4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/41d8b2526326f837cbe4.webp\" width=\"688\" height=\"737\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 320\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1465e2e758513424ef23.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1465e2e758513424ef23.webp\" width=\"907\" height=\"755\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 321\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Help the player identify different areas</strong></p>\n<p>This method is used to help the player with his ability to orient himself within the environment. It does so by distinguishing different areas from one another. This helps the player identify them and thus gives him a sense of position in the overall level. This only really makes sense in non-linear worlds such as hub-levels, multiplayer arenas or large open worlds.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>This is the Ashina Castle in Sekiro. In actuality, the area is much larger than what shown here, but we will just focus on the main quest for the sake of brevity. The quest is to rescue a person kept by a samurai general. These 9 particularly spots are divided into 4 different parts.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Picture 1 + 2 (Stairway):</strong> You are first introduced to a long, stretching stairway (landmark), and at the end of it, you will encounter a mini boss.</li><li><strong>Picture 2 + 3 + 4 (Rooftop):</strong>  You can sneak on this boss by moving on the rooftop on the side. Whether you choose to kill him or not, your next objective will be finding a way to infiltrate the castle.</li><li><strong>Picture 5 + 6 + 7 + 8 (Inside the castle): </strong>That small window is how you get inside the castle. From there you will be finding a way to reach the top, which leads to another mini boss.</li><li><strong>Picture 9 (Arena): </strong>You finally got to the top to fight the main boss of the area on the peak of the castle.</li></ul>\n<p>As you can see, while they are visually similar, there are still unique elements to help tell them apart.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/941a514fac8fbb762b22.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/941a514fac8fbb762b22.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"645\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 322\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1cfadf2760ffc2d964fa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1cfadf2760ffc2d964fa.webp\" width=\"771\" height=\"945\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 323\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Help player identify different areas: Landmark</strong></p>\n<p>Landmarks are immediately visible features of the environment. While within the area the landmark is often in view. This way it can give identity to a location. In smaller maps it could be a statue or maybe a large red truck. In bigger maps it could be a prominent bridge or a skyscraper. As you can imagine, landmarks and the weenies mentioned above can be one and the same object -- to both attract the player to a location and help him identify it.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p>Titanfall utilizes Landmarks to help its player memorize and understand the multiplayer maps better. By relating one landmark with another, they can know exactly where they are, therefore, react to the situation at hand accordingly.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/80c2d59a9307ad6f9917.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/80c2d59a9307ad6f9917.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"524\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 324\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Help player identify different areas: Style</strong></p>\n<p>Unlike the landmark, where there's one dominating feature, using style means that the difference permeates an entire area. It could be a different architectural style, indoor vs. outdoor or plainly and simply a different color through textures, light or post processing effects.</p>\n<p>It doesn't matter, as long as the different areas are distinct enough for the player to easily distinguish and remember. A downside is though that you still have to teach the player the relative position of the different locations. It's not really evident to a player standing in a back alley that the little pirate village is right next to the dark woods.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3c3e497f0b1de2fd5b90.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3c3e497f0b1de2fd5b90.webp\" width=\"848\" height=\"947\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 325\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ad436e6587cf620a1c9d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ad436e6587cf620a1c9d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"564\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 326\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Guide player to his own way</strong></p>\n<p>The guide method is building actual guides into the map itself. These guides clearly lead the player along to where he wants to be. This also makes them quite obvious since players need to consciously see and use them. Examples would be signs or colored lines throughout the level. In some cases there are even maps of the level displayed as textures on the wall.</p>\n<p>Of course this kind of tool only really makes sense in non-linear environments where there are multiple different places of importance to reach. Especially if the places are complex or there's lots of different ways to get from one to the other. In linear levels there's little need for this.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9230163364d8948fc6d3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9230163364d8948fc6d3.webp\" width=\"836\" height=\"421\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 327\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8a46beccf0235a82d08b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8a46beccf0235a82d08b.webp\" width=\"444\" height=\"225\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 328\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>The wall patterns in Darksiders 2 let you if you can run on it or not</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/030bda58b50944d4b4f2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/030bda58b50944d4b4f2.webp\" width=\"976\" height=\"612\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 329\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a4c8126aee9362f57ee8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a4c8126aee9362f57ee8.webp\" width=\"744\" height=\"419\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 330\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Guide player to his own way: Sign</strong></p>\n<p>Team Fortress 2 is an obvious example for guide signs. The game features complex multiplayer maps that sometimes even change during gameplay. To help the players learn and cope there's a lot of signage throughout the levels.</p>\n<p>These signs are clearly visible and generally make it easy for the player to figure out which route to take to get to a certain area. They also fit in well with the visual style and theme without seeming out of place, even though they are clearly huge.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Guide player to his own way: Line</strong></p>\n<p>Portal is a good example of lines. Even though the individual areas aren't too big, the interplay of the different interactive elements can be complex at times. To help here the game visualizes logical connections, such as between a button and a door, by using glowing lines. These are a great help when navigating the environment.</p>\n<p>They also have the added bonus that they transmit a lot of other information as well, something that's generally hard to do with immersed navigational tools. These lines also display the states of the different interactive objects (button pressed, energy on...) and their links (button A opens door B).</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/616d7b76a452a78e354f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/616d7b76a452a78e354f.webp\" width=\"768\" height=\"577\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 331\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c81d8a6d87745eb15f94.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c81d8a6d87745eb15f94.webp\" width=\"768\" height=\"432\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 332\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/16bdb5080db322d88ecf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/16bdb5080db322d88ecf.webp\" width=\"868\" height=\"948\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 333\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Lines are also used to great effect in the apple orchard level of the horror game. Playing an FBI investigator, the player has to use his equipment to follow clues such as blood trails. This technique is used throughout the game, but since almost all the levels up to this point are linear, it is of little importance.</p>\n<p>In this second-to-last map, however, the player is in an old farmhouse. Here he can freely explore the house, but to make sense of it he has to use his special lamp to light up the four different trails of scrawled writing. Following each of them to their conclusion is required before the player can continue the game. This works really well as the player is unable to fight back while using the light and ends up easily distracted following the writing as it crawls up walls and continues along the ceiling.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>The 2008 Prince of Persia video game also makes great use of lines, albeit a very subtle one. In this game, the player's wild acrobatics include walking along walls or climbing up columns. This variety of movements makes it harder to decide what to do when. To help with this the game features scratches and other damage to display the different paths. If there are scratches along a wall then that means that to continue the player has to perform a wall-run. This is a subtle method that works well with the setting but has the problem that once there are different routes the player isn't sure which one to follow.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/68f8ef6951c0c28080d4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/68f8ef6951c0c28080d4.webp\" width=\"784\" height=\"882\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 334\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Wrap up for this chapter</strong></p>\n<p>Okay. So that's all the tools and methods I can think of. I hope that you've enjoyed the read and that it got your gears spinning. However, to give you something to really work with I want to close the article with some tips on when and how to best use the presented tools and methods.</p>\n<p>The first step to using them is already done: now that you've read the article and are now definitely aware of their existence can begin to consciously use them to solve your design problems. This doesn't mean that you'll always have to throw so much information at your player that he feels like he has no other choice but to follow your instructions.</p>\n<p>An important part of all these tools is that they make it easier for the player to make <strong>meaningful decisions</strong>. They do <strong>not remove his ability to decide</strong>. This is why methods such as sealing off all doors after the player has passed through them to only allow him to move forward are not listed above.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c42ce4e5c3e909fed544.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c42ce4e5c3e909fed544.webp\" width=\"842\" height=\"952\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 335\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cbd1cf61dedbdeafcd5d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cbd1cf61dedbdeafcd5d.webp\" width=\"875\" height=\"510\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 336\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1de74d5944012891abeb.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1de74d5944012891abeb.webp\" width=\"742\" height=\"717\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 337\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>Also keep in mind that less is often, as they say, more. In some instances it can be desirable to not give any navigation hints. For example, the madness maze that can be found in Max Payne is such a case. Here the player is supposed to have a hard time finding the right path. All the walls look alike and so the player will wander around aimlessly. This can be annoying but since the area in the example is quite short it's not so frustrating as to become a problem.</p>\n<p>However if you decide to provide some help to the player, how do you go about it?</p>\n<p>Below I'll have a couple of notes and tips for you. There's some repetition of important information mentioned above but there's also some new points in there as well. I've chosen to use a bullet point format to make it easy to get an overview.</p>\n<p>So that said, let's get started:</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Attract methods</strong></p>\n<p>These methods are used to attract the player to a certain location to subtly steer him through the level.</p>\n<ul><li>Use these if your players always turn left and end up in a dead end while you want them to go right.</li></ul>\n<p>They are only really useful if there are specific routes you want to highlight, <strong>regardless whether the level is linear or not</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/149d1d81c31d865dafea.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/149d1d81c31d865dafea.webp\" width=\"932\" height=\"523\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 338\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7fa0812b868a8f13f452.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7fa0812b868a8f13f452.webp\" width=\"932\" height=\"520\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 339\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4f7443b9bb9cf9bab35b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4f7443b9bb9cf9bab35b.webp\" width=\"997\" height=\"499\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 340\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5304b9df20c3e90c6cc7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5304b9df20c3e90c6cc7.webp\" width=\"1001\" height=\"561\" style=\"--source-width:264px\" alt=\"Illustration 341\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Identify methods</strong></p>\n<p>Identify methods are used to distinguish different areas so the player knows his relative position within the level.</p>\n<ul><li>Identify your areas if your players get lost in the level and don't have a clue where they are.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>These only really make sense if you have <strong>open levels </strong>where the player can <strong>freely navigate </strong>or if you've got a lot of <strong>back and forth movement</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Awareness of the different areas</strong> still does not make the player understand their <strong>spatial relation</strong>.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>To help with this, the game can teach the space to the player so he will develop a mental model of the different areas.</li></ul>\n<p>Alternatively Maps and Guide can help as well. They are especially useful if they can easily be visually linked to the target (by style or color for example).</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Guide method</strong></p>\n<p>These methods are used to provide guides so the player can chose which way to go to reach his intended target.</p>\n<ul><li>If your players never know how to get to the place they want to visit.</li></ul>\n<ul><li>This is only really <strong>relevant</strong> if the player <strong>has a choice </strong>of routes to take, that means <strong>non-linear </strong>levels.</li></ul>\n<p>Guides are <strong>used consciously by the player</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1db922d4d66de5da0dce.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1db922d4d66de5da0dce.webp\" width=\"990\" height=\"495\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 342\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7049705fdda0ed156354.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7049705fdda0ed156354.webp\" width=\"989\" height=\"555\" style=\"--source-width:281px\" alt=\"Illustration 343\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h3>Chapter 09: Visual Logic &amp; Visual Storytelling</h3>\n\n<p><strong> </strong><strong>scroll-bookmark-197</strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>TABLE OF CONTENT</strong></p><p><strong>Chapter 09: Visual Logic &amp; Visual Storytelling</strong></p>\n<p>This chapter contains:</p>\n<ul><li>Visual Logic - Introduction</li><li>Visual Logic</li><li>How is it used in a video game level?</li><li>Why is it so important in a video game level?</li><li>How to achieve Visual Logic</li><li>Question yourself – the power of “WHY”</li><li>Limitation</li><li>Visual Storytelling</li><li>When should I use Visual Storytelling?</li><li>Visual Storytelling</li><li>Architecture</li><li>Examples</li><li>Special case: References and Crossed-Storytelling</li></ul></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>CONTENT</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Visual Logic - Introduction</strong></p>\n<p>Among Level Artist’s tools, <strong>Visual Logic </strong>and <strong>Visual Storytelling </strong>are two of the most <strong>powerful</strong> and <strong>complex </strong>to use. Their mastery is one of the key elements that distinguish a great Level Art from a poor one. In this lesson, we will learn how to ensure a correct Visual Logic to get <strong>a believable world</strong>, and then we’ll cover how to achieve some great Visual Storytelling in order to make your levels <strong>unique </strong>and <strong>unforgettable</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/907851cf1eacf7cf161e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/907851cf1eacf7cf161e.webp\" width=\"863\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 347\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c0a258a14533e7ee879d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c0a258a14533e7ee879d.webp\" width=\"897\" height=\"505\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 348\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>In the image above, there is </em><em><strong>debris</strong></em><em> on the ground. The story of how it got there is obvious, there is </em><em><strong>damage</strong></em><em> to the </em><em><strong>wall</strong></em><em> on the left, the </em><em><strong>materials match the building</strong></em><em>, </em><em><strong>stone</strong></em><em> and a </em><em><strong>wooden door</strong></em><em>. Also the </em><em><strong>spray pattern </strong></em><em>of the </em><em><strong>rubble</strong></em><em> is </em><em><strong>consistent</strong></em><em> </em><em><strong>with the damage to the building</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Visual Logic</strong></p>\n<p>Visual Logic is a term used to describe the <strong>realism</strong> in a scene. It is not “Does this model or texture look lifelike?” rather, “Does the scene in front of me tell a <strong>believable </strong>story?</p>\n<p>Ask yourself:</p>\n<ul><li>What is it?</li><li>Why is it there?</li><li>How did it get there?</li><li>What is its purpose?</li><li>Does it tell a believable story?</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>How is it used in a video game level?</strong></p>\n<p>Many times in games we will use these sorts of things to justify a blocker placed by the game designer, to flesh out the story, or perhaps show a path and lead the player.</p>\n<p>Ask yourself:</p>\n<ul><li>What is the story I am trying to tell?</li><li>Do I have the necessary props to tell the story properly?</li></ul>\n<p>Does it visually add to my scene? Or have I just made a mess?</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/951f0dfbcbdd25009787.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/951f0dfbcbdd25009787.webp\" width=\"958\" height=\"535\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 349\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Often it is done wrong with no logic or backstory. In the image above there is a lot of rubbish around the bin. This is logical: the bin is full, and people are lazy. The placement of the rubbish suggests people have </em><em><strong>opened the bin</strong></em><em>, </em><em><strong>found it full </strong></em><em>and just </em><em><strong>dumped it on the ground in front</strong></em><em>. Some smaller pieces of rubbish have blown around the alley. There are also </em><em><strong>stains</strong></em><em> on the ground where bags have </em><em><strong>leaked liquid </strong></em><em>in the past.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0498e7f1dfad236c09b7.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0498e7f1dfad236c09b7.webp\" width=\"948\" height=\"529\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 350\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Apart from appearing more realistic it adds backstory and can actually make the scene </em><em><strong>more dynamic</strong></em><em>. I like the image above. The cars are parked down the side of the street, they have been parked there for some time, But! There are no cars driving down the road… Why? Who would keep driving down the street when there is an enormous Evo doing battle with a strange teenage boy in the sky above you!</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Why is it so important in a video game level?</strong></p>\n<p>Good Visual logic is <strong>vital to an enjoyable game experience</strong>. If it is done <strong>right</strong>, you <strong>won’t even notice </strong>it. It is when it’s done <strong>badly</strong> that it causes a <strong>distraction</strong> to the player and begins to ruin the experience.</p>\n<p>Ask yourself:</p>\n<ul><li>Does my scene fit the gameplay?</li><li>Am I assisting or hindering the designer? Does it visually add to my scene? Or have I just made a mess?</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>How to achieve Visual Logic</strong></p>\n<p><strong>References from reality</strong></p>\n<p>To fully understand how the world is made, you just need to observe it carefully. Whatever you do in your everyday life, just <strong>keep on observing </strong>the world around you. When you walk back home after work, when you wake up and watch the ceiling of your bedroom, look at every little detail you’ve never watched before. How concrete debris looks like? Why a dirt road is always dirt at the same place? How a path made by animals looks different than a path made by human footsteps?</p>\n<p>Of course, if you need to create a level that doesn’t look like anything you’ve experienced before, just go get some reference from the Net!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3e390fa76ffb2b68e40a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3e390fa76ffb2b68e40a.webp\" width=\"917\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 351\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/c3786c2d0de54e990034.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/c3786c2d0de54e990034.webp\" width=\"960\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 352\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Question yourself – the power of “WHY”</strong></p>\n<p>There is no such thing as “randomness” in real life. Everything is at its place for a reason: someone or something put it here. Each time you place something in your level, always ask yourself “why?”</p>\n<p>Why every bike is parked at the same place? Because there is some shops or subway station nearby!</p>\n<p>Why is there some debris on the ground? Because the building next to it has collapsed!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Limitation</strong></p>\n<p>In a perfect world, we would always be able to keep a perfect visual logic through every element of the level. However, technology and production plans lead to two important limits:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Assets at your disposal </strong>(props, decals, textures). Do we have the asset we need to ensure visual logic everywhere?</li><li><strong>Time and performance</strong>. If we don’t have every necessary asset in our hands, do we have the time to produce it? Would it explode our performance budget (memory, number of entities)?</li></ul>\n<p>Often we are forced to use some slightly different assets that what we expected in order to achieve some visual logic at a lesser cost.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5f076f6521dc4be2ca3d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5f076f6521dc4be2ca3d.webp\" width=\"1009\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 353\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3380c06791867994939f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3380c06791867994939f.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"256\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 354\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Call of Duty Modern Warfare 2 (2009): Here a lot of bullets have been shot through the windows and the left walls. According to bullets trajectories, it’s logical to see bullets impacts also on the right wall!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bec3f9417b49af6e4530.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bec3f9417b49af6e4530.webp\" width=\"827\" height=\"298\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 355\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>With the tides, this pillar must have been submerged frequently, that’s why a ton of seashells have grown on it.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4ceb4cec2be3e61579d4.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4ceb4cec2be3e61579d4.webp\" width=\"797\" height=\"493\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 356\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Why is there a big hole in every floor above and a big pile of debris on the ground? Because of this heavy bomb that fell through it without exploding!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Visual Storytelling</strong></p>\n<p>Visual storytelling is a <strong>powerful extension of Visual Logic</strong>. Once you achieved a universe where everything has a cause and consequence, you can then tell the full story of this place with environment only!</p>\n<p><em><strong>Why is it important in a video game level? </strong></em></p>\n<ul><li>To tell a lot <strong>more information </strong>about the place than the <strong>player briefing </strong>and <strong>NPC interactions </strong>will do.</li><li>To <strong>reward</strong> player’s <strong>exploration</strong> and <strong>reflection</strong>.</li><li>To <strong>guide</strong> the player across the level by <strong>his own will</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>To play with player’s <strong>feelings</strong> (fear, laugh, stress, relief…).</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fabab52fb09d539fdfb3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fabab52fb09d539fdfb3.webp\" width=\"848\" height=\"479\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 357\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d7981e02042698129a8e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d7981e02042698129a8e.webp\" width=\"820\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 358\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/eaef8ab9a48accb09d53.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/eaef8ab9a48accb09d53.webp\" width=\"789\" height=\"507\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 359\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4d9a870db9ce9122c976.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4d9a870db9ce9122c976.webp\" width=\"787\" height=\"537\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 360\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>When should I use Visual Storytelling? </strong></p>\n<p><strong>A –</strong> Most of all, in any game (or part of level) that require the player to do <strong>research and investigation.</strong> If the player is specifically looking for a meaning to whatever he’s watching, you have to pay a close attention about the story you’re telling.</p>\n<p><strong>CSI: Hard Evidence </strong>(2006): Since it’s an <strong>investigation</strong> game, the player will look for <strong>everything that doesn’t make sense </strong>or <strong>every clue </strong>to help understand what happened, so the Visual Storytelling must be absolutely perfect!!</p>\n<p><strong>B – When the action calms down:</strong> when tension drops down between two action sequences is a good moment to tell some stories. The player can pay lmore attention to narrative details when he doesn’t feel stressed by enemies (streaming areas, corridor between two combat arenas, etc).</p>\n<p><strong>Metro 2033</strong> (2010): This area has <strong>no enemies</strong>, so the player can spend all his time looking at environment. That’s where we can put the most <strong>important details</strong>, like this kid playing who mean the whole place is his home.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>C – When the area is related to a particular character</strong> (his room, his house, his shop…). You can tell the <strong>whole story </strong>of someone by looking at his place! Everything is a clue to learn about <strong>his life</strong>, <strong>habits</strong>, <strong>behavior,</strong> etc. Whether the character itself is used in the main storyline or not has <strong>no importance</strong>. If you imagine a different character in each room you create and organize everything according to it, the whole level will become way more believable than if every room feels the same!!</p>\n<p><strong>Metro 2033 </strong>(2010): This kind of scene can tell by itself a large part of a character background. Where did he go? What does he like? Who are his friends and family? is he smart or stupid? All these answers are there!!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/bfdf8bf8903993a3aa24.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/bfdf8bf8903993a3aa24.webp\" width=\"902\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 361\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2e2bf8039d0926792198.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2e2bf8039d0926792198.webp\" width=\"717\" height=\"378\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 362\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Ghost Recon Future Soldier</strong></em><em>: In an Afghan cave, </em><em><strong>a strange shaped “cover”</strong></em><em> has been turned into an </em><em><strong>under construction handmade nuclear missile</strong></em><em>! From a Level Design element, we have now a useful storytelling element which emphasis the meaning of this cave! Notice also the </em><em><strong>white sheets and video camera </strong></em><em>on the left, which could be </em><em><strong>used by terrorists to broadcast their claims</strong></em><em>! If the cover was just </em><em><strong>another pile of sandbags </strong></em><em>and the wall just </em><em><strong>another rock wall</strong></em><em>, the scene could also be </em><em><strong>realistic</strong></em><em>, but with </em><em><strong>no meaning</strong></em><em>.</em></p></td><td><p><strong>D – In secondary paths and secret areas</strong>, as a <strong>reward</strong> for player’s <strong>exploration</strong>.  Narration is always a reward itself. Because it’s knowledge, and knowledge has a value. In the prehistory of videogame, we gave players some raw blocs of text to reward self-investigation, but players don’t like it anymore: they became <strong>more visual</strong>. Now, <strong>rewarding them is your job</strong>!</p>\n<p><strong>E – Whenever it makes sense!</strong> There is <strong>no “wrong place” </strong>to add visual storytelling. You can have not enough but you can’t have too much. Everything in your level can be an excuse to tell a piece of your level’s story. Each <strong>cover</strong>, <strong>wall</strong>, <strong>object</strong>, can tell something. In the worst case some player just won’t notice, but in the best one your whole level will become a true, deep and believable universe.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>What are the tools at my disposal to create it? <strong>Prop, objects, composition.</strong></p>\n<p>Obviously the most used tool, where props are placed and how did it get there is often enough to bring most stories to life. It’s quick and easy to tell a part of the story by placing props in a specific way, instead of just throwing them randomly across your level to fill empty spaces.</p>\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/486f31ad15c17fdc7bf6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/486f31ad15c17fdc7bf6.webp\" width=\"719\" height=\"389\" style=\"--source-width:132px\" alt=\"Illustration 363\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Call of Duty Modern Warfare 2 (2009)</strong></em><em>: the user of this computer must had a lot of work recently, his desk is full of post-it, cola cans and even a whisky bottle.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b615b67ec5d8b656b6ef.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b615b67ec5d8b656b6ef.webp\" width=\"607\" height=\"236\" style=\"--source-width:132px\" alt=\"Illustration 364\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Call of Duty Modern Warfare 2 (2009)</strong></em><em>: These favelas are really full of thugs. I don’t know who is this poor guy, but he must had some hard time lately!</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Visual Storytelling</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Real text (graffiti, advertisement)</strong></p>\n<p>Don’t underestimate the power of words! In any circumstance, <strong>writings mean</strong> “<strong>human activities</strong>”, past or present. Even without writing down the story, using a few handwritten words can tell the story of “people” who came/lived here before the player does!</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5fb1061078b0febcb998.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5fb1061078b0febcb998.webp\" width=\"594\" height=\"290\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 365\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Bioshock (2007):</strong></em><em> the graffiti gives some questions to the player: maybe all his thoughts were wrong?!</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0cf61c2f87856ae0efb2.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0cf61c2f87856ae0efb2.webp\" width=\"673\" height=\"505\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 366\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Left 4 Dead (2008):</strong></em><em> these ones tell many stories about other survivors. This is the only indications about other survivors in the whole game.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/57cdea434df3ae65f285.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/57cdea434df3ae65f285.webp\" width=\"599\" height=\"449\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 367\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Portal (2007):</strong></em><em> these graffiti are barely hidden in a secret area (not very difficult to find) and give the player a HUGE sense of discovery: before, he thought he was the first human in this lab !!!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/40d1bdbd45ffdee19994.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/40d1bdbd45ffdee19994.webp\" width=\"717\" height=\"368\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 368\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Call of Duty Modern Warfare 2 (2009)</strong></em><em>: the Moscow Airport level is full of Russian text, reminding the player where he is even while not paying particular attention to environment. That’s the power of text!</em></p></td><td><p>Text is also really useful to enhance immersion: look around you, in the real world, there’s text everywhere. If your level occurs in a foreign country, use the maximum of text written in the local language!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Decals, marks</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Stains</strong>, <strong>footsteps</strong>, <strong>scratches</strong>, are useful tools to tell what people did <strong>in the past</strong>.  Pay a close attention especially if the ground is soft (<strong>snow</strong>, <strong>mud</strong>, <strong>sand</strong>…) to use <strong>footsteps</strong> or <strong>special texture </strong>if you want to tell about <strong>human/animal activities </strong>in the area.</p>\n<p>On the other hand, to tell about <strong>a forgotten place</strong> that has <strong>never been discovered before</strong>, pay close attention to <strong>avoid using any mark of human activities </strong>in the surrounding!!!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a838e5b347ae21c88b0d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a838e5b347ae21c88b0d.webp\" width=\"720\" height=\"319\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 369\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Call of Duty Modern Warfare 2 (2009):</strong></em><em> A lot of soldiers came on the wood planks of this shooting range, carrying with them some sand from the desert around. Nobody had time to clean up, because of the war.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0a425649876b3fb47ee8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0a425649876b3fb47ee8.webp\" width=\"712\" height=\"458\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 370\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Saw (2009):</strong></em><em> the large stain of fresh blood on the floor immediately tells that something wrong happened here!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f97e24f0cc4065550f40.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f97e24f0cc4065550f40.webp\" width=\"716\" height=\"445\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 371\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>The Crossing (unreleased): </strong></em><em>In this alternative universe, Templars kept control of Europe until today. The crossover between actual Paris buildings and medieval architecture tell stories of this world better than any text.</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Architecture</strong></p>\n<p>This one is particular, since it is more the responsibility of leads and directors to decide. Architecture is a really powerful storyteller, because it tells about dozens, hundreds years in the past.</p>\n<p>Here a powerful tool can be a brutal change of architecture style: a modern element in a medieval environment will immediately be interpreted as a major element in the storyline. Be careful to keep a close control of these elements, to tell exactly what you want to tell and avoid giving misdirection to the player. (Think about encounter a polished steel wall in the middle of an old stone building).</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Examples</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Call of Duty Modern Warfare 2 (2009):</strong> the extreme amount of propaganda on this wall tells us that this Afghan town is under control of a dictator. His face is everywhere!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fe6daa9dea3e5783229d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fe6daa9dea3e5783229d.webp\" width=\"632\" height=\"219\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 372\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/851cdc4bf77f90550ad8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/851cdc4bf77f90550ad8.webp\" width=\"713\" height=\"458\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 373\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Call of Duty Modern Warfare 2 (2009): </strong>a funny reward for players who took the time to investigate: the president must have felt really lonely in his bunker!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Call of Duty Modern Warfare 2 (2009):</strong> Investigating a weapon dealer’s house, we can learn that:</p>\n<p>1- His men spend days and night in this house (sleeping bags on the floor)</p>\n<p>2- They are on drugs (cocaine all over the desk in first plan)</p>\n<p>3-… and they are perverts</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1ebbc0b659bb723749fa.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1ebbc0b659bb723749fa.webp\" width=\"823\" height=\"1000\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 374\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5d41aced090b33c2a5bd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5d41aced090b33c2a5bd.webp\" width=\"719\" height=\"310\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 375\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Ghost Recon Future Soldier:</strong> People lived here before the war. Looks like a birthday party had been organized for a kid, just before civilian evacuation.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Ghost Recon Future Soldier:</strong> Someone tried to escape these gunfights, but was shot while reaching the door, according to the blood dripping on the door handle.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9f0081ecdae1bbaced74.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9f0081ecdae1bbaced74.webp\" width=\"713\" height=\"514\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 376\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/23668e9e32491f4c826d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/23668e9e32491f4c826d.webp\" width=\"712\" height=\"400\" style=\"--source-width:279px\" alt=\"Illustration 377\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Bioshock (2007): </strong>Something terrible happened in this dentist office! We can easily imagine the crime with the opened dentist tools case. Did the murder got away covered of blood or the victim who crawled to sit on the closet ?</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Left 4 Dead (2008): </strong>While investigating, players can find this kind of funny message. As we said in part 2.2, “fun” is always a reward for players, and this can bring them to spend more time investigating through levels.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4abeda1234e9938ffb34.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4abeda1234e9938ffb34.webp\" width=\"720\" height=\"537\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 378\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/27aa05f48462a5168b67.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/27aa05f48462a5168b67.webp\" width=\"228\" height=\"199\" style=\"--source-width:132px\" alt=\"Illustration 379\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Legend of Zelda: A Link to the Past (1991):</strong></em><em>  a clear reference with the Mario portrait on the wall!</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/f16f391fa7b809f71af3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/f16f391fa7b809f71af3.webp\" width=\"353\" height=\"200\" style=\"--source-width:132px\" alt=\"Illustration 380\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Blood (1997):</strong></em><em>  In a secret place, you can find the dead body of Duke, from the game Duke Nukem (1996).</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b948d630ab49ad6ebbd5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b948d630ab49ad6ebbd5.webp\" width=\"452\" height=\"181\" style=\"--source-width:132px\" alt=\"Illustration 381\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Left for Dead 2 (2009):</strong></em><em> Here are the last words of Frank West, the hero of Dead Rising (2006), another zombie game</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6b97536ef1fc631eb57b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6b97536ef1fc631eb57b.webp\" width=\"204\" height=\"179\" style=\"--source-width:132px\" alt=\"Illustration 382\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><strong>World of Warcraft (2005):</strong>  In the sewers of Dalaran, a rat is teaching four turtles (tribute to TMNT).</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td><td><p><strong>Special case: References and Crossed-Storytelling </strong></p>\n<p>Sometimes, a visual element in a game level is a direct reference to another game. These kind of elements are extremely powerful and rewarding for players, but must be handled with great care. There are mainly two different kinds of visual references, the “<strong>Visual Tributes</strong>” and the “<strong>Visual Links</strong>”.</p>\n<p><strong>Visual Tribute</strong><br>Tributes are references to other games of the same genre. They are usually “Easter eggs”, hidden in secret places, and simply designed to be funny. Sometime it tells a story, sometime it doesn’t. In any way, it’s always rewarding to the player, since he has discovered something funny connected with his own knowledge. Tributes are widely used in Japanese games (you can find a Mario portrait in every Zelda games, since Adventure of Link (1987)) and Western games (World of Warcraft (2005) gathers more than a hundred of tributes to different games, movies, cartoon, etc).</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>However, these Visual Tributes may be subjects to copyright rules, so you must first check with your lead before attempting to use some in a game level!</p>\n<p>Note that these Tributes never truly affect the story of the level (except for “this character was here before”).</p>\n<p><strong>Visual link</strong></p>\n<p>More important than a simple tribute, the Visual Link really <strong>connects two different game’s storylines together</strong>, by binding their universe with even a simple detail!</p>\n<p>For the player, the reward is proportional to the amount of information about the story, and this kind of revelation is critical (as it tie a whole new storyline with this one).</p>\n<p>Of course, using a Visual Link to another game storyline <strong>MUST be known </strong>and <strong>validated by your game’s core team</strong>!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/8e1257166c799559993f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/8e1257166c799559993f.webp\" width=\"716\" height=\"537\" style=\"--source-width:266px\" alt=\"Illustration 383\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em><strong>Portal (2007):</strong></em><em> This element itself make the link between the game Half-Life (1998, Black Mesa was the name of the underground complex) and this one. This detail is HUGE in terms of storytelling for those who played Half-Life, because that means their stories are connected, it’s part of the same universe, and this laboratory already existed 9 years ago! This discovery is extremely rewarding, because the player feels he just discovered a critical link between two different universes, which bring a lot of information to light!</em></p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/27aa05f48462a5168b67.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/27aa05f48462a5168b67.webp\" width=\"228\" height=\"199\" style=\"--source-width:347px\" alt=\"Illustration 384\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<h3>Chapter 10: Level Optimization Guideline</h3>\n\n<p><strong>scroll-bookmark-216</strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong><strong> </strong><strong></strong></p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><strong>TABLE OF CONTENT</strong></p><p><strong>Chapter 10: Level Optimization Guideline</strong></p>\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>This chapter contains:</p>\n<ul><li>Optimization</li><li>Basic rules</li><li>What are my limits?</li><li>Art budget</li><li>Mesh density/Texture density</li><li>Texture density</li><li>Level maintenance</li><li>Collision</li><li>LOD &amp; Mipmap</li><li>Level streaming</li><li>Number of entities</li><li>Physic elements</li><li>Lighting issues: static lighting</li><li>Lighting issues: static lighting</li><li>You’re not alone (the importance of a Team)</li></ul></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"><p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p></div>\n\n<div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>CONTENT</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td><div class=\"source-table\"><table><tbody><tr><td><p><strong>Optimization</strong></p>\n<p>No one likes spending some time to optimize the level. It’s a fact. (I even suspect technical artists to dislike this part too… Am I right?) <strong>The problem is: no one likes playing a level with a bad framerate</strong>, huge loading time, wrong textures LOD, etc. So someone will need to do it, right? And for your level, dear Level Artist, this person is you.</p>\n<p><strong>Optimization is easy!</strong> Art is difficult, because it requires sensitivity, judgment; it’s subjective so maybe it won’t please everyone. But optimization is easy: Just follow the rules! Just go through your checklist <strong>frequently</strong> and ensure everything is stays within the limits, and everything’s gonna be alright…</p>\n<p><strong>Why do I need to optimize my level?</strong></p>\n<p>Here are two examples of games releases the same year (2007) on the same platform (Xbox 360). One is optimized, the other not. Make your choice!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/efdfab7b3e7e7e0e9411.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/efdfab7b3e7e7e0e9411.webp\" width=\"746\" height=\"842\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 388\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7443e74eac7f4ad0e155.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7443e74eac7f4ad0e155.webp\" width=\"788\" height=\"880\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 389\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Basic rules</strong></p>\n<p><strong>YOU are in charge of your level’s optimization!</strong></p>\n<p>Don’t expect someone to come after you’ve finished your work to repair everything that wasn’t done: You are at the end of the line, so the level’s optimization is your responsibility. It concerns the level’s framerate of course,  but also errors in the Log, physic behaviors of objects, tags and material sounds, collisions, memory overload, etc.</p>\n<p><strong>Know your engine!</strong></p>\n<p>Optimization is directly dependent on the game engine you’re using. If nobody gave you its specificities, please ask your technical artists/directors as soon as possible, otherwise you won’t be able to get the best of your tools.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Start optimization from the first day!</strong></p>\n<p>Your worst mistake would be to wait the first framerate loss to start thinking about optimization. Why? Because it would mean everything you’ve done until this moment can be wrong, and you will probably need to <strong>correct every single element</strong> of your level!!!</p>\n<p>From the very beginning, when your Level Designer is drawing his layout on a piece of paper and you’re dressing the list of props, start thinking about how your level will run. Even if it’s not precise yet, just a big idea: how many props will I need in this area? What is the maximum visibility distance I can achieve? Where can I load/unload my props and textures?</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/fac9143d65b40be47399.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/fac9143d65b40be47399.webp\" width=\"810\" height=\"851\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 390\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/995e649fdcc675306bca.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/995e649fdcc675306bca.webp\" width=\"807\" height=\"907\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 391\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Run your levels on different platforms frequently!</strong></p>\n<p>If your level runs correctly on your PC, it doesn’t mean it will run smoothly on the targeted platform. Take the habit to test your whole level <strong>at least one time a day on PC, and one time a week on console. </strong>It’s the only way to ensure you’re still in the correct direction. If the level doesn’t run smoothly, it means it’s time to stop adding some stuff and optimize what’s already there.</p>\n<p><strong>Don’t let other people’s problems ruin your level!</strong><br>If there is a code problem that slows down your level, a design problem that prevents you to play it, or everything else, <strong>you must inform your lead as soon as possible! </strong>You can also treat with them directly of course (especially if it’s your Level Designer partner, he should be right next to you!) but don’t wait until the problem gets even worse!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>What are my limits?</strong></p>\n<p>Let’s keep things simples: to produce awesome levels for PC and consoles, a Level Artist is limited by 3 main enemies:</p>\n<ul><li>Memory</li><li>CPU (Main processor power)</li><li>GPU (Graphic  processor power)</li></ul>\n<p>Of course there’s also “time” and “annoying clients”, but try to focus on technique for this time.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory is often our main problem on consoles</strong>, since every mesh, textures, animations, etc <strong>must be stored inside</strong>. We usually have processors issues when <strong>too many objects are rendered at the same time</strong>, <strong>or too many physic objects are activated</strong>, or <strong>too many dynamic lights</strong>.</p>\n<p>You need to think about it from the very beginning of the level conception, <strong>when you build your props list and draw the performance map with your LD partner.</strong></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cec8b1e8d0b9426ddb25.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cec8b1e8d0b9426ddb25.webp\" width=\"733\" height=\"908\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 392\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3d4ebb5f7197f1ec6a8b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3d4ebb5f7197f1ec6a8b.webp\" width=\"878\" height=\"950\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 393\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Art budget</strong></p>\n<p>“Budget” is a cursed word, nobody likes it… However, without precise budgets, we wouldn’t be able to achieve a single game which runs smoothly on any consoles! The budget is determined by your Technical Artist or Technical Director. Usually, they can quickly give you the Art Budget for props (polycount, texture sizes, number of textures, etc) to help you build your props list. However, since they’re the ones who know the best their engine and targeted platforms, try to get as much information as possible!</p>\n<ul><li>How much <strong>memory </strong>is allocated to environment at runtime?</li><li>What is the <strong>maximum number of entities </strong>that should be <strong>rendered simultaneously</strong>?</li><li>How many <strong>dynamic lights </strong>can affect a single prop in the same time?</li><li>How many <strong>milliseconds</strong> should be used to render environment?</li></ul>\n<p>It may not seem so important at the beginning of the level creation, but then the level maintenance will be much easier and precise if you have those.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>The concept of INSTANCE</strong></p>\n<p>Here is a useful concept to take into account while building your props list:</p>\n<p>Instancing is one of the main solutions to achieve a detailed enough level without memory issues. An instance is <strong>a single prop </strong>(<strong>mesh+texture</strong>) which is <strong>“instantiated” many times into a scene</strong>. So there are <strong>many objects </strong>in the scene, but it costs <strong>only one mesh </strong>and <strong>one texture in memory</strong>!</p>\n<p>Some hi-end engines, like Unreal Engine 3, have an “<strong>instance-based</strong>” <strong>renderer</strong>, so it can render a hundred of objects at almost the same cost of one, if these objects are instances of the same mesh.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/3440d9faa9028cebabe6.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/3440d9faa9028cebabe6.webp\" width=\"865\" height=\"390\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 394\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>“Ah, that’s why everyone is carrying exactly the same luggage!”</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/994fa3a0d3c147a1f4fd.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/994fa3a0d3c147a1f4fd.webp\" width=\"872\" height=\"512\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 395\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/7c903a8c58aa8c07a15a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/7c903a8c58aa8c07a15a.webp\" width=\"758\" height=\"960\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 396\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p>So that’s mean we can just create a single brick and build a whole house with 786 913 instances of only one prop???</p>\n<p>No, you can’t! Since <strong>memory is not our only limitation</strong>, we need to take care of <strong>a few other parameters</strong>: the <strong>number of entities</strong>, <strong>their size </strong>and <strong>their polycount</strong>. Basically, you need to find the golden mean between these three parameters while building your props list:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Too many entities</strong> will slow down the engine, take a lot of time to render lightmaps, make too many useless batches of collisions, etc.</li><li><strong>If assets are too big</strong>, they risk to be rendered every time even if 90% of the mesh is hidden. It means the engine will try to render a huge part of the level instead if rendering only what need to be displayed.</li><li><strong>If assets are using several materials</strong>, they will be rendered multiple times by the engine (once per material) and it will cost as much performance as if you had tons of assets even if the number of entities is low!</li></ul>\n<p>There is <strong>no perfect rule </strong>to determine exactly how to place yourself in the center of this triangle, but if you keep these concepts in mind while creating your list and building your level, you should stay in the right way without problems.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Mesh density/Texture density</strong></p>\n<p>You must always think of your level, props and environment meshes with the <strong>game camera </strong>in mind. Depending of the game, the camera will be either attached to the player (FPS, TPS) or locked on a path (beat’em all, platformer).</p>\n<p><strong>Mesh density</strong></p>\n<p>As an example we should think about the mesh requirements for our level as bands of “density” radiating out from the camera and the player path.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Yellow</strong> is the <strong>player path </strong>and 60-70% of the mesh detail should be <strong>focused</strong> in this area. During <strong>special camera sequences </strong>we might get <strong>very close to the game </strong>are within this space.</li></ul>\n<ul><li><strong>Orange</strong> is <strong>outside the player path</strong>, we can see it but the player <strong>can't approach it</strong> nor will we see it up close during kill cam sequences. 20% of the mesh density should be focused in this area. Keep in mind that these areas might require <strong>complex silhouettes</strong>, for these areas we should keep <strong>a little extra detail</strong> up our sleeve, in case we need to do <strong>something special to draw the player’s eye</strong> or just <strong>keep things interesting</strong>.</li></ul>\n<p>The <strong>Brown</strong> area is well <strong>outside the playable area</strong>, this should be handled with <strong>boxy mesh cards</strong> and <strong>matte paintings</strong>.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a8cc317ceb3d9ad92e95.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a8cc317ceb3d9ad92e95.webp\" width=\"837\" height=\"921\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 397\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/6fab20055ac8fe341e99.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/6fab20055ac8fe341e99.webp\" width=\"853\" height=\"932\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 398\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Texture density</strong></p>\n<p>Texture density is simpler since we can rely on pre-defined Pixel Ratio. The <strong>Pixel Ratio is the number of pixel/m&amp;sup2;</strong> of an object, depending on their visibility distance. Here are some examples:</p>\n<p>The Pixel Ratio for each layer should be defined by your Technical Artist or Director, but it can vary from a level to another. A level designed for a sniper gameplay can have a higher Pixel Ratio for background elements and lower for the navigable area.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>In any case, the <strong>platform memory can’t be extended</strong>, so you have to find by yourself the golden mean between every element in your scene.</p>\n<p>While building your props list, don’t forget to clearly specify which props will be used in <strong>playable areas </strong>and which will be in <strong>background</strong>, and <strong>how far</strong>. It would be useless to spend a lot of texture memory on background element.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/5136d6422689abfcae0e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/5136d6422689abfcae0e.webp\" width=\"909\" height=\"513\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 399\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Why are these two background buildings so close to the camera? Everyone will notice the sudden change of mesh density and pixel ratio.</em></p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/37d40952ff53ed407684.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/37d40952ff53ed407684.webp\" width=\"914\" height=\"514\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 400\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Good mesh density and pixel ratio, the background buildings match perfectly with those in gameplay area.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/dabc3b14fd009b71e370.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/dabc3b14fd009b71e370.webp\" width=\"918\" height=\"519\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 401\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>We can clearly see the difference of density in this level. The two buildings on the left don’t have a high enough pixel ratio to match those next to it.</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d91ecca89734a35356c5.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d91ecca89734a35356c5.webp\" width=\"791\" height=\"887\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 402\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Level maintenance</strong></p>\n<p>The Level Maintenance is <strong>one of your tasks </strong>as a Level Artist. Don’t wait until someone comes to say there are some issues with your level, go ahead and find any problem before it become too serious!</p>\n<p>The level maintenance should be done <strong>at least once a week </strong>(you can do <strong>more before milestones</strong>) I used to do it every Thursday, so I could fix any problem and be sure to have a clean and stable build on the server on Friday, when people usually test the game.</p>\n<p>Don’t worry, if everything is going well, you should be able to do all these tests and monitoring in no more than one hour or two, and then return working on the sweet parts.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Launch your level on every platform.</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Does it launch?</li><li>How is the framerate?</li><li>Do you have textures issues? Meshes disappearing?</li></ul>\n<p>You should immediately see what you need to improve. Even if everything seems to be fine, take a look at next steps, just in case.</p>\n<p><strong>Memory monitoring.</strong></p>\n<p>Every game editor has tool to monitor the memory used at runtime. <strong>Ensure that memory used by your level is still in the budget given by you Tech Artist.</strong></p>\n<p>If you exceed the budget early in production, maybe things will be fine for the moment. But later, when other people will add their resources into the game (sounds, animations, VFX, gameplay, characters, etc) your level won’t fit in memory anymore and won’t launch anymore!</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/0e9c2b0196b078033e4a.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/0e9c2b0196b078033e4a.webp\" width=\"791\" height=\"848\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 403\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/4dbe1de66f71ba22339d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/4dbe1de66f71ba22339d.webp\" width=\"877\" height=\"490\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 404\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Grass affords stealth in Uncharted 4.</em></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/afb4c8a20ecd672bd3a9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/afb4c8a20ecd672bd3a9.webp\" width=\"878\" height=\"492\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 405\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Character got stuck by an invisible wall.</em></p></td><td><p><strong>Collision</strong></p>\n<p>There are two things to check about collisions:</p>\n<p><strong>Does it serve the gameplay? </strong>Just play your level and ensure there’s no hole in ground collisions, navigation along walls is smooth, enemies aren’t block anywhere, etc. Anyway, if there was an issue with navigation collisions, your LD partner should have already warned you!</p>\n<p><strong>Are my collision meshes optimized? </strong>Take some time to <strong>deactivate </strong>or <strong>remove</strong> any <strong>useless collision </strong>(in <strong>background </strong>or <strong>out of player/enemies’ reach</strong>) and check if your collisions meshes <strong>aren’t too complex</strong>. The <strong>simpler</strong> the collision is, the <strong>faster</strong> it will process, saving some CPU processing power!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/d6e49a229a1b21087a1f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/d6e49a229a1b21087a1f.webp\" width=\"890\" height=\"503\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 406\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1f97e120abb7af37c233.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1f97e120abb7af37c233.webp\" width=\"897\" height=\"503\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 407\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Blurred textures: a typical case of memory overload. Check your mipmaps and free some memory!</em></p></td><td><p><strong>LOD &amp; Mipmap</strong></p>\n<p>Check if your LOD and mipmaps are correctly set up. There are two case of LOD mishandling:</p>\n<p><strong>Blurred textures everywhere</strong>: Usually happens when memory is overloaded, the smallest mipmap is displayed even where camera is close to the object.</p>\n<p><strong>Every props stays at LOD0</strong> (the most detailed LOD) even where camera is far. This one is more vicious: it’s more difficult to see, but can lead to memory overload and lower the framerate is it’s not fixed!</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Level streaming</strong></p>\n<p>Most <strong>single player levels </strong>are cut into streaming area (usually connected with “streaming sas”, double bended corridors that prevent players to see that the previous area has been unloaded).</p>\n<p>A common mistake while building this kind of level is to <strong>assign objects to the wrong streaming area</strong>. This can be a Level in UnrealEd, a Layer in DaVinci, a streaming volume or anything, depending of the engine.</p>\n<p>While playing the game, take a close attention to <strong>“popping” objects</strong>, props that appear or disappear suddenly, depending of the <strong>camera’s position</strong>. This happen the most with <strong>background objects</strong>, which should be placed in <strong>their own streaming area </strong>to prevent them for popping with the streaming.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/ab65a3c172ddff9470e9.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/ab65a3c172ddff9470e9.webp\" width=\"757\" height=\"393\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 408\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2c49287edcef58485aa0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2c49287edcef58485aa0.webp\" width=\"797\" height=\"388\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 409\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/e68da808d4e77e7ce53f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/e68da808d4e77e7ce53f.webp\" width=\"936\" height=\"525\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 410\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b48e008509ae2415115d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b48e008509ae2415115d.webp\" width=\"943\" height=\"530\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 411\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td><td><p><strong>Number of entities</strong></p>\n<p>We’ve seen before that some engine can handle a large number of instances in a scene, but some cannot.</p>\n<p>You should check frequently the number of entities displayed in each view (entities are <strong>props</strong>, <strong>light</strong>, <strong>physic objects</strong>, almost everything). Usually, this number can become extremely high because of small props.</p>\n<p>If your amount of entities blows up the budget, try grouping the smallest props into larger groups. This should ease the renderer.</p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1f59b2c8fcc432ca5c54.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1f59b2c8fcc432ca5c54.webp\" width=\"1107\" height=\"223\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 412\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>If these small rocks are instances of a single prop, there are 16 entities. If your prop is a group of 4 rocks, you can get the same picture with only 4 entities!</em></p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>Physic elements</strong></p>\n<p>Physics interactions are often framerate gaps. Try to play with physical objects and monitor their behaviors. Do they interact with complex collisions? Do they activate and deactivate immediately, or do they need some time to stop moving?</p>\n<p>Take a particular attention to physical objects that can itself activate many more if there’s not enough space between.</p>\n<p>If all these objects are physics, there is a risk of performance slowdown if they’re all “awaken” in the same time. If you experience some framerate loss due to physics, try putting more space between each of them, or use this scene in a sequence without much action.</p></td><td><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/2788106db47af569f8e8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/2788106db47af569f8e8.webp\" width=\"895\" height=\"904\" style=\"--source-width:275px\" alt=\"Illustration 413\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Lighting issues: static lighting</strong></p>\n<p>Most performances issues related to the lighting are dues to dynamic lights. If your game is using <strong>dynamic lighting</strong> or <strong>hybrid lighting </strong>(<strong>static</strong> + <strong>dynamic</strong>), you should pay a close attention to <strong>every light source</strong> since it can ruin your framerate.</p>\n<p><strong>How many dynamic lights affect your objects?  </strong></p>\n<p>One prop shouldn’t be affected by more than two dynamic lights (this number can change depending of your project, check with your tech artist). If some objects are affected by many different lights, try to <strong>exclude </strong>them from the light influence.</p>\n<p><strong>Props in the shadow don’t need sunlight!</strong></p>\n<p>You can remove the influence of a dynamic sun on objects when they are totally in the shadow of a bigger object (typically in interiors or underground sections).</p>\n<p><strong>Does this prop need to cast a shadow or is it useless?</strong></p>\n<p>Dynamic shadow casting use a lot of GPU time. Ask yourself if any props really need to cast a shadow, try to activate/deactivate the “cast shadow” option to see if there is a real difference. Usually, a lot of background props (far buildings…) and small props (little debris or junks…) don’t need to cast one.</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/22dcefa03421487411c1.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/22dcefa03421487411c1.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"708\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 414\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p><em>Some examples of dynamic shadows optimization</em></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>Lighting issues: static lighting</strong></p>\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/9b24a4176fe26ecee3bf.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/9b24a4176fe26ecee3bf.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"330\" style=\"--source-width:271px\" alt=\"Illustration 415\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n<p>Since static lighting is way <strong>lighter</strong> than dynamic lighting, most of issues you will encounter will be <strong>purely visual</strong>, with no <strong>impact on performances</strong>. However, there’s a few parameter to check:</p>\n<p><strong>The use of Lightmaps  </strong></p>\n<ul><li>When light is baked on a texture (lightmap) this texture will use some memory.  Always ask yourself if a lightmap is really <strong>necessary</strong> (vertex color baking is really lighter than lightmaps) and which <strong>size</strong> should it be. The best way is to start with <strong>vertex color</strong>, and only turn it into a lightmap if the result isn’t good enough. Start with a small lightmap, and upgrade the quality until the result satisfies you.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Lightmap glitches (bad UV2)</strong></p>\n<ul><li>The <strong>level maintenance </strong>is a good occasion to hunt <strong>lightmap glitches</strong>. If you find some abnormally <strong>black polygons</strong>, it often means that <strong>UV2 haven’t been unwrapped correctly on this prop</strong>, so you should give this information to your lead or directly the props artist in charge of this object.</li><li>If you encounter <strong>a mesh with some strange shaped lighting</strong>, it means its <strong>UV2 has been modified since the last time you baked lights</strong>. Just bake your level again to get rid of this ugly stuff.</li></ul></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\"><p><strong>You’re not alone (the importance of a Team)</strong></p>\n<p>Never forget that you’re <strong>not alone</strong>. Optimization is a complex set of interlaced rules which require some time and practice to fully understand. Your level won’t be perfect at your first shot, and that’s why you will often need to take advice from your teammates.</p>\n<p>Your <strong>Level Designer </strong>partner will be by your side every time, maybe his different job can lead him to know different things about the engine. <strong>Your level is his level too</strong>, so he will do everything he can to make it better. You should <strong>always share your problem </strong>with him.</p>\n<p>The other <strong>Level Artists </strong>of your team will also encounter the same problems you experienced. How did they solve it? Maybe they have found some useless techniques you can share! <strong>There’s no competition within the team</strong>. We don’t care about which level is the better or the most performing. We are all aiming to the same goal: <strong>make a great game</strong>!</p>\n<p><strong>Ask for people to play your level and share their feedbacks with you</strong>. Different people have different points of view, different way to look at things. <strong>Listen</strong> to their feedbacks, it’s always <strong>interesting</strong>.</p>\n<p>Try to find some time to <strong>test your teammates’ levels</strong>, even in <strong>early stages</strong>. Look how they did it, discuss about the difference with your work. The best ideas always come from <strong>good communication</strong>!</p></td></tr></tbody></table></div></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>";
